# REPAIR BRIEF r1 — Book 8, Movement 7 "The Straightedge" (ch41–46)

Same author, same method (NO-SOURCE: do not open `books/` or `v3-runs/`). You may read this edition's own closed text and the plan. The four Sol reads are in this folder (`plan-audit.md`, `source-tracking.md`, `review-editorial.md`, `review-cold.md`); read them, and read `plan-audit-apply.md` for what the coordinator took and declined and why. The reads quote the old text in places; write nothing from those quotations. The baseline is `pre-repair-r1/` (your draft plus the twenty-five coordinator-applied fixes). Write `REPAIR-REPORT-r1.md` when done and refresh AUTHOR-REPORT's "New canon minted" and "State at movement end".

**Verdict on the draft:** the beat order, the calendar, every sum, the knowledge perimeters and all the protected text you placed whole are ratified. Nothing is to be re-staged. The audit asked for seventy-six line fixes; fifty-one of them are declined, because they followed the old text against this edition's own closed chapters or against what the page already does well. This is a bounded repair: joins round sixteen restored units (seven `{{JOIN}}` beats), four small recompositions, two tracked chains, and a narrow referent pass. The movement stands at about 32,000 words; do not grow it.

## A. KEPT (ruled; not to change)

1. **Vastin rides on the level and walks every hill and the whole cut** — the dray's tail-board at his own copper, the keeper's beads, "a road has no keeper", and Teague's "It's owed a seat. For him. I'll not walk ten at the pace of one" on the 298th. The audit wanted him on foot for the whole road (the packet's "walked again on a bad knee"); that is declined, because closed Movement 6 has him let down a ladder on a rope, in a stall, on a handcart and on his seat on a stair until the 294th. Your answer is the one that body allows. He walks in the file from the ford on the 299th.
2. **The woman with the net** says "Eleven off the ridge." and Oryn "I know." (locked Book 7's own form). "Stand there." and "Who sewed this." are Oryn's, as you had them.
3. **"Twelve," … "And a mule."** stays Seln's.
4. **Oryn is told the eleventh as well as the twelfth; "not on my plank"; her half-notice** ("Something turned over … One of yours, in its sleep … It's nothing to me") stands. The four reasons in the log stand.
5. The horseman and the riderless horse; Teague's window and what he knows; the four heads of the contract in your words; Pike's twelve lines, the thirteenth, and the day put against the season line with nothing printed; the Force at the salt cart; the march order; the Millrace outcomes; the dividers opened by Karis on Cael's loan; "It's six people's. That's why."; "Known" under the 295th–297th only.

## B. Already applied by the coordinator (keep the protected words exactly; write the joins)

`{{JOIN}}` marks a place where a beat of YOURS belongs (a gesture, a look, a line of narration, or a sentence or two of speech in your own words where noted). Seven are on the page; none may remain. The attributions outside the quotation marks ("Vastin said,", "Teague said,", "Oryn said,") are the coordinator's and are flat on purpose: vary, move or drop them. The words inside the quotation marks are fixed.

1. **ch43, the cut:** `Vastin said, "Here."` — it is Vastin's, at the place, before the stick is lifted. Then, after the stick comes down: `"Two words," Vastin said. "Both of them ours."` — one continuous speech, also his; Cael's "Noted," answers it. Re-join the paragraph that precedes "Here." and the two after "Noted" so that they read true to that speaker: the two words he means are the two he has just used and heard on this road, and he is saying that they belong to this crew. Your paragraph "He had never before given it to this man" stands.
2. **ch44, the contract:** `Teague said, "Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four."` `{{JOIN}}` `Teague said, "Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more."` Your four heads follow the marker at present. Arrange it so that the count opens his speech, the heads come in the middle as you wrote them, and "Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more." CLOSES it (after "You'll be asked." or wherever the heads end); the join then needs only a beat. Check "A captain had just said six of them, aloud" against the restored words.
3. **ch44:** `Teague said, "The wall's still the wall."` — Teague's, in answer to Brom's "You've built your count on a thing that you've never seen stand." Cael's "There's a month on it" then answers Teague. Check the two paragraphs after it.
4. **ch44, the meadow:** `Teague said, "Don't put it in front of anything that needs doing twice."` — Teague's, of the Force. Stage whom he says it to (the carter who points at the second cart, or across the yard) so that Cael can honestly write it; Cael's entry may quote nothing of it.
5. **ch45, the grade:** `Karis said, "A mark is a grade. The one they give a boy at fourteen says what he's *for*."` `{{JOIN}}` `Karis said, "The one on a sheet says how *old* the thing is."` As you noted, the second half is earned only when the figures have been read. Split the unit if you wish: the first half where the marker stood, the second after "Two hundred and six." (or after the fifth place's corner). Both halves once, hers.
6. **ch45:** `"Somebody laid them down in order," said Karis.` and `Karis said, "It's observation. I'm telling you what the paper says. Not what it means."` — both Karis's. The first is the paper's showing in her mouth; Lira's "Who?" and Vastin's drawn breath come between, as you have them; the second stops him, and his first "Noted" follows.
7. **ch45, the doorway:** `Teague said, "Oldest last."` `{{JOIN}}` `Teague said, "Then that's where we're going."` The scene ends on the second. **Also return stake thirty-two to the tarred case** somewhere before the road (it was taken out at 45:35–39 and never put back); it is still never driven.
8. **ch45, the lean-to's post:** `"Nineteen days," Vastin said.` / `Oryn said, "Then it's eleven more."`
9. **ch45, the fence:** `{{JOIN}}` `Oryn said, "I'm a stop on my own route."` Only that sentence is fixed. Before it she answers Teague in your words; the facts: she owes three holds yet on her round, her road lies east with theirs for a while and then does not, and she is on nobody's count and is sent by nobody.
10. **ch45, the bench:** `Lira said, "Sixth chair."` / `Oryn said, "On my terms. Which are the only kind."` Then she sits.
11. **ch45, the arm:** Oryn, "You listened when it told you the second time." / Lira, "Once." / Oryn, "Once is once more than I priced."
12. **ch45, the knee:** `"It held in a cut," said Oryn.` Then her verdict in your words; then `"Noted," Vastin said.` / `Oryn said, "That's new."` / `Vastin said, "It's a useful word."` / `Oryn said, "It's his."` — and the scene ends there, with whatever look of hers you choose.
13. **ch46, the leaf offered:** `Oryn said, "Is it a finding?"` / `"It's a leaf," Lira said.` `{{JOIN}}` `Oryn said, "Then it's not mine."` `{{JOIN}}` `Oryn said, "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table."` `{{JOIN}}` `Oryn said, "Lie still when I say. That's still the term."` Your sense for the three joins: Lira may say one thing more of what the paper is, in her own plain words, WITHOUT any of the leaf's wording, its age, or what anything was built for; Oryn says in her own words why her table is for what she can put her hands on. If "Lie still when I say" sits better as her last word to Cael as she sends the two of them to bed, move it there. The paper is not unfolded.
14. **Small things done:** "That's what bears" (ch41) and "always behind a sign that bears" (ch45) — this edition has no noun "bearer" for a sign; "the ice had been gone more than four weeks" (ch44); Teague's "I said after the thaw. It's a month gone."; "how soft the thaw had left that corner"; Karis's speech at 45:75 joined into one; **the factor's seat for the old man is cut** (ch43, with the paragraph of his not answering, and the log's clause): where Vastin belongs is not this book's to open. Check that the factor's eyes and the rest of that scene still read whole.

## C. For you to recompose

1. **The ages (ch45, from "There was a number there" to the fifth place's corner).** Your corner figure stands, and Vastin's account of the form stands; the audit's wish to print a date instead is declined. But the passage now claims more than the plan gives. The plan's fact is that each place is dated FROM ITS FIRST SHEET: sixty-one, ninety-four, a hundred and thirty-eight, two hundred and six. So let the corner figure be the year of the series, counted from the first sheet that the office holds for that place — which is what Vastin already says. Take out what goes beyond that: "A sheet a year", "in every year for longer than any registry clerk…", "the others stood in a drawer", and Seln's "as much of it as had been kept from the people who ought to have received it". Nobody at that table knows whether anybody walked to the place in every one of those years; the paper says only how long ago the first sheet was drawn. Cael's sum becomes that, and Seln's "I had supposed that it was six years" may stand alone. The fifth place: two unread signs and "older than two hundred and six" stand.
2. **Lira in the cart-shed (ch46:241–263).** Only one line is wrong: "You went stiff, on the plank … I hadn't." The page says that he kept his jaw and hands loose and had never lain so still, and the plan says that the change is kept out of his wrist and his face. Re-ground what she knows in what she could honestly have seen or can hear: he would not look at the lantern in the lean-to and looked at the roof; his eyes are running now, in the hay; and she knows what it is that bills the eyes, having led a blind boy up a stair. Her inference is hers ("It only charges when it's looked"). The rest of the exchange stands as written, from "Something came" to "If there's road". He still tells her neither word.
3. **Two tracked chains (source-tracking.md) — break the ORDER, do not reword:**
   - **ch44:133 on, Pike's line right way up.** Chain: he writes the entry → Cael reads it from the keeper's side → a lifetime of reading across the plank → it happened without his choosing. Let him read the line before he notices where he is standing, and let the noticing come later, after Pike has kept him there for the empty columns or the season line; or keep the moment and drop the look back over a lifetime.
   - **ch46:181 on, the end of the reading.** Chain: the breastbone hand lifts → the other is drawn out by degrees → she finds the keg and sits → recovers → gives the finding. Enter after her hands are off, or let her give the finding before she sits, so that the greyness is what the saying costs.
4. **A narrow referent pass (cold read 3):** in ch41 from "The man had not sat down" through the two copies, and in ch46 from "It was at about the three hundredth breath" to the withheld word, a listener can lose which "the man", "the thing" or "it" is meant. Replace a handful at the points of decision with a name or the settled label (Cleon may be named by the narration now; "the twelfth"). No more than that.
5. **One bridge (cold read 2), ch45, after the log:** your "it was more than she would have let him say at a table" already marks the log as Cael's own inference. Make sure a reader sees which parts are his and not the paper's: "quiet for centuries" and "four hundred years" come from what he heard in a gallery, laid beside a place older than two hundred and six. A clause is enough. The log itself is protected and is not touched.

Do NOT undertake the editorial's cadence pass; the three working ranges pass on your prose.

## D. Standing constraints (unchanged)

Season-blind ("thaw", "first snow", "cold term" as events only); feet, yards, miles, paces, strides, cords, fathoms, furlongs; no count of years since the Kindling in narration; nineteen; no possessive before "Path" in narration; no new proper names; Vastin without an age; Teague's spear-carrier a tall man who has not spoken to the six; Teague does not say "Noted" in this movement; Force seen and not acquired (twelve; thirty-two); no drill under a wall; consent explicit at every reading; the twelfth's inward turn unbidden, *still* then *set*, told to no one; Oryn does not hear the leaf; nobody quotes the leaf; "four hundred" only in the fixed log; no "Architect", no *terminal*, *circuit*, *infrastructure*; Cleon's warning in no book; Seln's case shut and unnamed; every protected unit whole and once. Every chapter inside the three working ranges; gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected (`bash editions/monroe-1.3/tools/ed.sh overlap book-08-before-the-paths 7`); zero `{{JOIN}}` and zero `[[TIER-B` in every chapter. Do not commit; report back.
