## C. Recompositions

1. **The ages (45:79–103).** Vastin now says that the corner figure is "the year of its series: the count of years from the first sheet that the office holds upon the place", and adds: "It does not say that anybody walked there in each of those years. It says how long ago the first sheet was drawn, and no more." Karis: "stands in its sixty-first year… in its ninety-fourth… a hundred and thirty-eight… Two hundred and six." Cael's sum is only that a first sheet of the fenced place was drawn two hundred and six years before the one under her hand. **Removed:** "A sheet a year"; "in every year for longer than any registry clerk…"; "the others stood in a drawer"; Seln's sentence about sheets kept from the people who ought to have received them (his "I had supposed that it was six years" stands alone). The fifth place is unchanged.
2. **Lira in the cart-shed (46:249–265).** "You went stiff… I hadn't." is gone. She now grounds it in his eyes: he would not look at the lantern, he looked at the roof while Oryn told him, he crossed the yard "like a man going by a forge", and she can hear the water going; "I had you by the sleeve across a vault, seven days ago, with your lids shut. I know what it is that bills them. It only charges when it's looked." The exchange from "Something came" to "If there's road" stands as written. Neither word is told.
3. **Two tracked chains, re-ordered (not re-worded):**
   - **44:133–161.** Cael now reads the line as it is written "and thought only that it was short"; Pike goes on to the two empty columns and then to the season line; only after "Eleventh. And dated." does Pike look sideways and say "You're on my side.", and Cael see where the press has put him: he "had read two leaves of that ledger over the stump of an arm. Every line of them had lain the right way up, and he had not once marked it." The look back over a lifetime of reading across the plank is cut.
   - **46:181–199.** The scene now opens on "That's forty minutes," with her hands already off and Oryn on her feet; she gives the finding standing; then she finds the keg, sits and takes the cup, and "it came to him that the forty minutes had not done the whole of that." The ordered lifting of the two hands and the count to fifty are cut.
4. **Referent pass.** Chapter 41: "Cleon had not sat down"; "the writer's or the knife's"; "There's the writer's," said Cleon; "the red eyes"; "'Good,' said Cleon"; "with Cleon's finger on the last line" (six places). Chapter 46: "The twelfth had lain where she said"; "the twelfth came about to that"; "The twelfth leaned… at the middle of him"; "how the twelfth said that"; "The eleventh had come into him in a stair… found the word under her own glove"; "He did not let the next word come"; and in the cart-shed "He had not turned the twelfth" (seven places).
5. **The bridge after the log (45:231).** "Only the ranking was the paper's, with a place past the fourth that was older than two hundred and six. The centuries, and the figure near its foot, he had from a gallery, where a thing was read aloud once; he had laid them beside her morning's work himself, and it was more than she would have let him say at a table." The log is untouched. (Lira's "You've put a figure on it." and his answer were cut as now redundant.)
6. **Stake thirty-two** goes back into the tarred case in Vastin's hands at the end of 45.2 (45:117), before Teague comes to the door: "it had still never been driven."

## Cuts made so that the movement should not grow (about 455 words)

Chapter 45: Karis's "I must call them something…"; two clauses of Vastin's on what he is good for and what he never read; the second half of Cael's sum (the spring); two sentences on the ridge (the country falling away; the left-hand ruts) and the hand on the well's curb; one line of feet in the file; the forfeited slip at the weir; the bench's day; Teague's "in her ten days at Lowmarch"; one sentence of Oryn's at the fence; half of the seam's description and Oryn bending to it; "Something went across her tired face…" (her look now closes the scene instead); "If you'd brought the grey up…"; "He had known that it was coming since she said *run*."; the second half of the Denvash sentence. Chapter 46: the sentence on the leaf having been read once in a gallery; the sleepers in the hay.

