# Editorial review — movement 007 (chapters 41–46)

**Review type:** fresh-context, canon-informed editorial review; continuity/story and O'Connor 1.3 numerical-formula alignment. This is not a simulated human or cold read.

**Verdict:** **revise before acceptance.** The story underneath is strong, coherent, and unusually good at turning paperwork, bodily limits, and exact language into adventure. I found no substantive canon, chronology, resource, power-limit, or reserved-disclosure contradiction. The blocking defect is mechanical but pervasive: sixteen `[[TIER-B: ... coordinator to supply protected text]]` blocks remain inline. They replace required dialogue at several of the movement's largest payoffs, violate the audio-first Reader Standard, and are counted as prose by the supplied metric method.

## Unscored reader response

The back of the leaf caught me immediately. The best early turn is not the deciphering itself but Cleon and Karis doing two different kinds of honest work on the same object, followed by Cleon's “Work. Not a finding.” The bridge refusal in chapter 42 deepens Cael without announcing that it is doing so. The grade solution in chapter 45 is intellectually satisfying: *set*, kindling marks, site grades, annual sheets, the rising ages, and Hesk's dividers all become one line of proof. The deepest emotional sequence is Oryn's reading in chapter 46, because both of its truths cost somebody: Oryn admits the limit of her life's instrument, and Cael withholds the Rune's answer from the person whose hands drew it out.

Vastin matters most in this movement. His usefulness is no longer rank-shaped: he tallies boxes, names a form, accepts a field order without adding a clause, and finally adopts “Noted.” Cael remains the emotional centre, but Vastin supplies the clearest visible change.

I slipped only when the coordination blocks replaced dialogue. They are not marginal notes at scene edges; they occupy the cut revisit, the contract, the Force lesson, the grade's verbal conclusion, several Millrace treatment beats, and the refusal of the leaf. In those places the reading experience stops and becomes production correspondence.

I would keep reading. The line beyond the fourth site, the rider/watch pressure, Teague's demonstrated Force, and the incompatible Tide/Rune findings all point cleanly toward the Span and the oldest site. I would not call the current artifact reader-ready until the sixteen blocks are resolved.

