   101	The long room of the lower inn was as full as Cael had ever seen it, and Pike had the bench under the cloth.
   102	
   103	Every crew that had lain up for the cold was there, and a good many carters. The hide-woman had a stool by the hearth, and the three grey men had their board on a keg and were not throwing. It was the hour at which a one-armed man read out what the day had brought to his plank; and the room had gone quiet for it, as it had on a night of first snow, when a crew came in with white on its shoulders.
   104	
   105	Brom did not wait to be called. He went down between the benches with his box still on his shoulder, since he could not get it off without help, and laid a strip of bark on the open ledger.
   106	
   107	"That's yours," he said. "To draw the tack on."
   108	
   109	Pike took it up, and held it to the candle in his double fashion; a thumb had been set to its foot, in grease, and had left a mark like a crook.
   110	
