editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:53:The mild eyes rested on him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-54-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-55-"That is not all that he did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-57-"It's all that I've leave to say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:59:"Then it is all." Seln put his hands into his sleeves. "I should have said yes, on that stair. I should have been a good deal longer about it than a big man with a box. He has kept a hundred and ten strides of paper for thirty years, and has not put a ticket on a bundle; I have considered that fact for five days on my own account." He was silent a moment. "It is very nearly the only thing in this town that I trust."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-61-Karis looked up from her notebook, by the boxes. She had seventeen letters, and a strip of linen on her left hand that wanted changing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-63-"He showed me his joins," she said. "I do not think that a person who would do that would sell a boy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-64-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-81-"You can run, which is what I am afraid of; and you have a shoulder the colour of a plum."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-83-"Then it's me," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:85:He had not known that he would say it until it was out. He had been looked at for nine days, from a doorway and a kerb and the head of a stair, by people who had been told what to look for; and he found that he wished, once, to do the looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-87-"It is you whom they came to see," said Seln. "I have weighed that. If he turns and finds you behind him, he will have seen a porter walking in a street at night, which is no more than he has seen already." He got up off the rung. "You will open nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-89-"Nobody opens anything."
