editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:97:It was at the stable's far end, a low thing on leather hinges, and it gave upon a lane no wider than a barrow. They passed the end stall to reach it. There was a long shape in the straw there, under two blankets, with a bound leg laid out along a plank; and its eyes were open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-98-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-99-The old man did not speak; he looked at the two of them, and at the door, and then one raw hand came out from under the blanket and shut, slowly, into a fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-100-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-101-It was the second of the four hands that a road had taught him. *Seen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-102-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-118-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-119-*Away?* said the hiss in his own feet. It was not a thing that Wind asked often, standing still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-120-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-121-He told it no. And at his knees, where it had stood since the lane under the wall, the other thing lifted its head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-122-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:123:Shadow had come up at dusk, as it came every dusk, and he had told it no in a lane while his eyes ran. It had not lain down; it was full night now, in a town of doorways, and he was going to follow a man through it. There was a seal on that thing, and a reason for the seal which had not altered since Ostrand; and he had never felt it lean so against a shut door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-124-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-125-The man under the lamp stepped down into the street.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-126-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-127-He did not look to left or right; he went away up the house-fronts at a stroll, toward the open place, with his hands behind him, like somebody who has dined.
