--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-133-Cael stood in a doorway and looked at a place twenty strides beyond him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-135-It was a great deal harder than not looking at a crown. The crown did not move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:137:A light came round the corner by the chandler's, low, jogging, with a rumble of small wheels. It was the watch: two men in leather caps, and a handcart between them, and a lantern slung from a pole at its front.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-139-Seln's hand came up beside his coat, flat, palm down. *Stand.* Then two fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:141:They fell in behind the cart. It was done as a thing is done on a road, at a walk, without any word; and Cael understood it only when he was there. A watch looks where its own lantern falls; and anybody who turned to see that light would see the light, and two caps, and behind them a darkness in which a pair of porters might have been going home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-143-The watchmen did not turn round at all. One of them was telling the other about a goose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-145-They crossed the open place so, twenty strides behind a hood, to the mouth of the brokers' street. There the cart swung right, for the ring road; and the man with the cut boots went straight on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-146-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-162-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-163-The lane went down toward the river, and it smelled of tar and new hemp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-164-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-165-Cael knew where it led before he saw. He had stood in that place with a big man, nine days ago, by daylight, and watched him look into the cut end of a rope as a person looks into a flower. It had been full of people walking backward then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-166-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:167:It was a shed, open all down one side, and it ran away along the inside of the town wall for three hundred yards. There was a great wheel at its head, and beyond the wheel a floor of trodden boards as straight as a ruled line, under a roof on posts, with coils hanging the whole length of the wall like hats on a row of pegs. A lantern burned at the far end, very small, where the tar yard was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-168-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-169-The man in the hood walked in under the roof, past the wheel, and went on down the middle of the boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-170-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-171-Seln's fist had shut; then the palm came down flat, and stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-172-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-175-"It is a pipe," said Seln. He scarcely moved his lips. "Whoever goes into it behind him stands against that wheel and the sky for three hundred yards. Whoever goes round it by the open side has lost him for as long; and there is a quay at the bottom, and a lane up from the quay, and a wicket in the town wall for the tar-men. He has three doors out of the far end, and we should not know which."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-177-"He knows that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-178-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:179:"He chose it because of that. I should have." The grey eyes went along the roof. "I shall stay at the wheel, in case he has only gone in to come out again. You will go round by the open side, behind the drying-racks, to the barrels. Go softly, and be there before he is. And when you are there, you are a barrel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-181-"And if he comes out past me?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-182-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-183-"Then let him. Count where he goes. I shall find you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-184-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-190-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-191-Ten strides at a burst; thirty bursts; and a crack at the end of each like a carter's whip, in a town asleep. He told it no without breaking step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-193-He made one mistake, and it was not with his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:195:He glanced along the shed, to judge how much of it was left; he only meant to measure. But it was three hundred yards of a thing that somebody had built to go in one direction, with every post and plank and peg of it laid toward the far end; and the new thing behind his eyes came round to that as a weathercock comes round, before he had told it anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:197:*Goes*, it said. The whole roof leaned away from him, down the length of the wall, toward a lantern.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:199:He shut it, and it had been open for the space of a blink. A grain of sand came into each eye and sat there, and he ran the last hundred yards with his lids half down, and was angry, and had not the time to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-200-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-201-The tar yard was a square of cobbles above the river, with barrels stood on end in rows and a black kettle on a cold hearth. He went in among the barrels and found two that stood a little apart, and put himself between them with his back to the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-202-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-203-Then he waited for a man to come out of the end of the shed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-204-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-205-He counted, since there was nothing else to do with the time. A person at a stroll goes three hundred yards in a count of two hundred and forty, or thereabouts. He gave it three hundred, and then four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-206-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-207-Nobody came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-208-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:209:The lantern burned on its post at the head of the quay steps, and under it the river went by with a noise like cloth being drawn over a table. He could see the shed's mouth, a square of blacker dark, twelve yards off; and nothing came out of it, and there was no sound of boots on boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-210-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-211-At five hundred he understood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-212-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-213-The man had stopped. He was standing somewhere in that long roofed dark, among the coils, with his hands behind him, and he was doing what Cael was doing: he was listening for a second pair of feet to come down the boards after him, or round by the racks; and he was prepared to stand there until he heard them, or until it was morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-214-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-220-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-221-It was a good place for it, and he saw that plainly. A line stood from that post to the wall would have kept a man in a pipe like a cork in a bottle, until a grey porter came down from the wheel and they had him between them. It would have roared across half the district, and he told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-222-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-223-And Shadow asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-224-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:225:It had not once left off, and it asked now as water asks at a sluice. He was standing between two tar barrels at night, in a black coat, making himself a part of the dark by holding his breath; and there was a thing under a seal that would have made him so in truth, to any eyes at all, between one heartbeat and the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-226-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-227-*No*, he said. He had said it to that one twice a day since Ostrand. He said it now as a person leans on a door with somebody leaning on the other side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-228-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-229-At seven hundred and ten the boards spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-230-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-231----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-232-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-233-The boots came down the last of the shed without haste, and the man walked out of its mouth into the yard and stood still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:235:He was five yards off. Cael could have counted the buttons on the long coat, if there had been light to count by, and there was none on that side. The lantern was at the man's back, and it made of him a flat black shape with a hood on it and nothing under the hood at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-237-The hood turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-238-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-239-It went round the yard slowly, as a lamp is carried round a room: over the kettle, and the hearth, and the rows of barrels on end. It came to the two that stood apart, and rested there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-240-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-241-Cael did not breathe, and he did not want to be elsewhere. He had learned that in the corners of halls, and at the edges of yards, and it had never been asked of him by anybody who stood so near.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-242-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-243-He counted ten. The hood went on round, as slowly as it had come, to the wall and the quay steps; and the man went down the steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-244-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:245:At the foot, for the length of two strides, the lantern was on him from above. Cael saw a pair of horseman's boots go through its light, with a square of paler leather at each heel. He saw gloved hands, clasped behind, and the back of a hood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-246-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-247-He did not know whether he had been seen. He thought, afterward, that the man had looked at the place where a follower would be standing if there were one, and had neither found him nor needed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-248-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-249-"You did not come through after him," said a voice at his shoulder. "That was well judged."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-250-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-268-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-269-"He has shown us the ferry," said Seln. "He wished us to think of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-270-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-271-They followed by the steps. There was a lane above, and at its head the ring road, lying under the second wall like a ditch under a dyke; and there the man turned right, and went along it at his stroll, with the pale stone going up on his left hand higher than a house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-272-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:273:Cael knew that road. He had walked the whole round of it on his second day in the town, by himself, and had not once lifted his eyes above the top of that wall. He did not lift them now; he looked at a place twenty strides ahead of a long coat, on the cobbles, and went home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-274-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-275-It was a quarter of the way round the bluff, and it took the better part of an hour, and the man did not once look round. He stopped twice: once to do up a boot, with his back to them, for a long minute; and once for no reason, in the middle of the way, with his hands behind him. Each time one of the two walked on past him, on the far side, with his chin down; and each time the other had him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-276-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:277:The watch's cart came toward them, by the chandler's, with its lantern jogging. The man in the hood lifted two fingers to it as it passed, and one of the leather caps nodded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-278-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-279-"They know him," said Cael, in the dark of a warehouse door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-280-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-281-"They know that he may be out. It is a different thing, and it is the thing that matters." Seln's voice was as mild as ever. "You and I have gone round this town tonight like mice in a wall. He has gone round it like a man taking the air."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-282-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-352-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-353-"Eleven guards on one broker's doors can go through that gate at night, and every Iron in the district. I do not call it a finding. I call it a wall." Seln was looking up the street at the red point of the fire. "But I shall tell you what I have, since you have earned the half of it. He was not learning whether he was followed. He knew that from the trough. He took us round by the one shed where we must divide, and stood eleven minutes in it; he showed us a ferry; he showed us a watch that nods to him; and then he went to bed, through a thing that we cannot pass, and turned in the door so that we should be sure of having seen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-354-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-355-"Why?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-356-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:357:"So that we should know how much time they have. That is the whole of it. They need not take you in a stair. They may stand under a lamp for a month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-358-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-359-Cael thought about that, with the cold of the doorway at his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-360-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-361-"He wanted me to do something," he said. "At the barrels. Or at that gate. He stood five yards off and looked at where I was, for a count of ten. It'd have been the easiest thing I've ever done, to be gone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-362-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-363-"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-364-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:365:"I don't think it was the stair they shut, two nights ago. I think it was this. They want it shown, and they'll wait."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-366-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-367-"Then they have been shown a porter, twice." The small grey man came out of the doorway. "It was worth one night to learn that it is not worth two. I shall not follow him again; and I should be obliged if you did not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-368-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-369-They went back by the muck-lane. The fist came out from under two blankets as they passed the end stall, and opened, palm upward, on the straw. *Nothing?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-370-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:371:Cael turned his own hand over, empty, and the old man shut his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-372-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:373:There was a greyness over the roofs by then, and he washed at the pump, and the tar would not come off his palms; he had not known that he had leaned upon a barrel. He wrote three lines by the last of the lantern, with the book held at the length of his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-374-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-375-*Night of the 291st. Followed the boots, with S., round the whole of the lower town. All of them asked; Shadow asked for three hours. One look that I didn't mean, with the twelfth, at the rope-walk: a grain in each eye. Not counted. Nothing opened.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-376-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-377-*He may go through the blue gate at night. I may not go through it at all, except as a figure. It is a hand wide.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md-378-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-3-The tall book lay open on the table's bare end, and the ninth sheet of the Stair lay beside it, foot to foot, in the round of the carried lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-5-Cael had the best place in the gallery for both, as he had for everything, and the worst way up. From his knee he saw a page of the cramped brown hand, line under line, with a figure at the end of each: some clerk of the old town had set down what came in at a gate on one day, and what was paid on it. A hand's breadth off lay Karis's hard clean pencil, in rows, with the white pebbles and the black still lying where she had laid them that forenoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:7:The man had not begun at once. He stood with his hands behind him and his eyes shut, as he had stood on the first morning while she laid out fifty-two sheets. Karis stood on the rock where he had put her. She was a head shorter than he was, and she had come up a little on her toes without knowing it, to have her eyes where his would be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-9-Then he opened them, and looked at the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:11:He looked from its foot. Cael had moved the flame before the grey head moved, and it was there. The eyes went up the brown page slowly, without a blink, and the water came into them, and they went up the sheet beside it. Then, which they had never done in eight days, they went back and forth between the two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-13-He spoke while he looked. That was new as well. He had always looked first, and said afterward what the looking had found, in as few words as would carry it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-14-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-15-"A record," Cleon said, "sits."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-17-His chin had gone toward the brown page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-19-"A load came in at a gate. It was paid on, and when the day was done a clerk put it down. He might have begun at the head of his column or the foot of it; it'd be the same tally. There's not a mark on that leaf that's waiting for anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:21:The eyes came across to her pencil, and the water ran.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-22-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-23-"An instruction *leans*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-24-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-25-He said it more slowly, as a man says a thing that he has only ever said to himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-26-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-27-"Whoever cut this set each sign where it is for the sake of the one after it. Take them out of their order and you've a pile of chippings. Leave them in it, and you're handed on, sign to sign, whether you can read a letter of it or not. I don't read it. I'm walked down it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-29-He looked from the one to the other once more, the book and then the sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:31:"This is a stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-33-Nobody in the gallery moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-35-Brom had come up as far as the table's corner, and stopped there, with his arms hanging. Lira sat against a table's leg with her chin on her knees. On a box by the nearest bay, a long grey man had turned his head toward the two things on the table, and his hands had gone still on the ash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:37:Karis was not looking at the sheet any longer. She had turned, on the spot where she had been put, and her eyes were going down the gallery: over the Hall's twelve, and the long table with its three rows, and the Ring's eleven beyond. She looked at them as she had once looked at a bluff from six miles off, counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-39-"The strata lean too."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-41-She had said it very low, and to herself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-42-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-48-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-49-"There," said the man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-50-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-51-It came at that word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-52-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:53:He did not know it for what it was; he knew only that something at the tail of his eye had altered, and he took it for the lamp. A dark thing stood propped against the table's edge, with its head a little back, and it had four rows cut in it; and all four had gone aslant, the same way, like the rails of four stairs seen from below. He looked at his own hand: the ring hung from two fingers, and the flame stood up like a painted one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-54-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-55-So he looked at the sheet, where the finger was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-57-Nothing on the paper had stirred; the signs stood where her pencil had put them, square and small, each in its place. But the fifth row had a way to it now, as a field of barley has a way when the wind has gone over it and every stalk still bows a little toward where the wind went. The white sign at the head stood up straight, and all that followed it hung from it, and inclined, each toward the next. At the row's end the lean came down flat, like a foot arriving on a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-58-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-61-He knew it then, and he knew whose it was. He had been a hand to its left for six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-63-He had read every paper of his life from the wrong side of somebody's desk, with its head toward the person who owned it. It was as though a hand had reached across, without a word, and turned the sheet about, so that for once it lay facing him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:65:He put his eyes on his own knuckles, and kept them there. There was a table under that sheet, with pegs in it, and a ring of brass in his fingers, and a desk six strides off, and a stair of dark stone that somebody had laid; and the new thing inclined toward each of them in turn, mildly, like a head coming round. He did not let it look. He had been given a pair of eyes that were not his, in a room that belonged to the man they came from, and he did not know yet what they cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-67-It was the twelfth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-68-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-69----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-70-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-83-"Fifteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-85-Cael knelt, and kept the light where it was wanted; and he found that he had put it there, twice, before the finger moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:87:He had done as much for three days, and it was not the same. He had moved the lamp then because the eyes always went one way, and he had learned the way as a groom learns a horse. He moved it now because he could see where the row was going. A made thing was looked at from where it stood upon the ground, toward where it went, and a light set anywhere else lay across the grain of it. He was glad to have that; it explained six days of *left* and *up a finger*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-89-It also made him a thief, or would by the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:91:A man had shown a stranger the inside of his trade, for the first time in thirty years, because she had asked him honestly for something that he could not hand her; and the boy who held his lamp had come away with a piece of it. He had not reached. He never did reach. But he would kneel at that elbow tomorrow, and the day after, with the man's own way of looking folded behind his eyes; and each time that the finger went along a row, he would know where it was going before it went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-93-That was not a thing that had been taken once, in an alley, and now sat idle. It would be taken again every hour, for as long as the lamp was his. He would have to tell him. He did not see how; he saw only that it would not keep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-94-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-95-"Sixteen," said Karis. She had dust on her cheek, and her left hand had come up out of her lap and was holding the tall book's leaf flat, on its bound palm, as though it had forgotten that it was hurt. "It is a score. It is the old word for twenty, and there are a hundred of them on this page."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-96-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-100-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-101-The thing leaned at the tall book, when her hand turned the leaf. *Sits*, it would have said of that page, if he had let it: he could feel the word waiting, flat and level, like a board laid on two trestles. It leaned at the lamp-ring, and at the joint of the table's leg, a foot from his knee, where one peg did the work and another had been put in for show. He told it no each time. It was an easier thing to refuse than Shadow, which pulled, or the wall, which asked; this one only offered, like a clerk holding out a sheet that need not be taken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-102-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-103-Near the bell it offered one thing more, and that was not a table's leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-104-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:105:He had come to that town with a place under his breastbone which five readings had gone round and none had entered, and he had written at a fire that he wanted an instrument for what does not move. He had one now, resting an inch behind his eyes, as quiet as a folded rule. It would take no time; he need not so much as shift his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-106-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-107-*Write it down and stop touching it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-108-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-109-A healer had put that on him, on a plank, a long way east. He had got round her words once already, at a waypost, by going about the place and not into it; and he had been ashamed enough of that to write it, and to owe her the telling. He did not get round them now. She would be at a mill inside of eight days, and she could be asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-110-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:111:*Not until she's heard it*, he said, to the thing behind his eyes; and it lay down, having only ever offered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-112-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-113-"Seventeen," said Karis, when the flame had begun to smoke. She said it as a person says the name of a town that she has walked to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-114-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-115-"Of twenty-three," said the man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-116-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-120-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-121-"I know what I have. I did not know this morning that I should have it so quickly." She looked up at him, from the chair. "You have never shown that to anybody."
