editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:325:Cael had set his boot on the nineteenth tread, with the case, and the lamp was above him, eight steps up, in Lira's hand. It was a small brown flame in a smoked glass. He had knelt beside it for six days. It went into his eyes now like a handful of sand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-326-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:327:He shut them. That was worse; they burned behind the lids, and the water came out from under, hot, and ran down beside his nose. He opened them upon a smear. There was a blot of light, and a dark upright thing that might have been a girl, and where the steps should have been there was nothing that he could have set a foot to.
