editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:355:It was true, and it was the only part that he had leave to give her. She waited for the rest. He felt her wait, the whole length of a vault that he crossed with his lids shut and her fingers in his sleeve; and he let her go on waiting, which he had never in his life done to that girl before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-356-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md-357-Cael did not tell anyone. He remembered.
     1	# Chapter 39 — Aimed
     2	
     3	Vastin had been given three words in his life for the thing that he had served, and he set them out in their order, since he had never been able to think about a matter until it was set out.
     4	
     5	He did it on a box, by a bay of bundles, in the afternoon of the two hundred and ninety-third day. Nobody wanted him. The lamp had come back to the long table, and the boy knelt beside it, a hand to the left, as he had knelt on every other afternoon. The small pale woman was making a second copy. The man in the dusty coat sat in his chair with his eyes shut. It was a room with one office in it, and Vastin had not once that day felt his boot begin to move.
     6	
     7	The first word was *imperfect*.
     8	
     9	He had held that one for very nearly the whole of his service, and he had held it comfortably. A scale has an error. A careful officer learns the error, and allows for it, and does not throw away the scale. He had sat through eleven fields and nine thousand readings on that footing. Where a plate gave a wrong answer he had called it a wrong answer, and had written so, and had been thought rather too scrupulous by people who were not obliged to sign.
    10	
    11	The second had been given him by a file.
    12	
    13	He had read it by the candles of four inns, a long while ago, and had carried it up a bluff to a house above Ostrand; and there five young people had shown him what the office that he served did with honest readings. He had said one sentence afterward, to a boy, in a room that was not his own. He had it by heart. He had never been sure that the boy did.
    14	
    15	*I knew the system was imperfect. I didn't know it was designed to be.*
    16	
    17	That had cost him a seal, left in a tray on a morning of rain, and three hundred miles on a hedge-stick. He had thought it the end of the matter. A man finds that the scale was made to read false; he lays down his office; he goes to dig up the one buried thing that he has the knowledge to find. He had supposed, on the road, that the thing would turn out to be a fraud. He had been ready for a fraud. He had dealt with a great many.
    18	
    19	It was not a fraud. A fraud wants something. It leans the scale so that a house may keep its place, or a seat its revenue, and when the leaning is found there is at least a person behind it who has got richer.
    20	
    21	He had heard a leaf of skin read out that morning, once, by a woman who would not say it twice. He did not say it over. He had been asked not to, and he was a man who kept to what he was asked; he had learned that lately, and at some cost.
    22	
