     6	
     7	Brom worked the handle for him, low, from the wrist, and the cold took the sand out by degrees. By the time the pot was called at the half-door he could tell a lantern from the thing that it lit, and he went up the ladder on his own feet.
     8	
     9	There were six bowls in the loft, and a seventh that had gone down to a stall.
    10	
    11	"He's got straw to his chin," said Lira. She had carried it. "He said that he was obliged to the horse for the loan. I think he meant it."
    12	
    13	Cael ate, since Brom was watching him do it; and then he took out Hesk's book, and held it a long way from his face, and wrote the day.
    14	
    15	*Two hundred and ninety-first day. Treswick; the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty-four. By the carts' street, both ways, on wheels: a weight chalked at the bottom gate to the name on the card, and to nobody else. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
    16	
