295:The lines had stood behind his eyes for an hour, as each of the eleven had stood, and he had not looked at them. He had had a lamp to hold, and then a thing to say. He took them now, on a stair, a tread at a time, as he had taken the last upon a pallet: slowly, from the top, in the plain words in which they would have to go into Hesk's book.
