editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-259-"He saw you," said Lira. "That clerk. I watched him do it. He looked at you like a thing that he was going to draw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-260-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-261-"He did. He will know me in ten years, in another town, in a different coat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-262-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:263:The old man had come up the ladder last, on one leg and two hands, and he stood at the gable under the blanket and had not yet let go of the stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-264-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-265-"I have said that I am sorry. I am told that it is the wrong coin." His voice was level, and it cost him to keep it so. "I shall say then only what was done. You had kept your face from every desk in the Compact for fifteen years, and I spent it for you in a breath, to save a clerk an hour."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-266-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-267-"You spent nothing. I stood up. It was my own leg that I did it with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-268-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-269-"You might have had it cheaper," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-270-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-271-He had not meant to ask; but it had gone round in him through four hours of holding a lamp, and he wanted it out.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-295-"That's not well," she said, very low. "You've put that he's taking it as you did. You took it like a cat takes a bath."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-296-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-297-"I know how I took it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-298-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-299-"He said sorry. Out loud, in a yard, to the one of us he'd most cause to be afraid of." She gave him the book. "You never said it to anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-300-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-301-"There wasn't anybody to say it to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-302-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:303:"No," said Lira. "That was the trouble with it." She lay down on her pallet, on the right side, with the slow arm uppermost. "I held that lamp an hour. My hand's still going. How do you do it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-304-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-305-"I stop wanting to be anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-306-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-307-"That's horrible."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-308-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md-309-"It's useful," said Cael; and he shut the book on the pencil, and found that he meant both.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-1-# Chapter 38 — Read Once
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-3-The tall books went back first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-5-The man in the dusty coat had ruled it from his chair, on the two hundred and ninety-third morning, before any of them had a load off. It was the seventh day of his week; there were twelve of the old town's tally-books lying open along a table's edge, and he would not have a tape touched while one of them was out of its bay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-7-"As they stood. Third bay on the left, and the shelves she had them off. The lamp can go with them; I'll not want it here." His eyes were on the floor between two boxes. "I've a light of my own for this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:9:So Lira went up the ladder in the eastern gallery, with a book as broad as a paving stone against her chest, and Brom stood at the foot with the ring in his left fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-11-"You said that I was a wall," he said, over his shoulder, to nobody who was listening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:13:"A wall will do for a ladder," said Lira, from the dark. "Higher. No. Lower."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-14-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-15-Cael had turned to go with them. He had not been four strides from that flame since the third hour of the second day, and his hand felt wrong without it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-17-"Stay," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-19-She stood by the second chair, with her notebook against her chest. She had slept; he could see that she had, and that it had been done on purpose, as a person eats before a long road; and it had not taken the white out of her face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-21-"I should like one of you here. I do not wish to read it to a chair, and a man who shuts his eyes." She drew a breath. "You have knelt at this for eight days, and been given a sore knee for it. I think that you are owed the end of the week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-22-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-23-"I've nothing to hold."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-24-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-25-"Then hold nothing. Stand there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-26-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-27-He stood at the desk's left side, a hand off, where the lamp would have been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:29:There was a fourth in that end of the gallery. The old man sat on Brom's box by the nearest bay, six strides from the desk, with the bound leg out along its plank and both hands on the ash; he could not have followed a ladder, and he was only where he had been set down, as he was every morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-31-The man reached up to the shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-33-He took the bundle in both hands; it was thicker than a loaf, and tied across both ways, and it left a clean square in the dust where it had lain. He set it upon the slope of his desk, under the good lamp, and stood looking at the knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-35-"I'll not cut it," he said. "Whoever tied that meant it to be untied."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md-37-It took him some time. The tape had gone brown and stiff, and it cracked under his nails like the skin of an onion; but the knot came, a turn at a time, and he laid the two lengths out to either side as flat as rulers. Under the tape there were two thin boards of beech, and he lifted the upper one away.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-73-Vastin had stood on eleven fields. He had given that figure beside a hold's hearth, and again on a bank, and Cael had taken it for a count of honours. It was a count of something else. A man does not hold a line for forty years at gates and hearings without learning what is done at the other end of a street to make him look away from his own; and when a thing was shown him at the bottom of a stair, with a great noise, it was to the top that his head went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-74-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-75-"Above," he said. It was said quite low.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-76-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-77-Cael looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-79-There were four at the head of the cut, against the strip of sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:81:One knelt on the highest landing, with a thing laid along his forearm that Cael knew before he could see it plainly. It was short, and it had a bow across its end. A second stood over him with a stave. A third was already coming down the flight below them, in the middle of the steps, as a man walks who knows that the ground is his. And at the very top, where the slot opened beside the parapet, the fourth stood by himself. He had one hand on a bar of iron that was let into the wall there for people coming up under loads. In the other he held a lantern, low, against his coat, with its shutter closed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-83-They were hooded, all four, and there was a cloth across each face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-85-It was a stair a stride and a half wide. It had no door in it, and no window, and nothing to stand behind. There were ten yards of it between a crossbow and an old man's chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-87-Cael saw the bow come up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-89-The stick came down.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-137-Brom had not straightened. Something had gone in his left shoulder as the stone left him, deeper than what the dray had done, and he was bent over it with his teeth showing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-139-The second came past his fellow in one stride. His stave was up in both hands, above his hood, and it was coming down on a bowed head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-141-Lira went off the landing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-143-She had been on the second flight, two steps under Cael's heels, with a box on her back and a small pale woman between her and the fight. She had got out of her beckets. Cael never learned how; the box was found afterward standing square on the seventh step, as if she had set it there for a seat. She went down past Karis, who had pressed herself to the wall, and took the two strides that the landing gave her; and she did not go on down the flight. She stepped off its head into the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:145:It was not far, as she reckoned falls: the height of a table, and the length of a man outward. She came down over Brom's box, with her knees drawn up and the long knife in her right hand, and her left arm was folded against her ribs as tight as a wing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-147-She did not strike at the man. She struck at what he held. The knife went into the ash between his two fists, point first, with the whole drop behind it, and stood there to a third of its length.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-149-Her boots hit the step in the same instant. Her knees went, and took it. She hung from the hilt with all that she weighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-151-A stave cannot be swung with a girl upon it. It came down between them, slowly, like a gate-bar, and its shod end struck the tread and stayed. The man had a hand free. He let go with it, and reached for her hood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-152-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-153-Brom's boot met him at the hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-154-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-155-It was a carter's shove, with a leg like a newel post, from a man who had got his breath; and the hooded one went backward down three steps and into the other, and the pair of them into the gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-157-Lira had gone over to the left with the stave. The wall was there. She took it on the point of her shoulder, and Cael heard that. She slid down it, and sat, with a man's stave across her knees and her own knife standing up out of the middle of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:159:"It didn't touch," she said, to nobody, through her teeth. She was holding the left arm against her with the right. "It didn't touch a thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-160-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-161-There was a small sound higher up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-162-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-163-Karis had flattened herself to let the girl go by. She had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had carried them up and down that stair for seven days, and she had not a hand to spare for the wall. Her boot was on the sixth step. It was the wet one, worn into a dish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-164-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-165-It went from under her. She sat down hard on the stair, and the flat did not leave her arm. It was her left hand that went out, flat, to save the rest of her, and found the lip of the step above.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-166-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-167-That stone had not been worn round. It had been cut to an edge by whoever laid it, before there were walls on that hill, and seven days of porters going by on the other side had done nothing to blunt it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-168-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-169-She did not cry out. She looked at her palm, and then she shut it, and put it inside her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-170-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-171-"The flat is dry," she said, in a voice that was not quite steady. "I should like that known."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-172-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-173-The two at the gate had got up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:175:One had no stave now. He looked up the stair, past Brom, and it was not at any person that he looked. He looked at a box lying across a step. He went to it, low, with one arm up against a boot that did not come, and got his fist through its becket, and hauled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-177-Brom sat down on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-178-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:179:He could do nothing else, and so he did that. He lowered himself onto the lid, with his two arms hanging and his heels dug in against the tread. The man below pulled at a rope with a great deal of a Copper from Velmere seated on the other end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-181----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-182-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-183-Cael had turned back uphill before the slab was out of Brom's hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-184-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-185-It was the hardest thing that he had done on that stair. Everybody that he had was behind him: a girl going through the air, and a big man with no arms, and a small one with a courier's flat. He heard the stone go down, and the gate take a man's back. He heard a knife go into wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-186-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md-187-He had the whole ten, and they had come up in him like hounds to a gate.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-81-He had met the boy across a desk, with a plate between them, on a bluff. He had read him for three days, with every instrument that the seat owned, and had written that the instruments could not reach him. He had declined to read him again. He had told the boy why, on a bank, at dusk: the sound reason, and the poor one beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-83-He had thought, through all of that, that he was dealing with an error. A plate had failed upon one child, as a scale fails upon a weight that is out of its range. It had been his whole understanding of the case, and he had built a resignation and a road upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-85-He stopped there, on the dark stone, with one hand lifted to the next tread. He could feel that the thought had a second half; and the second half was the sentence again, come round by another door. He saw it for what it was. *Draft nothing. Just look.* He put the hand down upon the stone, which was cold and dry and laid without mortar, and looked at that instead until the thing had gone by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-87-He went on up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:89:There was a matter that he could settle, and he gave the second flight to it. They would sit in a yard tonight. The big young man had said so at noon: there would be a fire by the stall door, since one of them could not climb a ladder; and he knew who the one was. He would be at that fire. The leaf would be read there by those who had not heard it. And the boy would be across the flames from him, with all of it to carry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-91-He owed that boy a sentence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-93-He began, from forty years' habit, to set it in order. There would be three heads. The first would deal with what he had believed; the second with what he had been shown above Ostrand; the third —
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-94-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-95-He caught it at the third head, and very nearly laughed, alone, on a stair, in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-96-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-97-He would not draft it. He would say what was so, when the time came, in whatever words were in his mouth; and if it came out badly, it would at least not have been composed. He thought that the girl with the long knife would ask him something afterward. She generally did. He thought that he knew what, and that he would have to answer it truly, and that the true answer was not one that a man would choose to give across a fire.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md-277-"I didn't come to be fed."
