editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-77----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-79-The factor came down from the lip while she was counting, leading a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:81:It was a hold's mule, old and sour, with a sack over its eyes, and the factor took it by the cheek-strap and walked it into the cut at the foot, between the dead carts, on the dry edge. Cael understood that he was watching the only test that such a person trusted. They came up the whole hundred strides, past six people and a healer, with a pair of pale eyes going to each grey shape in turn. They went as far as the black wet rock at the head. They turned there, and came down again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-83-"Five," said the factor, at Brom's stone. "I said five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-85-"There were eight," said Cael. "Three lay under your north bank, where nobody could have counted them. They went back over it, and down, and out onto the ice. It let them through."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-86-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-178-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-179-"He'll pay till somebody tells him that he needn't," said Lira. "And then he'll want to know by what rule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-181-Oryn had been standing by the hearth's end with her bowl. She watched the door until it had shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-182-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:183:"He took every word of that," she said. "About the month. He heard it, and he told me that he couldn't undertake it. And he's not knelt since, or gone faster than a walk, or leaned on anything but that stick." Her spoon went round the bowl. "I've had patients who said *yes* to everything and did none of it. I never had one before who said *I cannot undertake*, and then did it to the letter, as if it were a thing that he'd thought of himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-184-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-185----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-186-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-187-Cael wrote it in the byre loft, on his knee, by the light that came up between the boards from the hold's lantern.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-188-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-189-The others were down already in the hay, where he could hear Brom turning his foot about to find a way that it would lie. He began as he began every entry, with the day, and with what could be counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-190-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-191-*Two hundred and seventy-ninth day. The shoulder hold. The cut is clear of eight: five killed, by a rock twice, by a knife twice, and once by a thing that I shall come to; three through the ice of the sink. Twenty paid at the gate. In hand, a hundred and thirty-eight. Brom's right foot, two toes, ten days. Vastin's right knee: four holes shut by Oryn, the joint bound, on a stick for a month by her word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:193:*I planned it wrong. They do not go to where a thing is declared. On that ice they go to where it stops. I put Karis and Oryn and the mule at the foot and called it the safe end.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-195-He stopped there, with the pencil's point on the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-197-He had gone back over it a dozen times since the ice. Eight hounds would have come down to the foot of that cut, and found three quiet things, and stood. They would have tried one of the three with their teeth. He believed that it would have held; a healer and a researcher knew how to be posts, and a mule answered with nothing that a hound hunted. He believed it. He had not been asked to watch it tried, since a man with a stick had put a road's width of refusal in front of it, on his own judgment, with a knee that he knew would not carry him. The rule had been broken, and he had broken a rule himself, twice, for a calf and for a carter. He could not find it in him to enter the breaking as a fault.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md-198-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-1-# Chapter 20 — Wayposts
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-3-The hold came off at the first hedge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:5:It was Seln who took it off. He had gone down the western side of the rise thirty yards ahead of the boxes, as he went ahead of everything; and at the foot, where the ruts ran in between two banks of thorn, he stopped and let the file come up to him. He had not done that on any road since the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-7-"We may talk," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-9-Nobody did. They had come two hundred and eighty-one days through a country where a road was a place for hands and not for mouths, and the habit had gone deeper into them than any of the six had known.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-10-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-193-Every line of it was about a thing that moved. It told him where the Wind leaned, and how the millstone went by. There was not one line in it about the place. There never had been. *Doesn't flow. Doesn't change.* He had written those on a leaf, and had taken them for findings about the place; and they were not. They were only the instrument's way of saying where it left off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-195-The going-along felt current, and it had never done anything else. It had been a healer's means of telling how the rivers ran in a body, quick or slow or choked, and it knew a river only by going along with it. Set it where nothing ran, and it did not come back and say *empty*. It did not come back and say *full*, either. It had nothing to go along with, and so it said nothing at all; and for the better part of a year he had been writing that nothing down in pencil, with the day at its head, as though it had been an answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:197:He had been putting a cord on a stone and writing down *no length*. A cord is made to be run out along a road, and it tells a person how far he may go. Laid on a boulder in a field it has nowhere to run. A patient man may walk it round the outside, and knot it, and learn how far the thing is about; but of what lies in the stone the cord gives no figure whatever, and it was that blank which he had been entering, with the day at its head, as though the stone had told him so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-199-He took his fingers from his wrist, and put his right hand down to push himself up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-200-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-201-It came down on the foot of the gatepost. The going-along had not been called home yet. It went down his arm to the palm, as it had gone to a healer's wrist when he had her leave, and there it stood. The stone gave it nothing to travel on. He had always known that it would not; the tenth did nothing whatever on a dead thing, and he had never troubled to think about that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md-202-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-135-*not read*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-137-"I do not write *illegible*," she said, to nobody. "A thing is illegible when the hand is poor. This was a good hand. Somebody prevented it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:139:She went down her copy against the sheet, a word at a time, with the butt of her pencil. Then she did as she had been asked. She slipped the tape of the uppermost row and lifted that row out whole, and slid the technician's sheet in at the foot of the row beneath, and laid the first back upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-141-"The second row, at its end," she said. "I shall know where to find it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-143-The lamp had begun to smoke. Brom said so, from the door, and Lira leaned over from the meal-sacks and pinched the wick down between a wetted finger and thumb; and it was in that small business that Cael looked for Seln, and did not find him where he had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-144-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-226-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-227----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-228-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-229-Six people stood in a room that had been built for sacks, and nobody put a hand to the latch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-230-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:231:Karis had come back to her stool. She took off the gloves a finger at a time and laid them on the flat. Under the lamp lay a courier's leather with six rows in it, and a barred sheet at the foot of the second, and beside the leather a broker's paper; and at the far end, in the half dark, there lay a page on a square of oilcloth, with a title on it and a place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-232-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-233-Cael's right hand was on the deal beside that page. It had been there since the lamp was first carried down to him, and it had not gone a finger nearer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:235:He knew what was written there, and so did everybody in the room. It was a town inside the Line, behind a gate with a book in it, under a station; and there was a paper in a drawer that would be served upon four of them within the hour if any one of their names were written there. That had been the price nine days ago. A man who used to certify such papers had told them at noon that it was the price still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-237-Nobody said the word for where the town lay. Karis had said it aloud once, at the board, and it had gone round in him for nine days; tonight the town itself lay beside his hand, in a square black copy, a hand's width off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-238-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-239-"You said that you'd rule when the boat was in," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md-240-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-137-"Could you stand on it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-139-"With two arms," said Lira. "I could now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:141:He let that go by, since she had not said it to be answered. He was looking at the foot of the cut, away to his left, where the banks ran out and the road came into the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-143-There was a cart there, on its side, with one wheel in the air. Beyond it lay the bed of a second, without wheels, and a scatter of grey staves that had been barrels. A third had gone clean off the road at the bottom and lay in the heather with its pole snapped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:145:"All three," he said. "Right at the foot. Not one of them's in the cut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-147-"They slid," said Lira. "It's a hill, and it's glass. Whatever goes on that ice at the top is at the bottom before it stops."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-149-"So that's where it was done. Whatever was done to them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-150-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-218-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-219-Karis stopped there, at the bank's end, with the mule's cheek-strap in one hand and Hesk's clock in the other. Oryn stood beside her with her arms bare to the elbow. Neither of them said anything, since there was nothing that the road allowed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-220-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-221-The cold came up out of the cut to meet him like the air from a cellar. He kept to the south edge, where a strip of dead turf and gravel ran beside the ice, a little wider than his two boots. The read asked at every stride what the stuff weighed, and Anchor asked *There?* of the wheel-ruts frozen into it, in its thin held note, as it asked at every sill; he told the one that he knew, and the other no. He did not look at the north bank, which was no higher there than his belt, or think of the strip under it that a lip of rock had hidden.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-222-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:223:At sixty strides there was a flat stone at the road's edge, and Brom stepped onto it and set his feet, with the rock held against his thigh. Vastin stopped behind him. Cael did not see where Lira went; there was a small dry rattle on the scree above, and then nothing. Seln had left them at the foot, and was already nowhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-224-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-225-Brom looked at him, once. Then the big man put his right hand flat, palm down, at his side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-226-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-227-Cael went on by himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-228-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-235-A seed of yellow stood on the tar. It grew for one breath to the size of a barley grain, and that was all he gave it. He shut Ember as he would have shut a hand. The heat that ought to have gone somewhere came home along his left arm instead, though he had been holding nothing, and the old weal across that palm woke and stung as though he had laid it on a kettle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-237-He turned his back on the mark, and walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-238-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:239:Wind had his feet before the first stride was down. It leaned him at the foot of the cut with everything that it had, and he told it no, and found that his own two legs had something to offer in its place: a walk, a little bent, close under the scree, such as a girl on a bench had beaten into him with the two halves of a count. He did not run. A thing that ran on that road would be looked at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-240-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-241-He heard them at the tenth stride. It was a sound like gravel poured from a cart, away up on his left, beyond the bank; and it did not come from three places. It came from the whole upper half of the cut together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-242-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-243-They came over the north bank at the mark, as Lira had said that they would, and he turned his head as far as a post may. There were five, grey and long, pouring over the worn places with their heads level. And behind those, out from under the strip that nobody had been able to see, there came three more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md-244-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md-197-Cael had never heard a room change so without a sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md-199-Nobody started, since they were not people who started. But Karis's right hand, which had been lying on her notebook, had gone flat upon it as if the leaf might blow away; Seln's eyes had left the yard, and Lira's fingers, on the board, had drawn in. Brom's two hands had stopped on the board. And at the board's end a healer who had swallowed a sentence nine days ago, at this same table, when a station was spoken of, stood with her bowl in both hands and looked at nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md-200-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:201:It was nine days since a small pale woman had laid her palm on a strip of oilcloth and said that town's name for the first time in his hearing. She had come to it from the east, off fifty-two sheets of rubbed stone and a dead man's ledger. This man had come to it from the other side, out of the Compact's own house and down a stair. Neither had known that the other was on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md-202-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md-203-He had a rule about lines. Two points could be ruled together by anybody, and he had been joined to things himself on no better evidence. He did not join these. He only saw where each of them stopped, and it was the same door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md-204-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md-205-Vastin had taken his eyes from Karis. They went along the board, in the order that they kept, and came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md-206-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-17-She had unloaded the three boxes herself and turned the mule out on the green. She had looked once at six heaps of an office's paper weighted down with river stones, and her mouth had gone as it went at the smell of a wound that had been left too long. Then she had taken her strip of canvas to the basin, and a cake of grey soap, and begun to wash linen with her back to all of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-19-"That's your trade," she said, when Lira asked. "I'll hear what it comes to. I'll not stand and watch it done."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:21:Brom sat on the basin's rim with his right boot off and his bound toes in the overflow, which was as cold as a knife. He had been told by nobody to do it, and the healer had looked at the foot, and said that it was sense, and gone on wringing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-22-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-23-"Now," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-24-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-25-She had gone back to the uppermost row. She went through it a third time, quickly, looking only at the heads; and this time she did not count upon her fingers, as she had by the lamp. She sorted. Where the four signs at a sheet's head were the same four as on the sheet before, it stayed where it lay. Where they were another four, she turned its corner up. She could not read a sign of them. She could tell one group from another as she could tell Brom's face from Lira's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-26-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-76-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-77-"That's the second," said Brom. A finger went up beside the thumb. He was looking at the water, and at nobody. "Their tools work in there. It's sigils that stop, and nothing else that they carried."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-79-Karis did not lift her eyes from the sheet. She had a hair pressed in the fold of her notebook, and a line under it that said *no inference entered*, and she turned the leaf of the abstract over a little faster than she had turned the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:81:"There is a sixth thing on the older forms," she said. "It is not a column. It is a space at the foot, ruled off, and headed *Remarks*. It is empty on every sheet in the three lowest rows but one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-83-She drew that one out. It was from the fourth row, and it had the Stair's signs at its head, and the number thirty-two. At its foot, in the ruled space, there were four words. They were not in any of the three clerks' hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-85-She did not read them at once. She reached behind her, to the tarred case, and brought out the dead man's ledger, and laid it open beside the sheet; and she looked from the one to the other for as long as it took Brom to draw his foot out of the water. Then she shut the ledger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-86-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-131-"The fifth is yours. You found the remark; I looked for what came after it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-133-He drew a sheet from the third row, and one from the second, and laid them beside the Stair's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:135:"On the form that those four words were written upon, there is a space at the foot for remarks. On the next year's printing there is none. The rule that closed it off has been taken out of the block, and the fifth column runs to the foot of the sheet." His mild voice did not alter. "Somebody read them. It is the only sign in six rows that anybody read anything at all; and what he did, having read them, was to see that no surveyor should have room to write four more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-137-Lira let her breath go through her teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-139-"That's the third, then, and it's the worst," said Brom. He had three fingers up, and he looked at them. "One man wrote down what he thought. The year after, there's nowhere on the sheet to think."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-140-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-254-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-255-A sleeve came past his shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-256-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-257-He had not heard anybody cross the turf. A small dry hand laid a single sheet down on the rock in front of him, between his two hands, and squared it with one finger, and went away again. It was thin stock, and newer than the rest, and it had been folded once. It had a printed head. At its foot a bar of black lay across the paper, as broad as a thumb, cracked like mud in a rut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md-258-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:259:It had not been laid at the foot of the second row, where it belonged, and it had not been given to Karis, who was the keeper of it. It had been set before him. And the man who had set it there did not say one word.
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-29-"I've laid a bowl for you since a heap of stones. I never thought that it meant you'd go down a hole."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-31-She looked at him, and the corner of her mouth went down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:33:"There's a thing that none of you has asked me in two hundred and eighty days, and I've been glad of it. I'll say it once, since it bears on what I want of him." She did not look at Cael. "I'd my rank off a board at the foot of a bed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-35-"Iron," said Lira. "The fourth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:37:"Provisional. I never stayed to have it confirmed." She rubbed her palms together slowly. "It was a house for the sick, inside a wall, with forty beds and six of us to tend them. Every bed in it had a sheet on a board at its foot. There was the name on it, and the complaint, and what had been spent. And there was a column for the patient's tier."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:39:"For the patient's," said Karis. Her pencil was not in her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-41-"Not for the healer's. For theirs. I filled that column in for a long while before I asked what it was for; I thought that it was like the name. Then there was a week of fever in the low town, and the house was full to the stairs, and I found out." She spoke as she spoke a finding. "We'd so much in us, the six of us together, as I've so much in me tonight, and it had to be spent on somebody. It went down the column from the top. There was a Gold in a bed by the window with a wrenched wrist. He had what he wanted on the first day. Then the Silvers had theirs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-43-Nobody at the fire moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-44-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:45:"There was a woman brought in from the river sheds on the third night. She'd no tier that anybody could find. She'd never been stood on a plate, or her paper was lost; I never knew which. So there was nothing to put in the column." Oryn's eyes had gone to the peats. "A sheet with an empty column goes to the bottom of the heap. It's not cruelty. It's only how a heap is sorted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-46-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-47-"Did she die?" said Lira, very low.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-48-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-49-"She did not. I'll not have you think it worse than it was. I went to her at night, off the sheet, with what I had left, and it was enough. And in the morning the woman who kept that house showed me the board, where there was a spending and no tier written against it. She said that a healer who spends off the sheet has stolen from whoever stood next on it." Her spoon was in her hand, and she turned it over. "She was right. That's the thing that I've never got past. By that house's sums she was right, and I couldn't fault a line of them. So I went to look for some other sums."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-50-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-51-"Where was it?" said Brom. He liked a thing to have a place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-52-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:53:"I'll keep that. It's not shame." She set the spoon down in the bowl. "If I put a name to it, you'd think that it was that house, in that town, and that the next would be kinder. I asked, before I went. They keep the same sheet at the foot of every bed inside the Line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-54-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-55-"You left," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-57-"I walked out of a gate in the coat that I stood in, and I've not been through one since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-59----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:61:She let them have that for the space of three breaths, and Cael did not need it explained. He had been a sheet with an empty column since his Kindling; and he understood now why a healer who had never asked him for a tier had once looked at his hand, on a mountain, as a person looks at a shut press.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-63-"So I know what leaving is," said Oryn. "I've done it the once. It's when you go and you don't mean to come back to it, and you don't tell them where you'll be. I'll not have this mistaken for that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-65-She had turned from the fire, and her face was as level as he had ever seen it, and a good deal less tired than it had been at the cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-66-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-87-She came round the fire then. She stopped at her stride and a half from Cael, and he saw her mouth open; and he knew the sentence before it came, not by its words but by its shape, since he had watched it arrive in her three times and be shut in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-89-This time she did not shut it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:91:"You said a station, at that board. He said it first, by the fire, and then with a bowl turned over. I know stations. There's one on the crown of every city inside the Line, with steps going up to it and a square in front, and I've stood in such a square with a line of children and watched them go up one at a time." She did not push up her sleeves. "It's the one thing in a town that's built to read whatever is brought near it. And I've had my hands on you, a long while back, and I found a thing that I made you write down and leave alone. I've not touched it since. I don't know what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-93-"Nor do I," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-94-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:95:"No. So I don't know what a station does with it. That's the whole of my reason, and it's no better than that. I've no finding. I've a thing that I can't read, and a thing that reads, and I'd not walk any patient of mine past the one with the other in him until I knew." Her voice had not risen. "You go inside, you don't go near the station. Not the steps, not the square. You promise me in that log of yours, because I've seen what the log does to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-96-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-97-Nobody spoke, and Karis had lowered her head. Vastin, in the gap, was looking at the healer with an attention that Cael could not read; he had certified those stations, and he said nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-98-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-99-Cael did not ask her what she meant about the log, since he knew. A thing that he had only said, he could weigh again in the morning. A thing that he had written in Hesk's book was done; he had kept a count of uses for a whole cold term because a small pale woman had seen him write the number.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md-100-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-35-"Mine is not on that paper. I do not propose to offer it to a gate for all that. I should be asked where I had been employed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-37-So that left one; and Cael felt the loft come round to it, as he had once felt a table take care not to look at himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:39:An old man leaned at the foot of the gable, under the blanket, with both hands on his stick. He had come in by a yard, and climbed a ladder on one leg with a healer's laths on the other, and had not yet been heard to say what he thought of either. There was a flatness under the breast of the brown coat, on the left side. Cael had taken it for a receipt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-41-Vastin had seen them turn. He did not wait to be asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-43-"I have papers I will not show. I want that said once, so that nobody asks twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-44-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-59----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-61-Cael went out an hour after noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:63:He had not meant to. He had lain on a pallet with his hands behind his head and listened to the street, and he had found that he could not lie under a town which he had not looked at. He had looked at the foot of it for a day. He wished to see how it stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-65-"Which way?" said Lira. She had sat up as soon as his boots moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-67-"Round. Along under the second wall, with the wall on my left hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:99:"Empty's what I posted. I never asked anybody for safe. I've lived on this shoulder since I was born, and that seep'll have its glass back by morning." A hand went into the apron. "There's another den'll find it, in a year, or in five. They'll have to learn it from the beginning, as these did; and by then I'll have had thirty carts of salt up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:"A hundred and thirty-eight in hand," said Brom. He put the slip inside his shirt with the purse. "That's his, to draw the tack on. I'll have it after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:191:*Two hundred and seventy-ninth day. The shoulder hold. The cut is clear of eight: five killed, by a rock twice, by a knife twice, and once by a thing that I shall come to; three through the ice of the sink. Twenty paid at the gate. In hand, a hundred and thirty-eight. Brom's right foot, two toes, ten days. Vastin's right knee: four holes shut by Oryn, the joint bound, on a stick for a month by her word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:215:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:199:"A hundred and thirty-six," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:217:It was a small hold. It had a wall of field-stone, and a gate of oak bars, and a gate-house built against the wall's outer face, with a bench along it under a roof of turf. She had sat on that bench on thirty rounds. She had never once been through the gate, and it was a long while since anybody there had asked her to come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:301:*Deployment since the Line: thirty.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:83:But there were seven, and one bank, and it was thirty feet from the road to the lip. A thing that could not climb it quickly could still climb it. Cael saw one of them go wide of her, up to the left, where the plates were bigger and lay still. He saw another, which she had sent down to the road, come in under Brom's stone on the low side, and shut its jaws on the nearest thing that was quiet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:207:She sat with the flat across her knees and her two gloved hands laid on it. Cael had seen her so on the hundred and thirty-third night, with a strip of brass and a calfskin, going past the edge of what she had. She did not look tired. She looked like a person who has added a long column and is afraid to read the foot of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:9:"You burn what you want, and you cut as much again before you go, from the hag behind," said Oryn. "It's been the rule here since before the stone was stood up. A fire with a wall round it and a roof on it isn't on a road. I've slept here on thirty rounds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:209:Seln made no move toward the third, and nobody looked for him to. He went thirty yards ahead of any column with his hands empty, since a crew does not load the one person in it whose work is to see what is coming; and he had a case on his chest already that he had never yet set down. The third box stood on the frozen turf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:277:He had a good deal to carry besides the box, and he went over it, a stride at a time, as he would have gone over a count. There was a job, done and paid, with a hundred and thirty-eight marks to show for it. There was a wall that he had stood behind for the space of one count, and an eleventh thing that had not come of it. There was a mark in a small ruled square, which was on four sheets and an iron stake and which nobody could read. He had a name, short and strange, for a man who sat under a town. He had two sentences in a book, in his own hand, about a flight of steps and a paved place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:167:It was a road again as soon as the landing was out of hearing, and the hold came down on all of them as it had on every road in that country. The sigils stayed in their pockets. Cael's ten lay shut; the read asked at the first bend, civilly, what the wet ruts weighed, and was told that it need not know. Seln went ahead by thirty yards with his hands a little away from his sides. Lira came next, with the bow on her back. Brom had the mule's cheek-strap, with Karis at the off side of the load and Oryn at the near, and Cael walked behind the mule, as he always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:265:*Deployment since the Line: thirty.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:5:It was Seln who took it off. He had gone down the western side of the rise thirty yards ahead of the boxes, as he went ahead of everything; and at the foot, where the ruts ran in between two banks of thorn, he stopped and let the file come up to him. He had not done that on any road since the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:231:Four signs stood at the head of the dead man's page, and after them the number thirty-two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:301:"Stand here. Where I stand. No; here." He had taken a pace to his right, and he pointed at the rock where his own boots had been. "I've given you bones for a week, from outside, with pebbles. That's all I've ever given anybody. There's another way that I see it, and I've not found the words for it in thirty years, since nobody wanted them." He rubbed his eyes with the heel of his hand. "You want his joins. I can't hand them to you. I can only show you where I'm standing when I see them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:83:She drew that one out. It was from the fourth row, and it had the Stair's signs at its head, and the number thirty-two. At its foot, in the ruled space, there were four words. They were not in any of the three clerks' hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:265:*Two hundred and eighty-third day. Treswick, the district under the first wall. A hundred and thirty-six. Saw the man, from a wood-yard, at five strides. He looked at one sheet for as long as it takes to swallow, and he did not read it as anything of mine has ever been read: he asked it nothing, and he started at the bottom. He wouldn't hear what she thinks they are, and he wouldn't have a name, and he never looked at the yard. I'd wanted that, and I find I'd wanted the other as well. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:269:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:31:He had gone to it without looking at his feet, at the flat of a hand thirty yards ahead of him. He stood in the wet bents at the road's edge with the stick's foot on turf, and he was not upright; he had let his shoulders come forward and his head hang a little, like a man who has stopped to get his breath on a hill. It was not well done. Anybody who knew him would have seen the effort in it. But there was nothing in that mist that knew him, and nothing in his two wrapped hands, which lay on the stick's head as quietly as they had lain on a trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:77:"I've had no salt up since the first frost. I've had no wheel up of any kind. They're stood on your landing, I don't doubt, thirty of them, saying that they'll come when it's fit." The crook pointed down over the wall, to where the spur fell away. "That's my road. It goes through the rock there by a cut that my grandfather's people made, a hundred strides and a cart's width, and it's the only way a wheel can come to this gate. Since the frost it's had three carts off me. And a carter, and two mules."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:111:Cael lay on his stomach there with Lira on his left and Seln on his right, and looked down thirty feet of scree into the cut. He had learned in a library, from a list of other people's bouts, that a place is fought in before it is stood in, and he had never had a better seat to learn one from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:29:"You're the healer," she said. "I'd have sent a boy up the road for you this morning, if I'd known there was a road with you on it. It's my son's wife. She's been at it since first light, and it's not coming. I've caught every child in this hold for thirty years, and I can feel a shoulder where there ought to be a head."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:13:The pencil was not light tonight. A man had waited ten years with a board, and thirty with a lamp in one hand, and he was going to build upon whatever she said next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:263:"A hundred and thirty-four," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:143:"A hundred and thirty," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:217:*Treswick; the loft over the stable; one in the stall. The two hundred and ninety-second day. Eight days inside. A hundred and thirty: four to a dinner, on Brom's ruling, and worth it. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:315:They were all there by the first grey: the whole hold, thirty or more, standing off beyond the kerb with their breath smoking, and not one of them speaking above a murmur. Oryn sat on the bench by the gate. It was forty yards. She had her hands in her sleeves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:331:It was a small movement, a little to the left, as a person turns toward a voice. Her eyes went with it. They went to a place in the air that was just beyond the corner of her sight, and when they came there the place had moved, and they followed it. Oryn watched a girl of fourteen look at something a hand's breadth from her own cheek that not one of thirty people could see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:243:"That's thirty years of one man," he said, of the cushion. "It's the shape of him. I'd not fit it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:287:*Two hundred and eighty-fifth day. Treswick; the loft. A hundred and thirty-six. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two. Lamp, from about the third hour. He says left and I go left.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:291:*Karis has been looking for this room her whole life. Cleon's been sitting in it for thirty years waiting for her. Vastin tried to open a door with his rank and nearly closed all of them — an Archmarshal's worth nothing here, which he's taking about as well as I took [SHATTERED]. Down here the stone's older than the Compact, so the Compact doesn't reach. First place inside walls I've ever stood where nobody can file me, and it's a hole under a city, and it's because everything down here is older than the machine that files. Karis says give her a week. I've got a box to carry and a promise to keep about a station upstairs. I can wait.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:273:"You've been reading the strata. Old, older, oldest — I know, I read your face, you've dated these past anything the Compact admits." His chin went toward the three tables, and his eyes did not leave her. "Here's what the strata can't tell you. These aren't the ruins of something. This is the *documentation* of something. Somebody wrote these down so the thing could be *built the same way twice*." The two fingers found the brown tape again, behind him, without his looking for it. "In thirty years I never had the stone to match the text. You walked in with three floors." He let the hand fall from the shelf. "I don't think you're going to like what you've found, Karis. I've had a piece of it for a decade and I've never once slept well since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:47:Three nights ago, in a stair, six people had come to see what that boy was; and he had stood upon a step with his stick, on the eleventh day of thirty, and put a wall in front of him. He had not known then what stood behind it. He had said so, on a pallet, when he could not hold a bowl. He knew a little more this afternoon. He found that he would do it again, knowing; and that in a day which had taken the floor out from under forty years, it was the single act of his that he could look at without wishing to revise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:163:He saw it from the gallery's mouth, with the carried lamp still thirty strides ahead of him: a long dark thing stood on its end against the table's edge, beside the Hall's fourth sheet, with a fold of sacking on the floor at its foot. It was a plank. It was of oak, as black as the shelves, as long as a man's arm and two hands broad; and as the light came up he saw that one face of it had been cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:311:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:15:*Two hundred and ninety-first day. Treswick; the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty-four. By the carts' street, both ways, on wheels: a weight chalked at the bottom gate to the name on the card, and to nobody else. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:59:"Then it is all." Seln put his hands into his sleeves. "I should have said yes, on that stair. I should have been a good deal longer about it than a big man with a box. He has kept a hundred and ten strides of paper for thirty years, and has not put a ticket on a bundle; I have considered that fact for five days on my own account." He was silent a moment. "It is very nearly the only thing in this town that I trust."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:129:"Count thirty," said Seln. "Then follow me, and not him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:191:Ten strides at a burst; thirty bursts; and a crack at the end of each like a carter's whip, in a town asleep. He told it no without breaking step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:379:*S.: they have time. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:161:"That's the top." She pointed with a bare heel, without troubling to get up. "Straight up. Through the blue, and through the buff, and then you're in the square. You go across it. It's the door that faces the steps." She spat, neatly. "I've carried paper to it for thirty years. You'll not get that far in those boots without a card."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:*Two hundred and ninety-seventh day. Lowmarch; the hearth's end, and one on the keeper's settle. Eleven miles, and the ferry. The rope is paid: sixty yards of four, and he would take no fare. Pike has twelve lines, and a thirteenth with nothing in its last two columns. He has put a day against the season. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: eighteen of thirty.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:223:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:197:It had cost her to say it. A person who has read every body that was ever laid before her, for thirty rounds of a country, had stood in front of a boy and a girl and told them the size of her own tool, and had only then sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:287:*Ruling: hold. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:323:"Of thirty," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:17:Brom set the kit on the table. Its case lay there in tarred canvas, with the strap coiled on the lid; inside were a cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, and a mallet. The stake had never been driven. Beside it Karis put a dead man's ledger, shut. The three boxes stood against the warm wall, and each of them had one leaf in it that was the same as its fellows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:101:She did not turn her head at the notch. She had left a good many gates in thirty rounds, and had found out in the first of them that a healer who looks back has only doubled what she is carrying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:131:It was a high bare way between two hills, with nothing on it but frost and a few sheep that belonged to nobody, and she slept each night with her back against the jenny's belly and her feet in her pannier. On the fourth afternoon the ground began to fall. There was a valley under her with a pale road in it, going east and west, and she knew that road; she had gone round a ring of stones by it, thirty times, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:191:She did not keep a book. She had said so to the pale one across a fire: a healer who needs a page to know who is owed has stopped looking at them. She had never in thirty rounds set down a word about any person whom she had had her hands in. But those five were two days south, with a saddle between that would be shut by morning, and she could not look at them. There was a girl down there with an arm in a knot of hers, who had been told *first snow*; and by the feel of that sky she would be looking at it out of a window tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:219:The carter came over the saddle on the hundred and thirty-first morning, with a hand's depth of snow on the road and his eyebrows white, and he had a strip of bark for Pike and another that was not for Pike at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:243:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two. Myself, twice: not counted, as before.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:17:"The first was a question, and I said so at a stake. It sent a crew to fetch what was not there, in order to learn what became of the crew. A hundred marks is a fair price for a question. It is high, since nobody had ever taken such a paper; but a carter could believe in it." His finger moved to the bracket. "Nobody who has kept a book believes in this. It is two days out and two home, for a case that one person can carry on a strap. There is not a hold on that sheet that would pay thirty for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:219:"No." Pike blew on the ink. "Anybody can ask. I've said yes to one man in thirty years, and he's sitting behind you. This one I'm putting on myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:227:It was not there, and she had known that it would not be. She had walked that track thirty times with a small brown head at her right shoulder, and her hand had thirty rounds of habit in it; it went out at every turn of the path, and closed on air, and came back. She let it. A person who has set a great many bones does not expect a habit to knit in a morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:241:The track went up through the hold's grazing, past a stone hut, toward a low place in the hill. The goats lifted their heads to see her go by with nothing on a rope. She had a pannier on her own back, with the strap across her chest, and it rode where no pack had ridden since her first year: tape, flask, shears, linen, a cup, a needle, and a loaf. She had the spare rope over her shoulder. It was thirty feet of good three-strand, and she had laid it up herself; and she had carried it for eleven rounds without once needing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:255:She would send word up the road by the first carter going that way: how late she was, and why, and where to send for her if the breathing changed. It was all that could be done from here, and she had done it a dozen times in thirty rounds, and it had never once felt like enough. She had learned, a long while ago, to tell the things that could be done from the things that could only be carried, and to put the second sort where they did not get into her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:199:"Teague says you need a column I don't have. I've kept this ledger thirty years. First time it's been wrong about a crew by being too small."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:45:The carts lay along the river side with their shafts down and a stone behind each wheel. The tents were farther in, in no order he could find, of hide and tarred canvas and old sail, and they were not the same tents from one hour to the next. While Brom was getting the gelding out of the shafts a crew of four struck one within thirty yards of him, rolled it, and walked out by the north road with it on a pole between two of them. Before the flattened grass had lifted, a woman with a handcart had pitched on the same place. He did not see anybody ask anybody's leave. He had counted seventeen tents from the ferry, out of habit, and there were twenty when he counted again; and by evening only nine of the first seventeen stood where they had stood at noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:151:"That's all a hirer wants to hear from me," he said. "Did they come back, and did his goods come with them. I've been asked it every day for thirty years and I've not yet been asked a second question."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:161:"Copper, Force, thirty years on the roads," he said. "Pike. Now you know less than you did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:49:Cael stood at the end of the sound rock with the others and saw where a girl had been running three days ago. The shelf stopped as if it had been bitten. Beyond the break there was only the wall of the gorge, and along that wall a scar where the fallen stone had been rooted: a strip of raw pale rock four feet wide, tipped outward like the sill of a window, and heaped with broken plates. It ran on for thirty yards or so, and then the ledge began again, grey and level under its roof, as though nothing had happened. Under the scar there was a great deal of air, and he looked at that once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:73:Brom had the third box on its end at the brink. He knelt in front of it and reached back over both shoulders, and stopped, since the healer was regarding his wrist from the far side of thirty-one paces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:91:Anchor asked, as he had expected. Here was a rim thirty yards long with a broken boy on it. *There? There?* Each stone under his boot was a place where a point might be fixed and told to stay, and at each one he said no, and moved his foot to the next mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:103:At the thirty-first pace there was a hand in his coat, and it was Brom's, the right one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:111:He counted them, as he had not been able to before, and there were twenty-five. That was five fewer than thirty, and it was not the same thing as saying that five were dead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:215:Karis had been studying the healer across the flames for some time, with her notebook on her knee. She could hold a pencil again, for as long as it took to write a line, and she had spent her line that evening on the foot of the long leaf. Cael had seen it: *Sixty-three. The ledge. Gone, for thirty-one paces. Cart: no.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:227:"Seven. I've been about them thirty-odd times, and I stopped counting rounds a while back, for what I count is days late." She turned the heel to the fire. "I've been late eleven times, and this is twelve."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:335:"It isn't there any longer," said Cael. "It went on the morning we came to it. There's a lip of rock thirty-one paces long that a mule can be led over, by somebody who knows how, and there's no road." He put his own finger on the margin, where Karis had written small. "And that's the herd. They're shale-backs, on the face over the bay. If anybody uses a Path beneath them, they run, and the hill comes with them, and that's how your cart went into the gorge. She's written that it's derived."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:355:"Earned, a hundred and thirty-two," she said. "In hand, a hundred and sixteen, and six owed to a smith."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:217:A second water came into the river at that place from the north. It was no wider than a cart, and brown, and it ran out of a fold in the hills between banks of alder, and beside it there went a track. Wheels had made it, a long time ago. The ruts had grown over with a short wiry turf, and here and there a sapling as thick as a wrist stood up in the middle of the way. Seln, who was thirty paces ahead, bent each one down with his boot as he passed, and the axle went over it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:3:There were thirty of them, and he counted twice to be sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:7:The head was small for such a body, and carried at the height of a man's knee. It ended in something like the beak of a tortoise, and with that the animal scraped the rock. He could hear it, now that he knew to listen: a dry patient rasping, thirty times over, like a yard full of masons dressing stone a long way off. Under that there was another sound, whenever one of them shifted its weight, for the plates moved on each other with a low clatter, as a slate roof settles when the sun goes off it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:17:There was none. No head was turned toward any other. Each of the thirty faced the rock in front of its own beak, and the gaps between them were so nearly equal that he could have laid Karis's cord in any one of them and found the same knot in the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:63:They gave it half an hour by Hesk's clock, with the fire built up to the size of a hat, and thirty beaks rasped on in the dark overhead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:81:Cael sat on her left with the notebook, and Lira lay on the rock at her right, on her stomach again. Brom stayed under the roof with the cart, and Seln had gone on westward before any of them woke. The sun had not yet reached into the gorge, and thirty heaps of grey stone lay along the upper shelf with frost on their backs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:91:The second thing took her until the sun was on the shelf. High on the face, a little west of the leading animal, a vein of white stone ran down the slope as broad as a door, and at the first hour by the clock the leader's beak had been a cart's length short of it. At the second hour the white vein was behind the leader's tail, and the whole line, thirty animals and all their gaps, lay forty yards farther along the mountain than it had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:129:It did so suddenly, and nothing on that shelf had moved so quickly in two days of his watching. Its off foreleg came up off the shelf and went down on the slope above, and the hind leg followed, and then both again, three steps, with a clatter of horn that reached him a moment later. Beside it the next did the same, and the next. He swept his eyes east along the shelf, and there was not one grey back in the whole line that was where it had been; all thirty had gone up the hill by the length of their own bodies, the calves scrambling inside them, and stood now on the open slope with their heads lifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:157:"There wasn't. I've got good eyes, and I've watched a starting line since I was small. I know what it looks like when one goes and the rest follow, because you can see it run along the row, and this didn't run along." Lira sat up. "It was like one animal thirty carts long."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:163:Up on the slope the thirty stood a little longer with their heads high. Then one beak went down to the rock, and another. They did not go back to the shelf they had left; they had begun to scrape where they stood, a body's length above it, and already the gaps between them were closing and opening, settling toward a length and a half.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:179:"Here, something reached thirty together. It wasn't sight, for there was a rib of the hill between and their heads were down. It wasn't sound, for the far end moved before the sound could have got to it, and a rock made a greater noise at noon and shifted two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:223:At the ford he had found a seam. He had sat on a gravel bar with four hurt people and gone over what he had counted, and it had yielded a first mover to give the pack, and a place to put a knife behind it. There was no seam in this. Nothing on that hill went first or could be led, and there was no cut dog; if those thirty ever ran, there would be no opponent to read, only a slope, and the single question of where a person ought to be standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:273:The fire settled. Overhead, thirty beaks went on scraping at the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:267:"Of thirty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:277:*Two hundred and ninety-third day. Treswick; the yard, and then the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty. A leaf read once, below, by Karis. A copy made. Read at a fire by the three who had not heard it. The reader came up, and ate half a bowl. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:75:She had asked for his joins, and he was giving them to her; he could not have done it with a pebble five days ago. He had needed to say aloud, to somebody who stood in his boots, what he had stood in them and seen for thirty years; and it seemed that the saying had loosened the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:91:A man had shown a stranger the inside of his trade, for the first time in thirty years, because she had asked him honestly for something that he could not hand her; and the boy who held his lamp had come away with a piece of it. He had not reached. He never did reach. But he would kneel at that elbow tomorrow, and the day after, with the man's own way of looking folded behind his eyes; and each time that the finger went along a row, he would know where it was going before it went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:179:"Seln told a clerk the truth in a yard," said Brom. "It cost him his face. He said that it was the only way he'd found that stayed right afterward." He looked at his hands. "The fellow's not a counter. He's kept all that for thirty years with never a ticket on a shelf. Tell him what's his. I'd not tell him ours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:15:"I'd not doubt it. He paid my woman a copper to be told where the straw was." The crook went out toward the moor. "Those are mine. I've not had a ewe on this top since the first frost, and I'd three hundred lambed in the yard for want of it. I've had thirty-one carts up that cut in a fortnight, where I told you thirty. The salting-shed's full to the door."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:195:*Two hundred and ninety-sixth day. The halting-ground in the fold; thirteen miles. The cut walked east. Water in it, and no glass, and sheep on the top. Vastin walked it: seventeen of thirty. He stood on the leg once, with the stick off the ground, where the line had been. The factor offered twenty at each first frost. Brom said no. A hundred and twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:199:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-51.md:173:"A fence that failed at dusk, at its knot, which was set on an iron bar that stands upon the very edge of the place; and directly afterward a boy, who stood up out of the old road beside that bar and declared. Nobody on that rim saw anything between the stair and the bar. I asked Lira what she saw from the thirty-fourth step, and she says that she saw a stair. They will write that he crept up a rut in bad light while they were watching a hole, and that a knot tied a hand's breadth from such an edge is a knot which nobody should have trusted. A man who lays fences for his living would far sooner put that on paper than the other thing, and he has no cause to think of the other thing at all. It holds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:151:"I've a knife for cheese." He turned the bill over. "So have you. I've looked at all five of us. There's the one long knife, and she chose it for how it sat on two fingers. Karis has a thing with a horn handle that she sharpens pencils on." He lowered his voice, though the fifth of them was thirty paces ahead on the verge. "And he's got one I've never seen, and I'd bet the forty that it's the best of the lot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:215:Cael paced it, at a yard to the stride, and she wrote what he called. The near channel was twenty yards from the beach to the gravel, and the far one was twenty. The bar was eight yards across where the road went over it. From its blunt head, a little above the ledge, to the point of its tail, it was thirty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:263:Cael watched her come down it and thought of a painted floor at Norhold. She had been quicker there, with the burst, and she had never looked like this, for there she had been running at a mark. Here she ran thirty yards of loose stones for no one, and stopped because she had decided to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:67:"Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and eight, less six." The hinge creaked. "A hundred and two. It is the first that I have paid out. And been glad to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:145:Nobody answered him. Cael had heard those words given, on a frozen road, by a square woman with her sleeves pushed up: that a man who stood a wall on that leg inside the month would keep the stick for the rest of his days. It was the eleventh day of thirty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:283:*Two hundred and ninetieth day; Treswick; the loft; a hundred and thirty-four. The cut, at dusk: six, hooded, two from the yard and four from the lane, with staves, one bow and one dark lantern, and no Path opened by any of them. Nobody dead, none taken, no faces. Karis: the left palm, on a step's edge; seven stitches, Brom's; no healer, since a healer here wants a name. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder, on a wall, and the arm did not take it. V.: a wall, on the eleventh day of thirty; the knee has gone again, and he cannot hold a bowl.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:289:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:271:*Entered: the not-asking.* She had written that thirty-six days ago at a fence, and had kept to it since, and it had not grown easier with practice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:25:The mule drew the thing away to the south, outside the line, a hundred yards and more downwind, and left a flattened track through the grass like the lane on the far side. Cael watched it go. He had held one of those by the head for the length of thirty counts, and thrown another, and been struck by a third in the dark; and in daylight it was a long grey animal with frost on its hide, being pulled over a field by its feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:139:The eighth was the longest, and she did nothing in it at all. She let go of the mule and walked three strides into quiet ground and stood there, square, with her arms hanging and her face toward the pale thing on the hill; and he counted a hundred before she moved. He thought that he understood it. She had told four holds' worth of carters, for thirty rounds, that she had no business in that place. She was finding out what she was when she stood in it: a woman in a brown coat, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:7:"Of thirty," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:197:"It's what it was. I stood where I've stood for thirty years, and said which way a thing leaned. You found out what it said." The red eyes were on the roll of card in her hand. "A finding's yours. You'll have to carry it up into the light and be believed, and I've told you what I think of that. Work's a thing that a man can go back down to in the morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:245:"There's carts on that road," said Lira. "We met thirty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:257:*Two hundred and ninety-fourth day. Treswick; the loft, and one in the stall; the last night of both. A hundred and thirty. The back of the leaf: a list, nine lines, a thing and its number to each. Two copies, hers and the reader's, and his goes with her. The fourth line is the mark's; of its last six signs, three are sure. Fifty-two sheets counted into three boxes under two hands, K. and V. The clerk wrote* withdrawn*. Vastin: fifteen of thirty, and the stair alone. Karis wrote with the sewn hand. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:37:She drew the iron out. It was a stake as long as her forearm, numbered thirty-two, with its point still bright, since nobody had ever driven it; and she laid its head under the window, beside the first page of a dead man's book and the corner of an office's sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:91:"Thank you. That is exactly as much as I wanted." Her finger had gone to the hide, and it touched each ring in turn, going east. "The Fallow's newest sheet stands in its sixty-first year, and the Stair's in its ninety-fourth; the hall's is a hundred and thirty-eight. And the place behind the iron—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:He took out Hesk's book, and the dividers slid from between its leaves into his palm, shut, with the cork upon their points. He had carried them for six days, under a ruling of his own that was a hundred and thirty days old: he did not put his hand to a thing that measured, since a cord had run short in it and two edges had disagreed. An old man in Denvash had sent them to step off a line with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:159:Cael looked along the skin, from the left hand to the right. There were four rings, and a point that was not yet drawn, and a number against each: sixty-one, and ninety-four, and a hundred and thirty-eight, and two hundred and six. They rose as they went. Beyond the last of them, in a strip of shadow, stood something that the office had stopped counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:223:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day. The fork above Millrace; the flat rock. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: nineteen of thirty. He rides, on the field's ruling. Karis opened the dividers. I lent them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:229:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:285:A boy brought the slip in at first light on the thirty-third day, on a mule that had been made to trot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:295:It was a dry stream cut deep through red earth, twelve feet down and thirty across at the bottom, with banks too steep to stand on. The road went into it by a ramp dug out of the eastern side and climbed out by another on the west. A trickle ran in the bed, no wider than a hand. Karis looked at that trickle before she looked at anything else, and went down and drove a peeled stick into the gravel at its edge, and cut a notch in the stick at the waterline with her knife.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:359:"I said no at the first stride," she said. "And the second. I lost count somewhere past thirty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:61:The bolt lay where it had stopped, on the thirty-first step, with its three grey feathers turned to the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:103:The healer had stopped at her stride and a half from the nearest wall. She had been round eleven stones on the Fallow thirty times and had never gone near enough to see that they were cut, and she had sat with her back to this for a whole day in the dark. She held up her two palms to it, slowly, as she held them to a patient whom she had not yet touched; and then she let them fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:169:It took as long as it takes to count thirty slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:179:It was Seln who put it on. He had been thirty yards ahead of the cobs all the morning. Where the last laid hedge gave out, and the ruts ran up onto open ground, he stood in the road with his right hand flat at his side, palm down, until the whole file had seen it. Nobody needed the words. The lean man spat out his straw. The girl who had talked for three miles about a broker's face shut her mouth on the rest, and every sigil on that road went into a pocket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:233:That was a thing to be entered too. Eight had laired round a sink on this moor, and five of them had been dragged out of a cut by a hold's people, and three had gone through rotten ice. It seemed that nothing had yet come to learn the road in their place. A wheel could go over that ground now from a river to a town. Five people had seen to that with a rock and a knife, for twenty marks; and an old man had done the rest of it with a knee. There had been thirty of them at the outer gate that morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:291:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:7:So she had the barn floor swept first. It was a threshing floor of beaten clay, twenty feet by thirty, and Brom went over it twice with a besom while the hold's hens stood by and resented him. She had the sack-menders' trestle carried to the north wall, under the shutter, for the chips and the brass and her three boxes. Then she had a keg set in the middle of the swept clay, and sat down on it, and held out her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:125:"I've walked round those stones thirty times. I never knew that they'd anything on them. I never went near enough. I was told that I'd no business there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:5:Four were left alive, and he could have put a finger on each. There was the pair from the near beach, wading away from Brom's stone, and the hound that had torn his coat, at the near edge of the gravel. And there was the old one with the torn ear, thirty yards off at the tail, which had not stirred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:255:He came slowly, flat-footed on the green rock, with a bow held up out of the wet. He had been told not to come down, and had told them so himself. He stopped at Brom's stone and stood looking into the deep water under the lip. Then he came up onto the gravel and went about it with the tip of his bow, touching each one as a woman touches loaves on a board: the first, at the far landing; Lira's, half in the river; the one that lay beside the boulder. He walked the thirty yards down to the tail by himself, and walked back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:265:"I've stood over that valley thirty years," he said, in his dry unused voice. "I know what it sounds like when they take a cart. You can hear it from the fence, with the wind right." He turned his eyes on Lira. "I stood on that bank through the whole of this, and none of you shouted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:413:He could have stood behind that white muzzle himself. He owned the means, and at dusk Shadow had been all but lifting his feet for him; nobody on that gravel would have had to go down thirty yards in the open and land locked, and there would be no holes in his leg tonight. He had chosen the loud thing, in front of a witness, and taken the bite, and it was the first time since he left the hill that the seal had sent him a bill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:3:The first narrowing came at the thirty-third hundred, and Karis gave it a number of its own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:43:He looked up the slope at thirty grey heaps, and thought of a page in his own book that had her three clauses on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:135:He had lived for five days on a shelf that he could have thrown a stone across. Now the wall on his left fell back in a long curve and came round again two hundred yards on, and between the wall and the drop there lay a bay forty yards deep, floored from side to side with fine loose shale that had come down the face and spread. The slope above it was the same roof-pitch of plates, but lower; the upper shelf ran across it not three hundred yards up, with thirty grey heaps along it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:75:Pike nodded slowly. He opened the book at the leaf with five names, and set the flat stone on its upper corner, and turned his shoulder to the meadow. There were perhaps thirty people within hearing, and Cael saw the hide-woman among them, with her knife on her thigh, and under the ash an old carter who lifted his chin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:109:He turned that over, since he had expected to. On the bank at Oxhollow he had written that he kept waiting to mind, and he had gone on waiting for six days of road. The line said that they had lost a cart, and it said so to thirty strangers, twice. And he stood under an eave with five bands of linen round his chest and felt only that a thing had been counted, by a person who counted everything alike; it was the seventh line under their names, and it was worth sixty-four, and both of those were so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:113:"I want one thing straight, for my own page," she said. "The seventy is in here. I counted it at a ford, and it hasn't altered on the road. Earned stands at a hundred and thirty-two. What you have written under that line is another figure, and I don't believe that it is money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:155:He did not need to look, for he knew whose it was, and he knew what was folded in the middle box, forty yards off, with two lines ruled under two of its words. Karis had heard those two words once before, dropped by a woman with a sack of barley on her hip, and she had asked nobody what they meant for thirty-two days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:205:"I said it. I'm bound to say it; twice, the day it came, to the whole grass, with the price." He turned the cream sheet a little with one finger. "The broker's clerk brought it up from the landing the morning after the last sack, sealed as you see it, with a hundred marks in a bag, counted. I've had that seal on bales for thirty years. I never had it before on a slip that sends a crew two days past Thornwater."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:211:He said that last quite mildly, and Cael understood that he had been told something about the man. Pike wrote down whatever was put in front of him and read it to anyone; but he had found, in thirty years, one paper that he would not hang up between two fences and a wheel, and he had kept it where a person must go looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:259:He had not meant to say it aloud. It had been going round in him since the plank, a thing that he could not sort. A man had walked a road for thirty years, and each time that he came to a certain place he had gone round, and each time he had made a mark in his head. He had never once put his foot over, and he knew the number of his own refusals to the unit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:7:Lira sat on the thirty-fourth step, six above the floor, with the bow along her knee, and Seln stood below her in the doorway at the stair's foot. Brom was on the ground against the wall to the north of that doorway, with three coats on him, and the healer sat on her heels at his left hand. Karis had put her back against the wall opposite; and in the middle of the floor, where nobody went, a case lay in the dark with its cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:43:"It's on the thirty-first step. I can see the feathers. That's three above my knee."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:49:"He has the hole. He has not the stair. That bolt came in at the top and spent itself on thirty steps of stone; it would not have gone through a coat when it reached her." Seln had not raised his voice. "He knows that as well as I do. He wished us to know that he can reach the door. It is a kind of knock."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:161:"Then that's what I can't do. I can't shout. If one of them sees a thing work on this side of that stone, there's a boat." He went through them aloud, since four of the people in that room knew them as well as he did. "The Wind cracks. The heavy one would lay the grass flat for thirty feet. Fire's fire; the Pressure can be seen gathering; the edge rings. That's five that shout. The read and the Compression are no use past the length of my arm, and the tenth does nothing at all in a fight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:213:He went up thirty steps on his own feet, like anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:231:It took the whole of his attention. He was a boy walking across thirty yards of bare stone in the last of the light, with six people looking at that stone; and he could not raise his eyes to them. He had them in his head only: two on the brow in front of him, two away on his left, one on his right, and one behind, where the west was still green. He walked with his eyes in the ground, and stepped over four bolts, and went round a fifth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:81:She tipped what was left of the chips into her lap, thirty of them or more, and sorted them by eye into three heaps on the floor. There were the honey-coloured stones, such as the walls of Ostrand were built of. There were the dark close ones, like a district hall's, and a pale kind with a sparkle in the break. She chose the freshest-broken from each heap and held the three against the grey together, in the slanting light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:21:He knew why. He was the cut; he had told a girl so in a loft, in so many words. The cut is the man placed where the subject will run, and he is never where the blow falls. At a ford he had lain in a pool for an hour and a quarter while four people were bled on a bar of gravel thirty yards from his face. And tonight, while a hound took a girl's arm on a white floor, he had been two hundred yards away with his fingers in the lashings of a mule's pack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:243:It was cold in the shaft, and it grew colder with every turn of the drum. The stones went by an arm's length from his nose, course under course, each with its edge; and Anchor asked about every one of them. *There? There?* A well was nothing but rims. Blade wanted the arris of the curb far over his head; and the Iron read stirred, and offered him the weight of thirty feet of stonework round him, and he kept it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:141:Karis had her notebook open, and her pencil was not moving. Cael knew what that stillness cost her. A primary source was sitting across a fire from her, pulling on a boot, and had been round the place thirty times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:179:"Then we shall have walked to a ring of stones, and a one-armed man has been wrong for thirty years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:253:"She's nothing in her for it to take." Oryn had the rein, and she stood looking at her animal's four small hooves with an expression that Cael could not read. "I've walked her round this place thirty times, by the ruts, because I was told to. I never once thought to ask what she'd have done if I'd let her choose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:63:Cael looked down the bar to its tail. It was thirty yards of pale stones with the water going by on either side, and there was nothing on it, and nothing beyond but the river running to its bend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:175:"I ran thirty yards of gravel at that ford with nothing in my feet," said Lira. "No Wind. No burst. I caught a dog. I've never run like it. I didn't know I could, and I'm telling you it was the best thing I've ever done on my two legs, and it cost—" She stopped. Her hand had gone toward the stave, and she took it back. "It cost what it cost. And there isn't a person alive who can say whether it was good. There's a man with a bow who lifted two fingers. There's a book that says nobody died."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:199:If he told her that it was better so, he would be asking her to feel his relief in place of her own loss. And there was a second thing that he might have said, which was worse: *I saw it. I'll tell you how good it was.* He had counted fighters from the east tier of a yard for two years, and he could have given her a figure for those thirty yards that she would have believed; and then he would be the instrument, and she would have a registry again with one clerk in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:283:He saw them from thirty yards off. He looked at a sling, and a stick, and two small hands in strips of linen, and at how the fifth of them stood with his weight on one leg; and then he looked at all of it again, and did not quicken his walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:75:"Karis turned thirty animals with a page she'd not tested," said Lira presently. "And Brom carried two boxes he's not allowed to lift. And you went back for the third and stood under one of those things with your hands shut. I saw the marks where you came down." A plate slid, somewhere above. "I ran away from it, and I was the only one who did what I was supposed to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:107:He went for water first. The seep was at the bay's eastern corner, a hundred and thirty yards from the rock, and every yard of the way lay under the stripped face, where the plates were still coming down singly, with no warning but a small dry tick. Cael watched him cross it without once hurrying. He stopped whenever a slide began, and judged its line, and stood still until it had gone by, sometimes within a few feet of him; and he came back in the rain with the skin full and grey grit to his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:115:"The ledge is whole for eighty yards from the bay," said Seln. "Beyond that it has gone, for thirty-one paces; I counted them, since I had to place a foot for each. What is left there is not a shelf. It is a lip of rock, where the shelf was rooted in the wall; four feet wide at the best of it, and tilted outward, and covered with what fell on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:157:"On the cloth. I never said the cloth was all of it." He looked at the fire for a breath, and then at his book, and he went on to the book, in the size of voice that he kept for things nobody had asked him. "I've had that sheet thirty years. Forty-odd crews have been on it, one time and another, and gone off it. Some went home. Some the road had. There's not one of the forty that ever went over a line and came back to tell me so; there's none that I ever heard of who tried. I've a line in here now that says it was done." His finger lay on the leaf. "Six in and six out, and the sixth a healer. I'd a good look for the column to put that in. There isn't one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:13:"One. A bar of iron of the registry's pattern, bearing its stamp and the number thirty-one, stands on the edge of the Quieting at the Fallow Ring, a boot's width from the place where I found that edge with my feet. Two. By its rust, and by the turf grown against it, it has stood there about five years. Three. It was driven at the far end of a measure, so that whoever drove it had found the same circle that I paced, and had a cord or a chain to find it with. Four. I do not know what the number counts, or what was written down afterward, or where it is kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:33:"Thirty-one," said Lira. She had been turning it over. "If there's a thirty-one, there's a thirty. And like as not a thirty-two."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:37:"I'm only saying that it's a number. You don't stamp one bar thirty-one and stop."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:179:"She's yours," said Lira. She had come up behind them. "You've had her thirty rounds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:181:"I've had her thirty rounds, and she's a mule. She'd as soon carry boxes as panniers. Boxes don't want mending at the end of the day." The healer spoke more quickly than she was used to, and looked at none of them. "There's a beast at every gate on my round. They feed mine, and one of them shoes her. The next has a jenny that's eaten her head off since the barley was in and done nothing for it. I'll have that one off them, and they'll thank me. You've nobody who'd lend you so much as a goat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:267:She was alone on the walk, and that was new. The lean man had gone down to his supper while there was still light in the west, which Cael had never known him do; and he had left a girl with one arm sitting against the southern post, and the yard bow lying spanned on the planks beside her knee. She had the whole valley in front of her, grey going to blue, with the frost coming down on a mile of pale grass and a white stone standing in it; and it came to him that in thirty years nobody but one man had sat where she was sitting at that hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:57:The woman in the cut had laid nineteen points round him while he fought her, each as fine as a fishing line, from fifteen feet, with the stillness of a person threading a needle. Whoever had laid these was forty paces off. And they were thick: every one was as thick as his wrist, and there were three where she would have used thirty. It was the same trade in a different hand, as a ship's cable and a stitch are the same trade: this hand threw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:159:He was on the floor himself then. It was as cold through his boots as the other had been, and as silent, and thirty yards of it had never seemed so far. The hole was in front of him. It was two strides square, with an edge as sharp as a box's; and on its near side a step went down, and another, and a third, each of them white with frost and sunlight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:233:She got up, with her jaw shut, and climbed six steps on her whole foot, and sat down again with the stock laid along her right knee. That was the thirty-fourth, counting from the top. Cael saw her set her boot on the step below, heel and toe together, and let her breath go out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:17:She had gone through them twice by the fire, with the cup of barley at her knee. There were thirty-three kinds on those twelve sheets. Every one of the thirty-three was among her forty-one, and there was not a stroke on any sheet that was not. She entered it so: *one wall; thirty-three of the forty-one; none strange; a sample, and no more.* It would have been very easy to write a larger sentence, and she had struck larger sentences out of better people's work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:113:"So there's a thirty-four," said Brom, from his blanket. He had been propped against the right-hand box, strapped like a parcel. "On along it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:149:"I shall enter the figure," said Karis. "And that you said, in a barn, that one does not stamp a bar thirty-one and stop."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:163:Seln went along the nearest for thirty yards, on the dry turf, and stooped twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:351:"The walls are cut on the inner side. I could see the courses through the gap, and I could not have read one stroke of them at thirty yards if I had sat on that kerb for a week. So it is the inside or it is nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:383:*Hundred and eighteenth day. The edge of the third one, at the track's end. A marsh, and the reeds stop where the sigils stop. One bar, thirty-three, old, and no second. A hall in the middle of it, and a thing under the hall's floor that a carter told the healer nobody has seen whole.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:151:He set the stock down on the rail of the walk. There was a place on that rail worn pale and hollow, where his own left hand had rested for thirty years, and the stock lay in it as an egg lies in a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:11:She stood on the kerb, where it was two strides broad, and set her pannier down between her feet. She was a long way inside the line, with no Path to count by, and she knew it. She folded her arms, and looked across thirty yards of water at a gap in a grey wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:139:Out on the kerb, thirty yards beyond the gap, a square brown shape had taken one step toward the water and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:161:There was nothing fine about how she went. She had stood on a kerb for an hour with her arms folded, thirty yards from a shoulder that she had mended, on the wrong side of a line, with nothing in her hands. She had watched that shoulder go, through a gap in a wall; and she was the nearest living person to a patient who had fallen. She stepped down into the water as she would have stepped off a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:165:She did not walk and she did not run. She skipped. She went from plate to plate with her arms out and her coat flapping, a short quick hop to each, and never the same plate twice, and never two counts in one place; a square woman in a dead man's coat, coming across thirty yards of broken floor like a child on stepping-stones. It would have been comical on any other morning of his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:311:There were thirty yards of plates between the hall and the kerb, and they were the longest that Cael had ever walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:5:Cael had not expected that to be hard, and it was the worst mile of the four he had made on that ground. There was no cart this time, and no buckle; there were five people in two ruts, with Seln thirty paces out on the left verge and Brom at the tail, and the pale grass stood chest-high on either hand with the light coming through it. The frogs creaked from one end of the valley to the other. Lira went the whole way with her right hand half lifted and her head a little on one side, listening for a hole in that noise, and her hand never rose above her belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:49:He was thirty-five or so, thin and brown, with three days of stubble on him. The arm was bound in clean linen from the tips of the fingers to the elbow, so thickly that it had no shape, and one of his legs was thrust out stiff before him. He did not shift it when they sat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:279:"A burst moves you. You'd land somewhere that none of us had picked, with every head on that river coming after you, and nobody within thirty feet." He held her eye. "You run. As fast as you've got, wherever you're needed, and you stop on your feet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:289:"It will have been remarked," said Seln, "that there is a quieter way to put a knife behind the seventh dog than by sending a person down thirty yards of gravel to meet it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:227:They were half an hour getting away, since a healer does not leave a gate as a crew leaves a camp. She went into the mill and came out with a loaf and a cheese that somebody had pressed on her. She showed the miller's wife how the hand was to be bound at night. And then she went round to the back of the cart-shed with thirty feet of three-strand rope over her shoulder, and what she brought back on the end of it made Brom put his hand over his mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:231:"They've had her standing since the barley was in," said Oryn, a little breathless. "She's eaten her head off and learned nothing. The miller says he'll thank me to take her as far as the snow." She threw her one pannier across the grey back and buckled it. "She leads as a gate swings. I've told them so on thirty rounds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:19:She said it to the arm, on a breath, and that was all the notice that she gave it. She had waited thirty rounds to learn what a line did to her hands, and had learned it at noon, and tonight she had no time to spare for having them back. She opened her eyes and looked up at five people, and spoke quickly, as she had spoken on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:243:On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:27:She was. She went out along the ledge under its roof of rock, close in, and from that distance there was nothing to show that one foot came down later than the other; she looked like a thing that the mountain had been keeping that shelf for. He made himself look at the fin. It stood ahead of them and a little to the right, pale in the half light, with the frozen shale banked along its upper side, and he judged that they were thirty yards short of its nearer end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:39:A length of the shelf simply tilted outward, thirty yards beyond the place where Lira was running, as a table-leaf drops when its bracket is drawn. Then the piece nearer to her did the same. She was on the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:51:They came up together. There was no first. One breath there were thirty heaps of frosted stone on a shelf, and the next there were thirty small beaked heads lifted at the height of a man's knee, every one of them pointed east, away from the place where the air had broken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:57:They went. He had watched them for five days and had never seen one of them hurry, and they went along that shelf now like barrels loosed down a ramp, all thirty, shoulder behind shoulder, with the calves between; and the clatter of their plates reached him across three hundred yards of slope like hail on a slate roof. They were running from her. They were running east, along a lip of rock that had dropped a slab under one frightened animal at a walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:77:"It will stop the stone," she said. "That's what it's stopped before. It won't stop them. That bank's a ramp, and there are thirty of them, and they'll come straight over the top of this and down on us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:121:He ran with it. It was thirty yards, and he had a count of ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:223:He could, after a fashion. He got his back against the stone and worked along it, a foot at a time, with his left arm clamped to his side and his left leg leaving a mark on the clean floor. The middle of the wall went by over his head, tall and pale. It took him as long as it had taken thirty animals to come down a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:293:She knew what a second one was. She had sat in a loft and heard it laid out with a charred stick. *Never two at once.* A burst would crack over that bay like the first, and thirty animals that were running from one loud thing would have another to run from, and it would be going off beside the only shelter on the mountain. She had been the loud thing once this morning without being asked. She would not be it a second time by choice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:201:"Two bars and one number," said Lira. "So it's the place that's thirty-two. It's not the bar."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:275:*Hundred and thirteenth day, night. At the edge of the second one. A stone, and two bars, both numbered thirty-two; one five cold terms old, one a cold term or two, with a mark under its number that Seln won't read. A floor with no stones on it, and a hole, and a stair. Nobody on the ruts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:283:He slept a little toward morning. When Brom woke him the stars were going out, and the frost lay so thick on the old road that it showed like a chalk line all the way down to the floor. Seln was coming back along the ruts from the north, and he shook his head at them from thirty yards away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:27:"It rode on my mule for thirty rounds in a cover, and I looked at it at noon one day, at a stone, and left it where it was. It's done more across your knee than it ever did for me." Oryn did not put out a hand. "There's one in it. See that it stays in till it's wanted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:101:By noon he could see for thirty miles. There was no road in any of it, and no smoke, and not a fence or a roof; there were hills going away blue behind hills, with the white lying in their folds, and a sky over them so large that he could not look at the whole of it at once. Four people were strung out along the turf ahead of him, small and dark. A man who knew every road out of a town was walking toward a place where there was none. A girl who had been ranked every week of her life was a mile from the nearest thing that could have measured her, with a bow on her back that she could shoot and could not bend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:13:So he walked back down the grass with two letters, and thirty people watched him do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:45:It lay as it had lain on four other crossings, dry and red, thirty yards from lip to lip, with a ramp cut down either side and the thorn stump standing on its eastern edge. The carter took his mules through at a walk and halted them on the far flat to blow, and the five sat down among the chock-stones where they had twice made a camp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:195:"The ring," she said. "I know it. I've been round it thirty times, with the mule, by the ruts." She set the bowl on the sill. "You broke on the seventy-eighth. It's twenty-one days by the rule, and I don't lift a tape on a guess, so that's the ninety-ninth. The long one wants a morning with nothing else in it. That's the hundredth. I'd meant to sit at this gate's table from tonight until then; the grain's moving, and that bench fills as fast as I empty it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:203:"The other's mine." Oryn looked at her own right hand. "Every carter on my round has told me the same thing about that place, for thirty rounds. That a healer's hands are no good in it. Not one of them's seen it. They had it from their fathers. I've let it go by me all that time, since I'd no business in there and a list to keep." Her mouth went hard. "I don't like a thing about my own hands that I've only been told."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:209:His left hand had been in a sling and a cuff, by turns, since a river on the fifty-third day. That made thirty-five days. Cael watched the two wrists, as he had watched them under a cart on the eighth narrowing, and no grey started at either. There was a hundredweight of copied paper on them and they were the colour of wrists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:9:They stood on the east, at the mouth of the den, and everything that they wanted was on the west. The floor lay between, twenty-six strides across. From its western lip the lane that the cord had trodden ran down through the grass, as straight as a rule, for two hundred and seven yards; and at the end of the lane there was a grey stone and a mule, and beyond the stone a Path was a Path again and a healer's hands were hands. That made two hundred and thirty-three yards from where his boots were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:247:He was thirty inside his time. He came over the lip with a long thing under one arm, out of its cover, and no breath to speak with. He gave the bow to Brom, and stood holding his own knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:121:Cael knew the place before anybody named it. The scrub ended, as though it had been cut with a knife, and the valley drew in between two long shoulders of hill, and from one shoulder to the other the whole floor was grass. It was the pale grass of the lower river, chest high, and with the light coming level along it every blade stood up gold on one side and grey on the other. The ruts went into it and were lost within thirty yards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:139:"I shall say where I want each of you, and I should be obliged if nobody argued until we are at the far end." His voice was hardly louder than the wind. "I go ahead, thirty paces, on the left verge. The boy and Lira walk ten paces in front of the mule, one to a rut, and you do not look at each other; you look outward. The carter keeps his beast's head. Karis is at its off shoulder, where the cart is between her and whatever is nearest. Brom is behind the tailboard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:171:He had not turned his head. He was thirty paces in front, a dark coat walking the verge, and his voice came back along the ruts as small and plain as a pebble skimmed on water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:181:It was a low room, longer than it was wide, with beams gone black and a hearth at the far end that a man could have stood in. Benches ran down both sides, with thirty or forty people on them, by crews, each crew at its own boards, and the noise dropped a little as the five came through the door and then rose again. He was looked at once, and not twice, by people who had heard a line said about him two hours ago and wanted nothing more just yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:35:*Give it one.* He had said that aloud on a gravel bar, and gone down thirty yards to do it. Whichever of them was nearest to a declaration moved first, and the rest fell in behind whatever moved; that was in his own handwriting, by Karis's dictation. He was the only thing between that floor and that stone that had one to give.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:53:"They hunt one. I've not seen one go through sixty yards of fire to reach it. I saw thirty head of horn turn off yours on a mountain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:77:At a hundred Lira's bad foot dragged, and she made no sound; he knew it only from how the healer's shoulder dropped to take her. At ninety Brom said, without turning, that there was something in the lane behind them, and Cael looked. One of the eight had come out onto the trodden grass thirty yards back, and was sitting there with its tongue out. It got up when they had gone on ten strides, and came after, and sat again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:227:He had counted his strides from the place where he dropped into the rut, and he was at a hundred and thirty when he heard it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:301:It went up out of the grass along sixty yards, thirty to the north of the stone and thirty to the south, a wall of yellow-white fire as high as a man's knee and as straight as her wrist could draw a curve that size. He felt her discharge go past him like the door of a furnace. It was the first thing that anybody but himself had declared on that hillside since noon, and after what he had carried down the slope it seemed to him a clean and ordinary thing, like bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:307:They split on it. The foremost flung left and the next flung right, and the rest went after one or the other, as thirty head of horn had gone on a mountain. They ran along the inside of that wall to its two ends. Then they were over the line, somewhere in the dark beyond the light, to the north and to the south; and the last that he knew of any of them was a rushing in the grass that went away along the line's outside in both directions, and thinned, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:131:He did not watch her go. If a one-armed man had been wrong for thirty years, the answer to it would come out of the grass on the eastern side, a quarter of a mile off, where three runs crossed a line; and somebody had to have his eyes there when the air broke. He fixed them on that place and kept them on it.

