editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md-55-"Two gates," she said. "And a view. That's all that it is. I've been trying to see what else there was since the first one." Her eyes went over the whole of it, from the parapet to the bridge, slowly, with her lips moving. "You go through a gate and the roofs get better. You go through another, and there's a tap that you needn't pay at. And when you're right at the top, what you've got is this. You can see everybody who isn't."
