editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-76-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-77-The road opened there into a small paved place, where the street from the trough came up to meet the arch. There was a cook-house built against the wall on the near hand, with smoke going up from it, and a water-butt at its corner. Cael put his shoulder against the butt and stood. Nobody looks twice at a person who is waiting beside water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-79-*There?* said Anchor, of the arch. It was a tall one, with a groove cut down each jamb where something heavy could be let fall. He told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:81:Two keepers stood in the arch, in long coats of dark blue. One had a board hung from his neck by a strap, with a sheet on it, and a pen. The other had nothing in his hands at all, and did the looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-83-He watched for a quarter of an hour, by the shadow of the butt, and it was as plain as a mill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-85-There were people whom the keepers knew. They were the greater number: a woman with a basket and a good cloak, and a pair of clerks, and an old man with a dog. They went by with a nod, and the pen did not move. They lived on the other side of that arch, and they had gone through it twice a day since they were children.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-87-There were strangers who came by right. He saw only one, a young man in travelling boots, who had the look of a courier. He took a folded paper from his coat without being asked, and the keeper with empty hands opened it and read it all, and read out a name and a district and a rank to his fellow; and the pen wrote for as long as it takes to say a line. The young man had his paper back and went up. He had been perfectly easy. He had been written in a book as a person is who has never had cause to mind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-89-And there were the slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:91:They came up the street in knots, with frames on their backs, and each knot had one card among it with a blue edge. The keeper took the card. He did not look at a single face. He looked at the figure on the card, and then he pointed with the feather of his pen at each cap as it went under the arch, and his lips moved. When the caps and the figure agreed, he made one stroke on his sheet, tore the corner off the card, and gave the card back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-93-"Three," Cael heard him say, to a load of firewood. "Three. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-94-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-95-A girl came up with a basket of eggs. She had a card. The keeper read it, and shook his head, and tapped the card with his pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-96-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-104-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-105-He had been afraid of that gate since a back room, and he saw now that he had been afraid of the wrong thing. He had pictured a machine that would catch him and grind. This one had not teeth of that kind. It sorted whatever it could see, by a colour, and what it could not sort it did not see at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-106-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-107-There was a colour for Copper, even. It was the street below, and the one behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-108-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:109:He had no tier of any sort. He stood by a water-butt, four strides from two men who read people for their living, with a box-strap's mark still red on his neck; and neither of them had so much as wondered what he was. There was no sieve in that town fine enough to hold him. He had fallen clean through the lowest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-110-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-111----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-112-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-113-He went on round, and the road began to go down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-114-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-210-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-211-A small grey man had told him at a spring what that hand was. He had not much cared to be told, and it had been true. He laid the hand on his knee again, palm down, and he looked at the room and did nothing to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-212-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-213-A keeper was eating, and the bench had made room. That was all that was in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-214-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:215:There was a paper under his hand, where it had stopped, and it was not the only one. He carried, in oilskin, three sheets under one seal, and they were what he had told a loft that he would not show; and beside them lay a receipt in a small upright hand, unsealed, which said that certain things had been read in part and understood in no part. He had read that sentence a good many times on the road, and he thought it the more exact document of the two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-216-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-217-And he carried one thing that was not paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-218-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-219-A boy had given it to him on a bank, at dusk, across a yard of turf. He had it by heart, as he had always had a finding: there was a place, which a healer had found; it had been read twice in a day; nothing had come back. He did not know what had read it. A man of his training could set out the possibilities, in their order, and on the road that morning he had begun to; and he had reached the second, and seen that it was a draft, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-220-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-316-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-317-"The square."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-318-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-319-"A porter told me, at the trough. It goes across."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-320-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:321:"It is the only way to the front door, and a load does not go in at a front door. There is a yard, and a yard has a gate, and carts must reach it by some road which is not a paved square with a station's steps on it." Seln got up off the ladder's head. "I have not found that road. I shall find it tomorrow; and tomorrow at noon somebody will stand at a cook-house counter with a sheet in her hand that a certain man cannot look away from. That is as far as I have got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-322-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-323-Karis laid her pencil down on the flat. Her hand was not quite steady.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-324-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md-325-Brom turned the lantern low. The blanket hung over the gable, with a faint paleness round its edges that did not flicker; and Cael lay back, with the book shut on his chest and his eyes on the slates a yard above him.
