editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-1-# Chapter 25 — The Slate Town
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-3-The second of the five gates was the one with the child in it, and Oryn came to it after dark on the third day, a good deal greyer than she cared to be seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:She had walked nine miles along the hill-foot since noon on legs that had wanted a bed. She had told a carter that she did not put her hands to hard work tired; and she had then spent an hour inside his wrist and set out on a road. It was not the first time that she had broken that rule. It was the first time in a long while that she had broken it before the day's second gate, with the gate still in front of her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-7-The child found her before she had the mule tied.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-9-He was three, or thereabouts, with a head of hair like a dandelion, and he had been a thing to worry over before the snow: a chest that whistled, and a mother who had walked him out to the bench twice in one round to ask whether it was the kind that passed. Oryn had said that she would hear him again before the cold. Then there had been seventeen days on a mountain, and a road shut behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-10-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-22-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-23-"You'd every need. You couldn't know which sort it was, and nor could I, from the far side of a shut road. Fear's what you had instead of me." She let the boy go, and he went straight back to the mule. "I'm sorry that it was all you had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-24-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-25-It was then that the gate-house door opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-26-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:27:That hold kept its keeper outside the wall. There was a room built against the wall's outer face, on the road side, with one window and a bed in it, since somebody must sleep on the wrong side of a bar to hear who knocks at night. An old woman stood in its doorway with her sleeves rolled and her hands held away from her apron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-29-"You're the healer," she said. "I'd have sent a boy up the road for you this morning, if I'd known there was a road with you on it. It's my son's wife. She's been at it since first light, and it's not coming. I've caught every child in this hold for thirty years, and I can feel a shoulder where there ought to be a head."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-31-Oryn stood up. The yard went grey at its edges, and came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-32-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-39-"You're spent," she said. It was one tradeswoman's finding to another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-41-"I'm spent. I gave it to a wrist this morning, and I'd do the same again, and that's no use to you at all. I told a man last night that I don't put my hands on this sort of thing tired." She dried them, a finger at a time. "A child doesn't wait on what I've told people."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:43:The room was hot, and smelled of tallow, and there was a young man in the corner who had been told to keep out of the way and could not find any. The woman on the bed had her eyes shut. She was perhaps twenty. She opened them when the pain let go of her, and looked at a strange square face in the lantern-light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-44-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-45-"I'm a healer," said Oryn. "My name's Oryn; I walk this road. Your child's lying crosswise in you, with its back to the door, and I can turn it. I do it from outside, with my hands on your belly, slowly, in between; it'll hurt you some, and a good deal less than what you're doing now." She waited until the eyes had hold of hers. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-46-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-47-"Yes. Yes. Do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-48-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-49-She laid her hands on, and counted, and went in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-50-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-58-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-65-It was a daughter. She came at first light, in a great hurry at the last, and she was very angry about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-67-Oryn heard the cry from the bench outside, where they had carried her a cup. She had not been able to lift the cup. She sat with her back against field-stone and watched the hill go from black to grey, and listened to a person who had been in the world for less than a minute telling it what she thought.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-68-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:69:Two rivers in one body; and she had never in her life had any trouble in telling which was which. Each had its own bed. She could have drawn the banks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-70-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-71-She had met one body in which ten ran together with no bank to any of them. In the middle of the ten there had been a place where nothing ran at all, and everything went round. She had called it a stone, in her own head, on a carter's wrist, and she had called it a join. Sitting here with her hands as heavy as wet sacks, she knew that it had been neither of those, and that she had only reached for the two nearest words. She did not have a word. She had a child, an hour old, that had lain turned the wrong way in the dark and could not have told anybody so; and she had a boy three days south who was walking into a town with a thing in him that he could not read. She had not read it. She had sent him in with a rule about a square, since a rule had been the only thing that she owned to give him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-72-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-120-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-121-"He will be told nothing by me. I have been practising; you may have observed it, and I am told that I do it very ill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-122-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-123-"If he asks who you are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-124-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:125:"Then I shall look at my boots, as a hand does, and somebody with more wit will answer him." The old mouth moved. "I am aware of what I am being entered as. I have signed a great many sheets with that column on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-126-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-127-"Nobody's ordered it," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-128-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-129-"No. I heard no order. I heard a count that came out one under, and I have never been able to leave such a thing alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-130-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-166-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-167-That was not a question either. It was Karis who took it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-168-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-169-"He carries the light one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-170-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:171:"He'll want to. It's a hill, from here." The feather dropped. He made a stroke on his sheet, a single one, and tore the corner off the card and gave the card back. "Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-172-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-173-Vastin went under the arch with the case on his shoulder and his eyes on the setts. It was a gate that he had ridden through on four occasions, with a keeper standing at each jamb and the board held out to him, open, so that he might see for himself how it was kept; and he went through it now as one stroke in four, on the wrong side of the pen, and Cael heard him let his breath go when the shadow of the arch was behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-175-Lira went under the arch second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-177-Cael had her back in front of him, and a box on it, and above the box the back of her head. He could not see her face. He saw what her head did. As she came level with the board that hung from the second keeper's neck, where a line stood for every stranger who had walked through that gate today on his own paper, her chin turned toward it by the width of a finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-178-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:179:There would be names on that sheet, in a clerk's hand, each with a district and a rank; and there was a pocket in her coat with such a line folded in it, sealed, which a station at Ostrand had made out to her, and which she had worked harder for than for anything else that she owned. She had only to put the box down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-181-Her chin came back. She shifted the beckets, and went on up the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-182-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-183----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-184-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-190-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-191-Nobody looked at them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-193-It was not as the outer street had not looked, where everybody was carrying and a box was only a thing in the way. People here were not carrying anything. They walked with their hands free, in good cloth, at their own pace; and their eyes went over four backs under loads as eyes go over a row of posts. A woman drew her skirt aside for Brom without breaking her talk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:195:Karis had not looked at a single slate. She walked with her face turned to the house-fronts on the upper side, where the street had been cut into the hill and the footings of the houses stood on the bluff's own rock; and twice she put out two gloved fingers, in passing, and drew them along a bed of pale stone at the height of her hip, as another person might have touched a dog.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-197-"Left foot," said Brom, to himself, on the steep of it. He was favouring two toes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:199:The street climbed harder past the cistern. It had been laid in long shallow steps there, a stride to each, with a raised kerb for the grip of hooves. Behind him the stick had begun to come down out of time. Cael did not turn round; he slowed, by a little, as one slows a line for the last on it, and heard the breath at his shoulder catch up, and shorten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md-200-
--
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-1-# Chapter 24 — Noon
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-3-Karis did not choose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:5:The day, when it came, was the two hundred and eighty-third. She had the second box unroped before there was any sun, and its lid off, and she knelt on the boards of the loft with fifty-two sheets in three heaps before her: eleven, and twenty-nine, and twelve. They were her own first copies, the ones that she had drawn on her knees under a ring of stones, and on a stair, and in a drowned hall, with a lantern held low. Cael had carried them a long way. He had not seen them out of their wrapper since a cold term.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:7:"I had meant to take him the best," she said. "I lay awake over which they were: there are three from the Stair that came out clean, where the cutting was deep, and one from the Hall that I am proud of." She squared the third heap with the side of her hand. "And then I saw what I was doing, which was choosing what a man was to think before he had looked. If I show him my four best, I have shown him four things that a careful person can draw; so I shall take him all of it, with the bad sheets in their places."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-9-"To a cook-house counter," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-11-"One, to the counter, since I am not a fool; and the rest under my arm, in their order, where he can see how thick the roll is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-12-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-63-Then Karis came in by the street door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-65-She was very small in that doorway, with the light behind her, and a roll of oilcloth held against her chest. He saw her look down the room and find the dusty coat. She did not hurry. She came along behind the clerks with her chin level, as she had once walked up to a broker's plank with a hide; and she stopped at the counter's end, in the place that nobody took.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:67:She did not speak. She undid the tape, and drew out the uppermost sheet, and laid it on the counter beside his bowl, and squared it to the counter's edge with one finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-68-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-69-The spoon went on to his mouth, and came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-70-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:71:It was the paper that he heard, Cael thought, or the breath of it settling. The grey head turned a little, and the eyes opened; and they were not as Cael had expected, since they were red at the rims, and wet, like the eyes of a man who has been all night over a small flame. They went to the sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-72-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-73-He did not read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-74-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:75:Cael had watched a great many people read. He had watched registrars do it, and magisters, and a small pale woman who went along a line as a hen goes along a row of seed; and this man's eyes did not go along anything. They went to the foot of the sheet first, and stayed there, and then they climbed, as Brom's had climbed a pier on a river bank, from the footing upward, stopping at whatever seemed to be holding weight. He might have been looking at a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-76-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-77-It took him no longer than it takes to swallow, and then he laid the spoon down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-79-"Off floors," Cleon said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:81:"Fused. Grey. No join. Three of them. I copied every mark on all three. Fifty-two sheets," Karis said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:83:"Fifty-two sheets," Cleon said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:85:The eyes had gone back to the sheet. One square pale finger came out, and did not touch it; it moved along above a row of her drawing, an inch off the paper, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:87:"Rubbed, or drawn?" His voice was rough, as a hinge is that is seldom used.
