editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md-263-Nobody had sorted him to the bottom. There was no column out here. There was a top road, and snow on it, and one healer with one pair of feet; and the snow had done exactly what a board at a bed's foot would have done, and had not needed a clerk. She could not fault those sums, either. She had only liked them better because nobody had written them down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md-264-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md-265-She turned over, and the mule shifted its feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md-266-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md-267-And there was a thing that she had not got to at all. She had walked away from it at a fork two days south, with her sleeve half pushed up, and had told a boy that it would keep three weeks. She had read a hundred bodies since she first laid hands on his. She knew a wrist with a stone in it when she met one. She had met something in him a long while back that everything went round, as that carter's current went round a bad join; and she had not been able to say then whether it was a join, or a stone, or a thing that had always lain there. She had told him to leave it be. Then she had walked north, owing him the one reading that might have told her.
