    32	
    33	She did the line. Five, then four — the old woman's knee among them, done again as she had said at this ford in the autumn she would, the ladder order repeated and, by the woman's face, refused again — then the small ones, in the register, one hand each for the small ones and the current for none of them, because none needed it and she did not have it to spend, and Cael sat on the bank with the log and did not open it, and watched a woman do the thing she owned.
    34	
    35	Then she came to the bank.
    36	
    37	She did not sit. She stood at the edge of it with the roll on the table behind her and the ford at her back, square to the six of them as she stood square to everything, and said the terms, and he had known she would, because she had said them at a fork and at a table and by post, and a woman who says a thing three times at three sizes is a woman who has decided that it needs saying until nobody can mistake it.
    38	
    39	"I'm on my route." She said it to the six of them and past them to the road, where Teague stood, because Teague was the one on the road who had not heard it said. "Seven holds north of here owe me a visit and I've walked four of them since the fork and there are three I haven't, and there's an old woman at Thornwater with a knee that held through the winter and a ladder she'll not stop climbing. That's mine. Nobody at this ford gets a share of it." A breath. "Your bearing runs east. So does the road to the last three holds, for two days, and then it doesn't. I'll walk the two days because they're on my way. I'll walk further than that because there's a man on this bank with ribs I've been owed a reading of since a marsh, and because ten people are going into two places that stop Paths at a stride, and I've seen what that costs, and I'll not have it said I was a day north when it cost it." She did not look at Teague when she said the next thing, and it was for him. "I'm not a recruit. I've never been one. I'm a stop on my own route, and the route's mine, and the day I have to turn north I'll turn north, and nobody at this ford will ask me not to, because the one who'd have the right to has already said he won't."
    40	
    41	She looked at Cael, then.
    42	
    43	"On the road, if there's road," he said. "Not before."
    44	
    45	"Good," she said.
    46	
    47	Teague, on the road, said nothing, which was the right answer, and Cael watched him hear it and file it, because the man filed things too, in his way — a healer of no Path and a healer of Tide, and both of them on their own terms, and neither of them his to move — and the tall woman moved the spear a hand's width, and that was Teague's crew hearing it, and it was done.
    48	
    49	Lira, from the bank: "Sixth chair."
    50	
    51	"Sixth chair," Oryn said. "On my terms. Which are the only kind." And then, because the terms were said and the rest was work: "Brom. Shoulder."
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	She read them in order, on the plank table, with the light going, and asked each of them first, as she asked every time — "May I?" — and each said yes in their own register: Brom with a grunt that was a word, Lira with a nod she turned into one, Karis with "Yes" and no look up from the notebook, Vastin with "You may," which was the form's word and the only one he had; and he catalogued each one, because each was a cost he had carried in the log since the autumn and a healer's hand on it was the entry that closed the row.
    56	
    57	Brom's shoulder. She put the flat hand on it, as she had wanted to at a marsh and had been told *binding, not hands* by a woman with a cloth bag, and she held it there, and came out of it, and said, "She was right. It wanted leaving alone and it was left alone and it's a shoulder. Carry with the other one another month. You will anyway." Brom said, "Aye," which was his whole speech on the subject of his shoulder for the year.
    58	
    59	Lira's arm. Two fingers on the wrist, the flat hand on the forearm, the count: "Fifteen days at the fork. It's had thirty-three. It's an arm." She looked at Lira's two hands, both at her sides and neither in her coat. "You listened when it told you the second time."
    60	
    61	"Once," Lira said.
    62	
    63	"Once is once more than I priced." That was all of it, and Lira took it as praise, because from Oryn it was.
    64	
    65	Karis's hand. The bound one, the cut across the back of the left hand from a step's edge in a cut, eight days old, bound by Brom with thread and a strip of shirt and his teeth, and Oryn unbound it and looked at it a long time in the going light and said, "Who sewed this."
    66	
    67	"Brom."
    68	
    69	"With what."
    70	
    71	"Thread. His teeth for the knot."
    72	
    73	Oryn looked at Brom, once, and said nothing, and rebound the hand with her own linen, tighter and flatter, and said to Karis, "It'll close. It'll leave a line. Don't write with it for a week — the other hand. You've got one." And Karis, who had been writing with it for eight days and would not stop for a week, said "Noted," which from Karis was not agreement, and Oryn heard that it was not and let it go, because a healer picks the orders she enforces.
    74	
    75	Vastin's knee. He came to the table with the stick and she said, "Sit," and he sat, and she put both hands on the knee through the trouser and then said, "Trouser," and he rolled it, and the knee was a knee with a binding on it that had been put on at a cut nineteen days ago by the hands that were on it now, and she read it. It took longer than the others. He watched her face, because her face did the reading's work in the register her voice did not, and it did not change, and then it did, a little, at the corner, and she took her hands off.
    76	
    77	"It held in a cut," she said.
    78	
    79	Vastin did not say anything.
    80	
    81	"You stood on it. With the stick off the ground. You've done it once, and it was in a cut, and you did it because a cut was where it went and you wanted to stand on the place." She did not ask him. She had read it. "It held. That's not the same as the month being up. The month's the month. Eleven more days on the stick and then I'll say, and if you put it down before I say I'll know, because I'll read it, and it'll tell me."
    82	
    83	"Noted," Vastin said, and she looked at him. "That's new."
    84	
    85	"It's a useful word."
    86	
    87	"It's his." She tilted her head at the bank, at Cael, and rebound the knee, and it was done, and the man with the stick went back to standing where he had been told to stand, and stood.
    88	
    89	Then she wiped her hands, and looked at the pan, and did not drink from it, and said, to the ford, "Cael. Bench. Lie still when I say."
    90	
    91	---
    92	
    93	He lay still.
    94	
    95	He had lain still for nobody, in five years, except her, and for her three times, on a sack and on a table and at a marsh's edge, and each of those had been a minute at her count, and this was not going to be a minute, and he knew it before she put her hands on him, because she had said *the long one* at a fork and had meant it, and because she had carried this reading on her route since the autumn and was going to have it whole.
    96	
    97	The bench was the lean-to's, against the back wall, under the turf, out of the ford's noise. He lay on it on his back with the log on the ground beside his hand where he could reach it and would not, and his coat open, and the straightedge and the dividers taken out of it and put on the plank table in her order, because she had said *nothing in the coat* and had meant the brass. The light was nearly gone. She did not light the lantern. She sat on the stool beside the bench and said, "May I?" because she said it every time, and he said, "Yes," and she put two fingers on his wrist, as she began every reading, and waited for the count to settle, and it settled, and then she put the flat hand on his ribs, on the left, low, where the front hand had been on a table in the autumn and the ribs had been quiet since a marsh, and went in.
    98	
    99	He felt her go. He always had; he had told her so, at a fork, and she had said *I know*. Her current came in through the flat hand as it came in through everything, steadily, without a check in it, and went down through the ten — the twelve — as water goes down through a bed of stones, finding the ways between, and he lay still and let it, and did not go with it, because that was the term and he did not shave terms.
   100	
   101	It took a long time.
   102	
   103	He did not count it. He had decided not to before she put the hand down, because a count was a thing done, and lying still was the absence of a thing done, and he could not do both. So he lay, and the ford ran outside, and the wheel turned, and the light went, and somewhere on the bank Brom said a word to Lira and Lira did not answer it, and the flat hand on his ribs did not move, and Oryn's breathing beside him went slow, and slower, and stopped being a thing he could hear, and he understood that she was as far in as she had ever been in anyone, and that she was going round.
   104	
   105	Going round. He felt her do it. Her current, which ran to things — that was Tide; a current that ran *to* whatever was hurt, and arrived, and did the work — ran down through the twelve to the middle, to the place, and did not arrive. It went round. As the twelve went round. As everything went round, and had, as far back as he could feel, and he felt her feel it, from his own side: the current bending where the bed bent, and the bed bending because of a stone that the river had gone round for so long that the going-round was the river's shape, and the stone at the middle not doing anything, because doing was not what it was.
   106	
   107	She stayed there. A long time. He felt her try — not push; she did not push; a healer who had spent her life reading current did not push a current at a thing that was not one — but *stay*, at the edge of it, with all that she was held still at the bank of a thing that had no bank, and read it as nothing, and read the everything around it, and read the nothing again, and it was the same.
   108	
   109	And while she stayed there, because he was lying still and had nothing to do with his hands or his count or his eyes, and because a man who owns twelve things and has been told to lie still with them does not stop owning them, he turned the twelfth.
   110	
   111	He did not decide to. He entered that afterward, honestly: he had not decided. It had turned as a floor leans that you have stood on for an hour; the eye going along a row whether he told it to or not. Cleon's inside. The lean. He had had it four days and had turned it on nothing since a board, because there had been nothing made to turn it on, on a road; and here, lying still under a turf roof with a healer's current going round a place in him, the twelfth turned, by itself, inward, toward the place, because the place was where everything in him was looking, and the twelfth looked where he looked.
   112	
   113	It should have found nothing. That was the card: *made things only; not a river; not a man.* He was a man. The still place was in a man. A record of a thing that was not made would read as what a heap read as, which was nothing with a shape, and he lay and waited for the nothing, and it was the only time in the reading that he was not lying still, and he knew it, and could not stop it any more than he could stop his eye going along six marks in a row.
   114	
   115	It did not read as nothing.
   116	
   117	It read as *still*. That first, and the same as Oryn's finding, and he held there, because it agreed with a healer and a thing that agrees with a healer is a thing you can enter. Still. Not flowing. Not empty. And then, under the still, as six marks on a board had been six marks and then were six marks with a direction — not a movement; a *lean* — the still place leaned.
   118	
   119	Not toward anything. He could not have said toward. It was not a row; it did not go left to right; there was no bearer and no borne. But it was not a heap. A heap sat, and this did not sit; it was *set*, as a thing is set that somebody has put where it is so that a next thing can sit on it, and the twelfth, which could tell him a thing was an instruction and could not tell him what it instructed, told him that the still place at the middle of him had the inside of a made thing, and would not tell him what it was made for, and he lay under the turf roof with a healer's hand on his ribs and did not move, and did not breathe differently, and did not let it into his face, because her fingers were on his wrist and she would feel a check in him as she felt everything, and there was no check, because he had lain still for her before with a thing found in him and this was only another one.
   120	
   121	He did not name it. He had no name that was not a guess. He had said so to her in a yard and it was on a page.
   122	
   123	But he heard Cleon, at a table with a cup: *If that fragment ever reads something for you that shouldn't be made and reads it anyway — Don't tell me. Don't tell anyone. Just remember I said it.* He had entered it at the time under the observation he could not file. He filed it now. It had been an instruction, and it had leaned, and he had not seen the lean until the thing it pointed at was under his own ribs.
   124	
   125	The eyes were paying already, in the dark, which was the one place they could pay unseen: the lean-to's open side gone to a smear, the lantern that was not lit no darker for it, and he let them pay and did not blink against it, because a blink is a thing a healer's fingers feel.
   126	
   127	Her hand came off.
   128	
   129	He felt her come up out of it — not fast; a healer does not come up out of a long reading fast — and the fingers left his wrist last, as they always did, and he heard her breathe again, and the stool creaked, and she sat a while in the near dark with her hands in her lap before she said anything, and he lay still, because she had not yet said he could stop, and did not.
   130	
   131	"Up," she said.
   132	
   133	He sat up. He picked up the log from the ground and did not open it.
   134	
   135	She said it plainly, in the register, as she said every finding, and she said it looking at him and not at the ford, which was not the register and was the other thing.
   136	
   137	"There's a place in you I can't read because it isn't *flow*. I've spent my life reading flow. Whatever that is, it's not the kind of thing my Path was built for."
   138	
   139	He said, "Noted," because it was the word, and because it was true, and because it was the same finding she had given him at a marsh with less certainty and had now given him with all of it, and a finding that has been made certain is a different entry from one that has been made.
   140	
   141	"It hasn't changed." She looked at her hands. "Not since the autumn. Not a hair. I went as far in as I've ever gone in anyone and I stayed as long as I've ever stayed, and it's a still place that everything goes around, and I can tell you every current in you to the bank, now, all twelve — twelve; you've two I didn't have in the autumn, and one of them's not for fighting and I don't know what it's for and I'll not ask — and I can't tell you one thing about the place they go around, because there's nothing there for a healer to read. Nothing. And everything, round it." She stood up. "That's my finding. It's the same finding. It's not going to be a different one, because I've read it as deep as I can read, and that's the bottom of what I am."
   142	
   143	He did not tell her.
   144	
   145	He entered that too, that night, honestly, and the reasons in their order, because a thing withheld from a healer who has just gone as deep as she can go on your behalf is a thing that wants its reasons written down. Cleon had said not to, and Cleon had been right about the stair. He had no words that were not a guess, and he had told her once that he would not guess. And she had gone to the bottom of what she was, and had come up with her own finding, whole, and it was hers, and a man does not lay a rival instrument's reading over a healer's the minute she has finished, on her own bench, in the dark, as if her finding were the lesser one because it had found nothing. It had found nothing. That was a finding. The other had found a lean. That was a finding too. Two instruments, one place, two findings that did not agree, and he did not know which was the true one or whether both were, and he was not going to decide it on a bench, and he was not going to make her carry a thing she had not found and could not read.
