editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:1:# Chapter 44 — A Wall's Count
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:3:Teague counted them off the scow, from the window on the rise, as he counted anything that came over that river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:5:There were six, and there was no mule; and he marked the mule's absence first. A crew that went west with a beast and came home without one had sold it, or eaten it, or lent it; and that crew did not sell what belonged to a healer. So she was off on her own road, with the beast under her, and they had carried three boxes on their backs for as long as she had been gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:7:They carried them poorly: the big one had his on the right shoulder, which was the bound one, and neither of his arms came up above the elbow when he shifted it. The small pale one walked with her left hand laid flat against her coat. The girl had both her arms, which was new since the thaw; she used the left as a person uses a tool that has just come back from the smith. The boy with the book was sound, and came last but one, as he always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:9:Last of all a stick came off the planks, and a stiff leg after it, and then the old man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:11:Teague looked at him longer than at the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:13:He had been home from the shoulder hold for a week. He had taken that salt up himself, behind the first carts, when he might have sent it with the bow and saved his own boots; the bow had said so, and had been right. But a cut that had eaten three carts in one cold term had been opened in an afternoon by the eleventh crew on Pike's cloth, and Teague had wished to see the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:15:There had been little to see, since it was a wet road between two banks. He had stood in it for a quarter of an hour, and then he had gone up to the gate, where a grey factor with a crook was counting sacks; and he had asked what was done there, and by whom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:17:He had been told about a rock, and a knife, and a boy who lit a thing no bigger than a barley grain and walked away from it. He had expected as much, since those five fought as he would have had his own fight, with nothing showing. Then the factor told him the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:19:Eight had come down that road at a run, and had stopped in the air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:21:He had made the factor say it twice. The old man with the stick had stepped out, and struck the ice once, and there had been a thing across the cut from one bank to the other, as high as the scree. Eight hounds broke on it. It stood for exactly as long as the man never lifted a foot; and when it went, he went down with it, like a tent with the pole drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:23:Teague had never seen an Iron Wall. He had heard of them, as a man who works the holds hears of what is kept behind the walls of towns. They belonged to tribunals, and a person did not come by one in a meadow. Whoever carried such a thing up a border road on a stick had been somebody, a long way from here, and had left off being it; and Teague was not such a fool as to ask what.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:25:He had asked something else, and it was what a captain needed to know. Could the man move?
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:27:He could not. That was the whole of it, and Teague had carried it home down a moor, turning it over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:29:The Force went out along a line and was done. He had been paid for that for the better part of his life, and it had made him first on a sheet of sailcloth: he could put his palm to a thing that six men could not shift, and it shifted. It was a thing for roads: a block came down on a ledge, or a wheel went into a ditch, or a gate would not open; and he arrived, and it was settled, and the wrist kept the account.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:31:A wall did not arrive. It stayed. It could not come to the trouble; the trouble had to be brought to it, and whoever stood behind it had to be worth the bringing. He had seen that much from a factor's telling; the boy with the book had seen it from two feet away, and had gone and stood there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:33:So a wall wanted a count. It was the first thing that such a tool asked of whoever meant to use it: how many are behind? A captain who did not know that figure before the thing went up would learn it afterward, by who was left outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:35:He had it counted before he was off the moor. There were their five, and the old man made six; there were his own three, and himself; and it made ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:37:The scow's bow touched. He watched six people go up the bank toward the lower inn, with a stick behind them, and saw that the stick was no longer a dozen strides back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:41:"It pays nothing," said the bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:43:He was on the settle, with the bad leg to the heat, running a thumb along a string that did not want it. He had said the same on a night of snow, and again when the ice went; he was a man who liked a thing said three times before he would leave it alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:45:"It pays nothing that I can show you," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:47:"Then what's it for? We've the cloth. We've salt work waiting on four holds, and a weir at the mill that wants three stakes yet. I'm not a young man, and that's a week's walk to stand at the edge of a field and watch another crew go into it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:49:"We'll not be watching."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:51:"We'll not be going in to any purpose, either. You said it yourself, at their hearth. In there I'm a man with a bad hip."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:53:Teague did not answer at once. He owed that man a true answer; the bow had walked behind him for a great many cold terms, and had never once asked to be told a comfortable thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:55:"I've been first on that sheet a long while," he said. "It says who brought the goods home. That's all it was ever for, and I've never asked more of it." He looked at the fire. "For the whole of a cold term the only thing in this country worth a man's knowing went on in a shed down that bank, behind a hurdle, with a guard on the door. I was first, and I was on the wrong side of the hurdle. I didn't care for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:57:"So it's pride."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:59:"It's that I mean to be at the front of the thing. I'll not dress it. A captain who tells his people that he's going for their sake, when he's going for his own, gets them killed for a lie." He turned his head. "You're not bound. I said my crew comes, and I said it to fifty people. I never said that every man of it must."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:The bow snorted, and let the string go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:63:"I'd like to see you find the mill without me," he said. It was his whole answer, and Teague took it as he would have taken a thumb on a slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:65:The old woman had not looked up from her board, where she was cutting linen into lengths with a small knife.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:67:"There's a valley up the north water that breaks its legs every dry spell," she said. "It's done so without me before now. I'm coming for that wrist. I'll not be counted, and I'll not be told where to stand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:69:"I'd not have counted you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:71:"See that the others don't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:73:The boy came with the cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:75:There was no frost to speak of; the ice had been gone more than four weeks, and the joint had not talked since the board came off. He brought it all the same, wrung out of the kettle, on his two palms, as he had on every night since he joined. Teague held out the arm; he had tried to tell him once that it was not wanted, and had seen the boy's face, and had let it be wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:77:"Ten," the boy said. He had been listening on the stair, as he listened to everything. "You said ten, to him. Their six, and us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:79:"I did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:81:"Is that four of us?" He had gone red. "Or is it three, and me carrying?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:83:Teague looked at him, and he was all wrists still. He had grown the width of two fingers since the snow, and had learned from a girl on a bench to wait for a pole; but he went at each thing a half-beat before he had seen the whole of it, and nobody had yet got that out of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:85:"You've two legs, and a Path, and you do what you're told a little too soon," he said. "That's a man. I can count as far as four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:87:The boy nodded, with his whole head, like a horse; and he went away to the stair very quickly, so that nobody should see what his face was doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:89:Teague laced the leather, and stood up, and went over his terms once more, as he went over a load before the ropes were drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:91:There were ten on the count, and neither healer; a healer who is counted is a healer who is sent. Whatever the thing paid was to be shared by that count, and so was whatever it cost. The field was his. When it came to steel on a road there would be one voice, and he had heard the boy admit, with his feet, in a square of snow, whose voice it ought to be. And the reading was the pale one's. He could read a slip, and a ledger, and a man's hands; he could not read a calfskin with four rings on it, and he would not stand at her shoulder pretending.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:93:There was one thing more, and it was the name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:He had seen two crews lie up together for a cold term and come out of it as one. He had seen them bury each other's dead as kin. It was a fine thing, and it was how a captain came to turn back for a man who was not his, on a ledge, with his own people strung out behind him. He had four, and he meant to walk four home. He would call the thing by the smallest word that would hold it, and he would not let anybody make it larger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:97:He put his thumbs in his belt, and went down the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:101:The long room of the lower inn was as full as Cael had ever seen it, and Pike had the bench under the cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:103:Every crew that had lain up for the cold was there, and a good many carters. The hide-woman had a stool by the hearth, and the three grey men had their board on a keg and were not throwing. It was the hour at which a one-armed man read out what the day had brought to his plank; and the room had gone quiet for it, as it had on a night of first snow, when a crew came in with white on its shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:105:Brom did not wait to be called. He went down between the benches with his box still on his shoulder, since he could not get it off without help, and laid a strip of bark on the open ledger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:107:"That's yours," he said. "To draw the tack on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:109:Pike took it up, and held it to the candle in his double fashion; a thumb had been set to its foot, in grease, and had left a mark like a crook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:111:"The shoulder hold. I had a carter in here ten days since, with salt money, who said that he'd come down that cut with his wheels in running water and never a finger lit." He dipped his pen. "I don't write on a carter's word. I write on a thumb."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:113:He wrote, and said it over, twice, to the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:115:"*Clearance; the cut below the shoulder hold. Eight. Twenty; paid.* That's twelve lines." The stone went back on the leaf. "And then you never came home. You left word with me: the cut, and whatever lay beyond. A crew's been gone eighteen days past its slip, and I've a line to rule for it, and I don't know what to put. Where's beyond?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:117:"Treswick," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:119:He felt the room take that. It was a registry town, the last on the Line, with three walls and a book at its gate; and there was not a crew on that cloth which had not come east to be out of reach of such places. Somebody set a cup down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:121:"What'd you do there?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:123:"We read a survey," said Karis. She stood at Cael's shoulder, with the flat under her arm. "An office has measured four places beyond this river for six years. I had its sheets, and I could not read them; and there was a man there who could show me how such a thing is put together. I have read them now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:125:"What'd it pay?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:127:"Nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:129:"And what came of it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:131:"A bearing," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:133:Pike considered him. Then the pen went into the horn, and he wrote, slowly, in the square black hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:135:*Treswick. Survey read. Bearing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:137:Cael read it as it went down, a word at a time, and thought only that it was short.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:139:The pen had gone on to the two ruled columns at the line's end, where a price stood, and what became of it. It hung over the first, and passed on, and hung over the second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:141:"I've nothing for those," said Pike. "It paid nothing, and it's not out yet. They'll stand empty, and I'll say that they're empty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:143:He said so, to the room. Then he turned back three leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:145:It was the season's line, with its ruled space at the end. Cael had seen that space left blank on purpose at first snow, and carried blank over a thaw onto a new cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:147:"I said that I'd leave that until I'd a column for it," said Pike. "I've not found the column. I've had a hundred and sixty-eight days to look." He dipped. "A blank's a kind of entry, so long as a man knows why it's there. But a blank with nothing against it can't be told from one that was forgotten. So I'll put a day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:149:He wrote it in the space, small: the day, by his own book's reckoning, and no figure after it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:151:"That's when I left off looking," he said. "It's not a number. It says that on this day the keeper of this book knew what he didn't know, and signed for it." He blotted the place with the heel of his hand. "Eleventh. And dated."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:153:Then he looked sideways, and up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:155:"You're on my side."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:157:Cael looked at where his own boots were. The room had pressed in behind Brom, and he had been carried round the bench's end with the press, and had fetched up at the one-armed man's elbow, on the side where the candle was. He had read two leaves of that ledger over the stump of an arm. Every line of them had lain the right way up, and he had not once marked it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:159:"I'll go round."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:161:"Stop where you are. It reads the same from there. I never said that it didn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:165:Teague came down between the benches while the ink was wet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:167:He did not hurry; he stopped a stride from the bench's end, with his thumbs in his belt over the hand-axe, as he had stopped at that hearth on a night of snow; and the room, which had begun to talk, left off again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:169:"I said after the thaw. It's a month gone. I said whatever's on that bearing, and I've just heard him write the word." His eyes went along them, and they did not stop at five; they went on to the door-post, where a stick was grounded. "I'll say it here, where it's heard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:171:He let the room wait for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:173:"Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:175:Cael heard the count before he heard anything else, and so did the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:177:There had been five names on that cloth for the whole of a cold term and more. A captain had just said six of them, aloud, and nobody at the bench had corrected him; it was a count for a contract. It was not an admission, and it had not been made by any of the five; and Cael, who did not turn his head, knew by the stillness at the door-post that an old man had heard exactly how much it was and how much it was not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:179:"Say what it holds," said Brom. "I'll not put my thumb to a figure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:181:"It holds four things. The count's the first, and you've had it. Neither healer's on it. A healer goes where she's wanted, and I'll not have one sent." Teague drew a thumb out of his belt. "Shares by the count. Whatever it pays is cut ten ways; and what it costs is carried the same, so far as a cost can be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:183:"It's paid nothing yet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:185:"I know what it's paid. My bow's told me three times." He drew the other thumb. "The field's mine. On a road, with steel out, there's one voice, and it's the one that's had the most practice. I'd not ask it if I thought that you'd say otherwise."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:187:It was said to Cael, and it was said level.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:189:He priced it, as he had been taught to price a thing by a big man who would not let a figure go unsaid. He had led five people into three places where a Path was worth nothing, and had brought them out; nobody else in that room could say as much. But he had been touched three times in a square of snow without once seeing it come, and the man in front of him had learned more from his boots in those three touches than Cael had learned from a face in a year. On open ground, with a watch to be read and ten people to be put somewhere, there was one of them who was better. Cael had written so in a book. He had not been asked to say it in front of fifty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:191:"It's yours," he said. "I'd have asked you to take it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:193:"And the reading's hers." The chin went toward Karis, by the width of a finger. "Where it runs, and what a place is when we're stood in front of it. I'll take that from her as I'd take a ford from a man who'd crossed it. I can't read that skin, and I'll not pretend."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:195:"I shall want to be asked before anybody steps over a thing," said Karis. "I shall not always be quick."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:197:"You'll be asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:199:"And him?" said Brom. He had not looked toward the door-post. "You've built your count on a thing that you've never seen stand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:201:Teague had not looked that way either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:203:"The wall's still the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:205:"There's a month on it," said Cael. "It's a healer's, and it's got twelve days to run. He stood one in the middle of it, against her word, for us, and it very nearly cost him the leg. Nobody stands it up before the month's out. Not for your field, and not for mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:207:"I'd not ask a thing with a month on it to be shown for a count." Teague looked once more toward the door-post, and it was the look of a man reading a load that he does not mean to lift yet. "I asked what I needed, of a factor. I know where it stands, and that it stays. That'll do me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:209:He put both thumbs back into his belt, like a man drawing the last rope over a load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:211:"Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:213:He nodded, by the width of a finger, and it was done, and he had not offered his hand. There was a cloth on a nail behind Pike's head with one name alone on its first line and five in a row on its eleventh; and a contract did not take either of them down. Cael found that he did not wish it to. He would sooner walk east beside a man who was ten places better at a thing, and said so, than beside a friend who had to be reminded which of them the ledger preferred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:215:He wrote it at the hearth's end, when the room had thinned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:*Two hundred and ninety-seventh day. Lowmarch; the hearth's end, and one on the keeper's settle. Eleven miles, and the ferry. The rope is paid: sixty yards of four, and he would take no fare. Pike has twelve lines, and a thirteenth with nothing in its last two columns. He has put a day against the season. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: eighteen of thirty.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:219:*Teague's term is taken. Ten on his count, by a contract and nothing else. The field is his. He is still the better at it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:221:*Known: Pike had a carter's word for the cut before we were home, and Teague had his terms by heart when he came down the room. I read Pike's line from his own side of the bench.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:223:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:227:The cart had gone down in the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:229:Cael saw it from the bench under the great ash as soon as there was light to see by, on the two hundred and ninety-eighth day, when the meadow was grey and forty fires were being blown up out of their ashes. It was a salt cart, with a pair of mules, and it had been drawn onto the grass at dusk by a carter who did not know how soft the thaw had left that corner. It stood now with its near wheels sunk to the axle. Half its sacks lay on the turf beside it. The carter had the mules out and was standing with his cap in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:231:He was standing before Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:233:They were too far off to be heard: twenty strides, across the square of the old silent yard, where the pegs still stood. Brom sat at Cael's side with his arms hanging. They watched a captain look at a cart for some time, as he looked at any load, and then at a lean old woman who had come down the bank with her board under her arm. She said one thing to him, and he answered it; and she shut her mouth, and stood back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:235:"He's going to do something," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:237:Teague walked round the cart's tail. The ash had put a root out there, as thick as a man's thigh, a pace behind the sunk wheel. He set his right heel against it, with care, shifting the boot twice, like a mason bedding a stone. Then he laid his right palm flat on the end of the cart's bed, with the fingers spread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:239:What he did next took him longer than the thing itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:241:He lined himself up; Cael had no better word for it. The heel was set, and the knee came in over the heel, and the hip over the knee; the shoulder dropped, and turned, until the whole of that side of him ran straight from a root in the ground to a palm on a board, like a prop that is set under a beam before the wedges are driven. Not a part of him lay off that line. He stood so for three breaths, and Cael saw the boy move up behind him, on the left, with a cloth steaming across both hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:243:There was no light, and there was no sound but the cart's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:245:It went forward. It did not roll; it was put. The mud let go of two wheels with a noise like a boot drawn out of a bog, and a ton and more of oak and salt went the length of a man's body up onto firm ground, and stopped there, dead, as though a team of six had taken it up at once and then been cut out of the traces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:247:It went that far and not a hand farther.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:249:Something came back. Cael saw it arrive: the straight arm jarred, from the palm to the shoulder, like a pole that has been struck on its end. And he heard the wrist, at twenty strides, across a meadow: one small dry click, such as a latch makes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:251:The cloth was round it before the arm had dropped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:253:"That's a Force," said Brom, very low. "I'd wondered what he kept in that leather. It comes up out of the ground, through a man, and out at the hand; I saw one shift a block at a quarry once, when I was small. They'd sent for him from three valleys off." He was watching the old woman, who had the arm on her board already. "They said that he'd been a deal stronger at twenty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:255:The carter had found his tongue, and was pointing down the meadow, to where a second cart stood canted in the same soft ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:257:Teague looked where he pointed. He answered across the boy's head, in the voice that he kept for a string of mules, and it carried to the bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:259:"Don't put it in front of anything that needs doing twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:261:Then the tall man and the bow went down the meadow with three iron bars and a baulk of timber, and the second cart was shifted as a carter would have shifted it, in the better part of an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:263:Cael had the book on his knee before the first bar was set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:265:Nothing had come to him, and he had not looked for it. He was twenty strides off, on a bench, and nobody in that meadow had meant anything at anybody; a man had put his hand to a cart. But he had seen a line drawn through a body, plainer than he had ever seen one drawn on a hide, and he knew better than to trust it to his memory.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:267:*Seen, at first light, the meadow, from twenty strides. Teague's is Force. He set a heel against a root and a palm against a cart's tail, and stood until every joint between the two lay straight. Then it went: one push, the length of a body, and no more of it after. It wants firm ground behind and a thing in front. What comes back comes down the same road and is paid where it left him. The wrist. I heard it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:269:*One use, and the arm is on a healer's board. He would not do a second. Nothing came. Twelve.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:271:He did not know, that morning, where he would next see that line drawn, or what it would be drawn at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:275:"File," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:277:He had the leather on again, over a turn of the old woman's linen, and he carried the arm a little away from his side. He stood under the ash with eight people before him, in the square where they had once hit one another with sticks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:279:"I'll not drill you. There's no time, and you've your way and I've mine. But ten people who've never walked a road together will walk it like two crews, and a thing that hunts will count two. So we'll walk this meadow, round, till it's one. Nobody speaks. Nobody lights a finger. I'm told that there are hands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:281:"There are four," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:283:She showed them, as she had shown them to an old man on a moor. A flat hand, palm down, was *stand*. A shut fist was *seen*. Two fingers lifted were *come on*, and a hand turned over empty was *nothing*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:285:The tall man made each of them once, with a big chapped hand, exactly, and grounded his spear. He had not spoken to any of the six in all the days that Cael had known him, and it appeared that four more words which were not spoken suited him very well. The bow made them from the settle of his hip, sourly; and the boy, who had them at the first showing, made them again, quickly, to himself, and then a third time behind his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:287:"You're early on the flat one," said Lira. "It's not a race. You go still when you've seen it, not when you think I'm about to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:289:"I saw your shoulder go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:291:"Then you stood for my shoulder. Somebody else'll have a different shoulder." She did not smile; she had been taught by a healer how a thing was told. "Wait for the hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:293:He nodded with his whole head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:295:Teague set the file as Cael would have laid out a bout, from the ground upward, and he gave a reason for each place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:297:"The quiet one's out front, where he's always been. I'll not move him. I couldn't find him to move." It was said without a smile, and Seln, at the north-west peg, inclined his head by a hair. "Then my spear. Then me; and the one with the book at my left hand, a stride back, since he sees a thing before he knows that he's seen it, and I want to be told. Then the girl, and the boy behind her. He'll watch her feet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:299:"And the papers?" said Brom. "She's at a table, and she'll not be fetched from it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:301:"Then she'll be told. In the middle, with whoever's healing, and a beast when there's a beast. Then the big one, since he's slow to answer and hard to hurt." His eyes came to the end of the row, where an old man stood on a stick. "Then you. A stride behind him. I'm told that's where you've stood."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:303:"It is," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:305:"And my bow last, where he can see all our backs and the road behind them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:307:It was the first time that those two had exchanged a word, and Cael marked it. The old man had opened his mouth afterward, as he opened it after any instruction, and Cael waited for the clause; but Teague had already turned to the pegs, and whatever had been prepared was not called for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:309:They walked the meadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:311:They went round it in file, at a road's pace, between the fires. Carters stopped with their bowls half lifted to watch nine people go by without a word. It went badly at first: the two halves of the file kept a different distance, and at the first flat hand the front stood like posts while the back was still closing up. At the first fist the boy looked, and the bow did not, since he had never in his life been told where to look by a girl's hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:313:By the third round they stood together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:315:It was a small thing. Cael, at a captain's left shoulder, a stride back, felt it happen behind him, as a person feels a door shut somewhere in his own house: nine people stopped on one flat hand, and there was not a sound in that corner of the meadow but the fires. He glanced once to his right, and Teague had not turned his head. But the chin had come up, by the width of a finger, at nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:317:They were in the fourth when Lira left the file. A small clear voice had called her name from the gate of the inn yard, twice, in a tone that Cael knew. She was gone for as long as it takes to cross a yard, and she came back at a run, and touched his sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:319:Her face had a look on it that he had last seen at a parapet, above a town, when she priced the whole of a hill in one breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:321:"She wants you," said Lira. "Now. She's ruled three boxes on a sheet, and I don't think that she's drawn breath since the second."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:1:# Chapter 45 — Oldest Last
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:3:She had the store behind the chimney, where five people had lain out a cold term, and there was not a hand's breadth of the deal that was not covered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:5:Cael stopped in the door. It was a little past sunrise on the two hundred and ninety-eighth day, and the one small window laid a square of thin light across the whole of it. The courier's flat stood open at the table's head, with its six rows out and a pebble on each. The calfskin lay unrolled beneath them, under the Ternhall brass, and a dead man's book was open at its first page. Three of the Stair's sheets had been taken from their box and set in a row; and in the middle of everything, side by side, lay a leaf of her notebook and a square of stiff card with a turn of brown tape beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:7:Karis stood over the two with her gloves off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:9:"Shut that," she said, without turning. "And come round to this side. I will not have it read wrong way up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:He came round. Lira had the door, and Brom filled it a moment after, with a quiet man at his elbow who had not been seen to cross the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:13:"I have it," said Karis. "I had it at first light, by the candle, and I have spent an hour and a half since trying to break it. I have not been able to, and I should like six people to try."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:15:She put the butt of her pencil on the card.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:17:"This is his drawing of the fourth line. He drew what was there, and where he could tell a stroke of the writer's from a scratch of the knife's, he has ticked it. This is mine, made the same afternoon with a sewn hand." The pencil crossed to the leaf. "I was sure of three signs in the last six, and I told him so. In that gallery, by one lamp, with the skin before me, it was the truth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:19:"And by a window?" said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:21:"By a window I have two copies made by two people who see in two different ways, and I can lay the one upon the other. Where his tick stands and my stroke stands, in the same place, that is a stroke. It gives me the second sign." She drew breath. "Four, of six. And a word of six signs of which I have the first, the second, the fourth and the last is a word that I have met. I kept it under a glove for three days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:23:She had turned to the Stair's sheets. Her finger went to a group of signs on the nearest; and to the same group on the next, lower down; and to the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:25:"It stands four times on that floor, always behind a sign that bears. I uncovered it on my ninth day under the town, and it was not the word that I had wished for. It was only *set*, as a stone is set, or a stake."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:27:Nobody spoke. Vastin had come to the door by then, on the stick, with the tarred case under his arm; he had stopped on the sill as a man stops who is not sure of a room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:29:"So the fourth line says this," said Karis. "It says: the mark. And hung from the mark, level, with neither above the other, two things that take it. The one is those who are kindled, and the other is what is set. And after both, a number, which is three."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:31:"What's set?" said Lira. "Set where?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:33:"I do not know where; the list does not say, and I shall not say it for the list." She held out her bound hand toward the door, palm upward, without looking. "I should like the case."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:35:The old man brought it to the table himself, and undid the strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:37:She drew the iron out. It was a stake as long as her forearm, numbered thirty-two, with its point still bright, since nobody had ever driven it; and she laid its head under the window, beside the first page of a dead man's book and the corner of an office's sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:39:A short stroke had been struck into the iron, and beneath the stroke a bar. The same stood in pencil in the book, and in a clerk's ink in the sheet's ruled square.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:41:"That is a thing which was made to be set in the ground," said Karis. "It has a mark on it, which I turned over for a hundred and eighty-three days, and called two copies of one ignorance. I had the word for it all the time. I had read it aloud, once, in another line, and it had not occurred to me that a hole in a moor and a child of fourteen could be given a thing out of the same drawer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:43:She laid her bound hand flat beside the three of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:45:"A mark is a grade. The one they give a boy at fourteen says what he's *for*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:47:Cael looked at the three marks, in iron and lead and ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:49:He had been stood on a plate once; and something had been written about him at the head of a sheet, in a place that a printer had ruled for it, and he had carried it ever since. He had known at a spring that a hole in the ground could be filed in the same fashion. He had not known until this morning that the two filings were one word, in one line, on the back of a skin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:51:He put it where he put the rest. It had its verb already, and the verb was hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:55:"It says three," said Karis. "I have only ever seen one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:57:She had a clean sheet before her already; she laid the brass along it, held the strip down with the heel of her bound hand, which she was not to lean on, and ruled three boxes side by side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:59:"There is a stroke with a bar. That is on the stake, and in the book, and on every sheet of the four places that I matched to the hide." She drew it in the middle box, small. "I told you at the spring that there were other sheets, under other signs, and that I would not call them places. I turned their corners down, and I did not tell you everything that I had seen on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:61:"You said nothing of their squares," said Seln, from the sill. "I remarked it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:63:"I thought that you had; I saw the square, and I shut my mouth on it. A second mark, on sheets that I would not so much as call a place, was the first step of a theory; and I had six people round a rock who would have taken the second step for me." She had drawn the sheets out while she spoke, from under six turned corners, one from each row. "They are one group: the same four signs on all six, one sheet to a year. And this is what their square holds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:65:It was a stroke, and beneath the stroke two bars.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:67:She drew that in the right-hand box; and then she laid her pencil down on the left-hand one, which was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:69:"I have been through all six rows this morning, by the squares alone. There is no sheet with a stroke and nothing under it. I have ruled a box for it because the list says three, and because a thing with one bar and a thing with two want something to stand before them. Nothing goes in it, and I shall not guess what would."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:71:"So there's a kind that there aren't any of," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:73:"There is a box that the office has not used in six years. That is all that I will sign."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:75:"It's tiers," said Lira. She had come to the table's corner, and she was looking at the three ruled squares as she had looked at three walls from a rise. "First, second, third. They've a ladder for holes in the ground."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:77:"It is three marks. Call them what you like." Karis took the pencil up. "But a grade grades something. The one that is given at a kindling grades a person, and there is a man in this room who signed for a good many. This one is not for size, as I showed you at the spring; a place of twice the measure has the same bar. It is not for which is fenced. So I have gone through everything else in which one of these sheets differs from another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:79:She turned the Stair's newest sheet so that its foot lay toward them, and set her finger in the right-hand corner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:81:There was a number there, small, after a clerk's flourish; Cael had seen it at the spring, on every sheet, and had taken no more notice of it than of the printer's name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:83:"Ninety-four," said Karis. "On the row beneath it, the same place has ninety-three, and then ninety-two; it goes down by one with every row, to eighty-nine. I took it for a drawer's number, and so it is. I never asked what the drawer was counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:85:"It is a series number," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:87:He had not moved from the table's end; he spoke to the sheet, low, and with great care, as a man walks across ice that has let him through once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:89:"I can tell you what the form is. A return of that kind carries the year of its series: the count of years from the first sheet that the office holds upon the place. The figure is carried forward by whoever draws the next, and nobody is obliged to ask where it began." He stopped. "It does not say that anybody walked there in each of those years. It says how long ago the first sheet was drawn, and no more. I say what the form is; I do not say who drew the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:91:"Thank you. That is exactly as much as I wanted." Her finger had gone to the hide, and it touched each ring in turn, going east. "The Fallow's newest sheet stands in its sixty-first year, and the Stair's in its ninety-fourth; the hall's is a hundred and thirty-eight. And the place behind the iron—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:93:She laid the sheet down upon the fourth ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:95:"Two hundred and six."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:97:Nobody at that table said anything at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:99:Cael did the sum, since it was there to be done. The paper did not say who had gone to that fence, or how often. It said only that a first sheet of the place behind it had been drawn two hundred and six years before the one that lay under her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:101:"I had supposed that it was six years," said Seln, very quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:103:"And the two bars?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:105:Karis turned up the corner of the sixth sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:107:"It has no figure; there are two signs there, out of the index, which I cannot read." She looked down the table. "Is that the form?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:109:"A series that was opened before the present manner of numbering is carried under its old style," said Vastin. "I should expect it, and I do not know when the manner changed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:111:"Then I can say that it is older than two hundred and six, and I can say nothing more. I shall not put a figure to it until I have one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:113:She laid her pencil down across the three ruled boxes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:115:"The one on a sheet says how *old* the thing is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:117:The old man had taken the iron up from under the window while she spoke. He laid it back in the tarred case, beside the cord and the mallet, and drew the strap; it had still never been driven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:121:There was a man in the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:123:Cael had not heard him come, and did not know how long he had stood there. He had his thumbs in his belt, and his right arm hung a little away from his side, in its leather. He had not come in, and he had not so much as looked at three ruled boxes; his eyes were on the calfskin, and on a strip of brass that lay along its pencil line and ran off the edge of the skin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:125:"I'm not here for the marks," said Teague. "I'd not know one from another; I want to know how far."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:127:"I was coming to that," said Karis. She did not seem put out. "The sheets with two bars have one line that the others lack, above their eight stations. It gives the four signs of the place behind the iron, and then a point of the compass, and then a measure in lengths. I saw that at the spring as well, and took it for a fence's survey tied to its own floor." Her pencil tapped the line. "It is a run, from the fourth place to this one. An office has written down how far it is from the one to the other, and which way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:129:"Which way?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:131:"By a point of the compass, which I shall lay against the hide before I say it aloud." She held out her right hand, across the table, toward Cael. "You have a pair of brass legs in your coat. I asked for the loan of them when I should have a hide in front of me again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:He took out Hesk's book, and the dividers slid from between its leaves into his palm, shut, with the cork upon their points. He had carried them for six days, under a ruling of his own that was a hundred and thirty days old: he did not put his hand to a thing that measured, since a cord had run short in it and two edges had disagreed. An old man in Denvash had sent them to step off a line with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:135:Cael laid them on the deal, and pushed them across with one finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:137:"They're lent," he said. "I'll not open them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:139:"I had not asked you to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:141:She drew off the cork, and opened the legs, with her right hand, upon the hide; it was the first time that they had been opened on a distance by anybody. One point went down in the middle of the hall's ring, and the other in the middle of the fourth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:143:"The carter who drew this skin drew it in days. Brom. The hall to the iron."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:145:"Three," said Brom. "With a mule, and a girl in a sling, and a healer gone north."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:147:"A surveyor's length I know to the stride. I walked behind that cord for a whole cold term, and I know what a day of ours comes to in it." Her lips moved over the office's figure. "This run is three such days, less a little." She closed the legs by the width of a hair; then she lifted one point, and swung it round, along the brass, as a person walks a pair of compasses across a chart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:149:It came down off the skin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:151:It stood on the bare deal, a hand's breadth beyond the calfskin's edge, hard against the Ternhall strip; and the low sun from the window laid the brass's shadow over it, so that the point stood in a narrow dark line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:153:"There," said Karis. "I have laid the office's point against the carter's line, and they do not differ by the width of this strip. It is under the edge, and I did not put it there. I laid a figure on a hide, and that is where it fell."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:155:"Three days past the iron," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:157:"As we walk. A little less."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:159:Cael looked along the skin, from the left hand to the right. There were four rings, and a point that was not yet drawn, and a number against each: sixty-one, and ninety-four, and a hundred and thirty-eight, and two hundred and six. They rose as they went. Beyond the last of them, in a strip of shadow, stood something that the office had stopped counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:161:"Somebody laid them down in order," said Karis. She said it to the hide, as she would have read out a measure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:163:"Who?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:165:It was the question, which had been asked on a road once, of a pattern, and had not been answered. The old man at the table's end had drawn breath, and Cael saw the right hand leave the stick, and begin to rise.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:167:Karis had not turned her head; but her bound hand had come down flat upon the sheet of three boxes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:169:"It's observation. I'm telling you what the paper says. Not what it means."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:171:The hand came down upon the ash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:173:"Noted," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:175:It was one word. He had not begged anybody's pardon, or said that he was drafting. He had made a stroke at the foot of the thing, as a person does who has heard a term and means to keep it; and then he looked up the table at the boy whose word it was, with something in the grey face that was very nearly surprise at himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:177:Lira had turned her head. So had Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:179:"That's ours," she said, under her breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:181:"He's had it by him long enough," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:183:The man in the door had not followed any of that; he had been looking at the hide the whole while, as he looked at a load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:185:"Oldest last," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:187:It was not put as a question. Karis answered it all the same, with one small nod, as she would have passed a figure that had been read back to her correctly; and he took his thumbs out of his belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:189:"Then that's where we're going."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:193:They were on the south road by the middle of the forenoon, in the file that a captain had set under an ash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:195:It held. Cael had not been sure that it would, outside a meadow. A quiet man went ahead, small against the pale grass, and after him seven feet of ash with a leaf of steel on it. Then came a pair of shoulders that did not look round; and Cael walked a stride behind the left of those, where he had been put. He had never in his life walked at anybody's shoulder to be asked what he saw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:197:Behind him he could hear the rest by their feet. There was Karis's, with the flat and the hide, and beside it the tap of a lean old woman's staff. A big man came next with a box; and last of all there was a slow uneven step, the bad hip of a man with a crossbow on his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:199:There was no stick in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:201:That had been settled in the yard, and it was the first thing that the field had cost anybody. The salt cart that had been put out of the mud was going down to the mill for meal, light, behind its two mules; and its carter had said to Teague, cap in hand, that a man's arm was owed something. Teague had looked at an old man with a slat on his leg, and at the length of a ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:203:"It's owed a seat," he had said. "For him. I'll not walk ten at the pace of one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:205:The old man had opened his mouth. Then he had looked at the nine who would have had to wait for him, and had shut it, and gone to the tail-board. He had offered his copper, and the carter would not have it; he said that it was the captain's fare, and paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:207:So a cart went on a furlong ahead, with a brown coat on its tail, and for the fourth day together Cael walked toward a pair of boots that hung.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:209:They passed the well at the fork, which had a house over it now, with a door hung and a new rope on the windlass; a crew had taken that from the plank in the cold. Then the road climbed, and they were on the back of the ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:211:It was the same pale grass, combed all one way, and the same wind. He had come along it once in the dark before a frost, behind a mule, to ask a healer whether she would come; and he had gone back along it with the answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:213:Nobody spoke. Twice a flat hand went up, far ahead, for nothing that Cael ever saw, and the file stood, and went on at two fingers. The second time the boy stood exactly when Lira did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:215:They came to the fork a little after noon, where one pair of ruts kept the ridge. The other went down the western face through a notch that wheels had worn, and the cart had stopped at its head, where the carter was locking a wheel. The old man had got down there, as he got down for every hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:217:There was a flat rock there. Teague lifted his chin at it, and the file broke, and sat, and the old woman undid a strap of leather and looked at a wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:219:Cael took out the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:221:He had sat on this rock with it once, and written two lines about a healer. The mill's wheel was to be heard below, very small, like a tired man splitting wood. He set the day at the head, and the count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:223:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day. The fork above Millrace; the flat rock. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: nineteen of thirty. He rides, on the field's ruling. Karis opened the dividers. I lent them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:225:Then he wrote the rest as it came, and it came all in one piece.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:227:*Karis cracked the grade. The sites are ranked by age and the bearing runs oldest-last — somebody laid them out in order, and the order runs into the deep edge, toward the first one they ever made. Second-tier's the fourth site, the one the Compact fenced. Third-tier's out past it, on the line, quiet for centuries. Teague's crew came after the thaw like he said — ten of us now, and he's still better at this than I am, and the ledger still says so. Two crews, one bearing, and a straightedge my guardian sent me because my grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. Turns out she was right about the line. Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:229:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:231:He read it through. Only the ranking was the paper's, with a place past the fourth that was older than two hundred and six. The centuries, and the figure near its foot, he had from a gallery, where a thing was read aloud once; he had laid them beside her morning's work himself, and it was more than she would have let him say at a table. He had not said it at a table. He had put it where he put what he thought, with his own name to it, and he let it stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:233:A hand came over his shoulder; he gave Lira the book, and she read it with her lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:235:"Your grandmother," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:237:"Hesk's wife, and I never knew her. She's only ever been a sentence on an order-leaf."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:239:"It's a good one," she said. She shut the book and handed it back, and looked down the notch, where a wheel was knocking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:243:The valley was green, and the water came down it brown and fast over a weir of stakes and stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:245:Three of the stakes were gone from the weir, and Cael saw a captain see the gap. The tall grey building stood beyond, with its wheel going round in a white fall. There was the fence of split posts, and the dozen roofs, and the ford below with a flat stone on either bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:247:A woman sat on the nearer stone with a net across her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:249:She raised her head at the sound of boots, and counted them down the track with her chin, one at a time, and did not hurry over it. Then she turned and called toward the mill, without getting up, in a voice that had been pitched to carry over a wheel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:251:"Eleven off the ridge."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:253:The answer came from a lean-to against the mill's southern wall, low and hoarse, from somebody who had not troubled to come to the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:255:"I know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:257:There was a brown mule tied to a fence-post, with a white foot. She had her head up already, and both ears pricked at a big man with a box; and Brom, who could not lift an arm to her, stopped in the track and said something under his breath that was not meant for anybody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:259:The bench along the mill's wall was empty, at the end of a day. The old man had come down the notch last, a tread at a time, behind the locked wheel; he reached the bench as the others reached the fence, and looked at it, and plainly thought it wiser to be found on his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:261:Oryn came out of the lean-to, wiping her hands a finger at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:263:She was thinner. That was the first thing, and Cael had not expected it; three weeks of five gates had come off her as a cold term comes off a horse. She stopped at her stride and a half and looked the eleven of them over, and it took her no longer than it had in a cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:265:Her eyes passed over him as they passed over a gatepost. They went to a girl who stood with both hands on her hips; to a small pale woman with one of hers inside her coat; to a big man whose arms hung. They went to four people whom she had seen across a meadow, and never had her hands in. And they stopped on a leg that was twice the size it had been when she bound it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:267:Her mouth went as it went at the smell of a wound left too long.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:269:The lean old woman had come up along the wall, with her staff and her board. She stood a pace from the healer's elbow, and neither of them said a word. They looked at one another once, for the length of a breath, as two carpenters look who have each been shown the other's joint; and then both of them, together, turned and looked at Brom's right shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:271:"I'll come to that," said Oryn. "It's had a box on it; I can see the lean from here." She had not taken her eyes from the big man. "And I'll come to you," she said, in the same voice, to the old one by the bench. "Stand there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:273:It was a stride to his left, by the lean-to's post, where the light was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:275:Cael watched him. He had heard that man answer every instruction that was ever put to him, for three hundred miles: with *I cannot undertake*, with *as to the last*, with a clause hung on the end like a seal on a ribbon. He saw the mouth open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:277:It shut again. The stick moved, and the leg after it, and the old man stood where he had been told. He hung no clause upon it. He gave her a figure instead, as he would have handed up a return.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:279:"Nineteen days," Vastin said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:281:She did not so much as glance at the slat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:283:"Then it's eleven more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:285:"There's a count," said Teague.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:287:He had come down to the fence with his thumbs in his belt. It was the first time that he had spoken to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:289:"Ten on it, for a line that runs east. It's contract, and it's mine in the field, and neither of you is on it." His chin went to the old woman. "She's said that she'll not be counted. I'd sooner hear yours from you than be told it by a boy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:291:Oryn looked at him as she looked at a patient who has walked in on a broken foot and wishes to discuss the weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:293:"I've three holds owed yet on this round," she said. "They lie the way that you're walking, for a day and some; and after that my road turns, and yours doesn't. You'll not count me, and you'll not send me. A crew doesn't write a well on its sheet because the road happens to pass it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:295:She threw the cloth over her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:297:"I'm a stop on my own route."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:299:Nobody argued it. Brom had gone into the mill by then, and he came out with the miller's wife behind him and a sack on the bound shoulder; it was a mark's worth of meal and a flitch, for five days of country with no gate in it. He said the figure as he came: a hundred and twenty-seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:301:Then he set down what he had carried under his box's lashing for seventeen days, which was a wooden bowl, on the end of the bench; and he turned a keg up on its end before it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:303:Lira looked at the keg, and then at the healer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:305:"Sixth chair," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:307:Oryn considered it for the space of a breath, as she had once considered a milking stool.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:309:"On my terms. Which are the only kind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:311:She sat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:313:It was a keg, and not a chair. She lowered herself onto it as she did everything, squarely, and took up the bowl; and Cael, who had watched that woman eat standing for as long as he had known her, a stride and a half from the nearest of them, found that he had to look at the weir for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:317:"Shoulder," said Oryn. "The left. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:319:She had lit a lantern in the lean-to, and hung it from the turf roof's pole. The wheel was still going on the other side of the stone, and Brom sat on the plank across two kegs, with his shirt off, like a boulder that somebody had brought indoors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:321:"Aye," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:323:The old woman had already gone to the right, and she did not ask; it was her own knot, and nobody else had been let to touch it. She picked at the drover's turn with her nails, and it came, and four yards of webbing went slack across his chest. Oryn watched her do it, and did not put out a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:325:Then her own thick fingers went round the left shoulder, before and behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:327:"That's a wrench," she said, with her eyes shut. "It's a fortnight old, near enough, and you've used it since. You took a weight on it that was coming down, and you took it as a man. There's nothing of yours in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:329:"I'd a clerk's yard all round me; I'd have been a line in a book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:331:"I'm not blaming you; I'm reading it." Her lips moved. "I can shut that, since it's torn, and a torn thing I can do. Hold still."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:333:It took her a quarter of an hour. When her hands came away she was grey about the mouth, and Brom lifted his left arm slowly, as a man lifts a lid that he expects to be nailed. It went to the height of his chin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:335:She had moved to the right, where the old woman's hands were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:337:"And this I said I'd not touch. Twice mended, and not a third. It's had a box on it for twelve days, against what I told you at a fork." Two fingers pressed. "It's no worse. I can't think why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:339:The old woman said nothing. She took the webbing up again, and laid it shorter this time, in two yards and not four, with the turn behind the arm, and drew it down with a jerk that made the big man grunt. Then she looked at the healer, and Oryn looked at the knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:341:"That'll do," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:343:It was as much as either had said to the other, and Cael thought that it was a good deal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:345:"Arm," she said next. "The left. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:347:"Yes," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:349:She had it out already, bare to the elbow, with the eight marks pale along it. She sat on the plank with her chin up, as she had sat on a block at Thornwater when a knot was undone; and the healer took the forearm in her two hands and went along it from the wrist, with her eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:351:"You listened when it told you the second time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:353:"Once," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:355:Cael thought of a girl coming off a ladder, under a town, with that arm tucked against her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:357:"Once is once more than I priced." The healer opened her eyes. "It's an arm," she said, and laid it down in the girl's lap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:359:Lira looked at it there, and shut the hand, and opened it. She did not say anything; but she drew her sleeve down over the eight marks herself, which Cael had not seen her do since the Fallow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:361:Karis had to be asked twice, since she had brought her notebook to the lean-to's post and was writing in it by the lantern.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:363:"Hand. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:365:"Yes. I beg your pardon. Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:367:The strip of shirt came off, turn by turn. Under it the palm was laid open to the light: a long straight seam across the heel, drawn together with seven stitches of black waxed thread, each as thick as a bootlace's end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:369:"Who sewed this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:371:"I did," said Brom. He had gone red. "With a roping needle. There wasn't a healer inside that gate who'd touch her without a name."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:373:"It's the ugliest piece of sewing that I ever saw hold," she said. "It's held; it's shut from end to end, and it's clean." She had a small knife out. "They've done what they were put in for. I'll have them out, and I'll spend a little where the thumb pulls. You've been writing with it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:375:"It was required."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:377:"I don't doubt it. Write. Don't lean."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:379:The stitches came out one at a time, and Karis counted them, under her breath, in a tongue that nobody else there had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:381:That left the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:383:He was still standing at it; he had stood there for the better part of an hour, on one leg and a stick, in the lantern's edge; and when she turned to him at last, wiping the knife, she stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:385:"You've not moved."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:387:"I was told to stand there," said Vastin. "I was not told that I might leave off."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:389:"Sit on the plank. Leg out. I want my hands on that knee, and on your wrist. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:391:"Yes." He let himself down. "There is a thing which you must have first. I asked that you be told it, eight days ago, and I find that I had rather tell you. I stood a wall upon that leg on the eleventh day, and I had your words in my head as the stick came down. I heard them, and I did it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:393:"I can see what you did. I've been looking at it since you came down that notch."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:395:Her hands went round the joint, above the slat and below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:397:"It held in a cut," said Oryn. Her thumbs moved. "And then you asked it for a second, with eleven days on it. I can feel the pair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:399:She did not spend on it. She took the slat off, and bound the joint again in her own linen with two new laths; and what she said, when she had counted his wrist, was that the month stood as it had been counted, and that he was not to mistake that for a kindness.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:401:"Noted," Vastin said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:403:Her hands stopped on the linen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:405:"That's new."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:407:"It's a useful word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:409:"It's his." She did not look round at the lean-to's post, where a boy stood. She tied off the last turn, and her mouth went down at one corner, which with her was most of the way to a smile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:1:# Chapter 43 — Here
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:3:There were sheep on the high moor, and that was the first thing which told Cael that the country had changed while they were under a town.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:5:He saw them at the third mile on the two hundred and ninety-sixth morning: a scatter of grey backs on the swell ahead, with their heads down in the bents and the east wind laying their wool over. There had been none on that ground sixteen days ago. A flock is not turned out where eight things lie up round a hole in the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:7:At the fourth mile the ruts began to draw in toward a black place in the rock, and a spare grey person stood beside them with a crook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:9:It was the factor. There was the leather apron, and the slate on its cord, and the pair of pale eyes that had gone along six people and a healer at a gate and counted them. They counted now. They went from a quiet man, who had come up level with the cobs, to four with loads; and they rested last upon the tail-board, where a pair of raw hands lay on an ash stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:11:"Five," said the factor. "And the one that walked behind. He's not behind today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:13:"He's paid for where he's sitting," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:15:"I'd not doubt it. He paid my woman a copper to be told where the straw was." The crook went out toward the moor. "Those are mine. I've not had a ewe on this top since the first frost, and I'd three hundred lambed in the yard for want of it. I've had thirty-one carts up that cut in a fortnight, where I told you thirty. The salting-shed's full to the door."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:17:Nobody answered, since it was a road. But they were at the lip of a hold's own ground, with the hold's keeper speaking, and Cael judged that the rule would bear a low voice there, as it had borne one in that yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:19:"We're glad of it," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:21:"I didn't come up onto this top to make you glad. I've a thing to put to you, and I'll put it once, since I was told beforehand what I'd hear." The pale eyes had come to Brom, who had the purse. "That seep'll have its glass back at the first frost. Another den'll find it, in a year, or in five. I said so on that bank with your wet one standing by. I'd as soon not sit in my yard and wait to learn which."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:23:"What's the offer?" said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:25:"Twenty, at each first frost, on the same bar, to the five that walk it before I let a wheel on. The long house's fire for the week that it takes, and the pot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:27:Brom priced it. Cael watched him do it, with his head a little on one side and his weight on both feet, as he priced any wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:29:"It's a fair wage," he said. "It's a better one than the first, though it's the same twenty; you didn't know the first time that it could be done." He rubbed his jaw with the back of his wrist. "We're walking east. There's a line that we've undertaken to follow, and it goes clean away from your frost, and I can't tell you which side of a cold term it ends on. I'll not take a hold's silver against a week that I can't swear to be standing in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:31:The factor nodded, once, like a person who has heard a figure read back correctly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:33:"He said you'd say that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:35:"Who did?" said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:37:"Him that's first on the one-armed man's cloth. He's leather on his wrist." The slate was turned over, where a column of strokes stood. "He brought my salt up himself, behind the first of the carts, with three of his own. He asked me what I'd seen from that lip; and when I'd told him, he asked it again, which nobody ever did before. Then I said that I meant to keep the five that had done it, if silver would keep them. He said that they were for the east. He said I might offer, and welcome, and that I'd have it back in very nearly those words."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:39:Cael heard that with a small cold shock, as a person hears his own step counted by somebody behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:41:"What did he ask you twice?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:43:"How long the thing stood, that the eight ran into. And whether the old man lifted a foot while it did." The pale eyes went to the tail-board, and stayed. "I told him what I saw. It stood for as long as he never moved; and he never moved. I said that the man who made it called himself a fool for the place he'd made it in. He didn't seem to take the fool's word for that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:45:"I had hoped that he would," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:47:It was low, and exact, and it was the only thing that he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:51:The dray went into the cut first, with its wheels in the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:53:There was no glass. A fortnight and more of thaw had come up that hill since a stick cracked it, and the seep ran down the middle of the road now as a seep ought, a stride wide and a finger deep, talking over the stones. The lean man had his near cob by the cheek-strap. He walked it down on the wet with its head held, and the other followed, as beasts had been walked down that place all through a cold term by people who had the nerve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:55:The six went after on the dry edge, in file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:57:Cael had gone up this strip of gravel with a twist of tow in his fist and his mouth as dry as the tow. He had come down it afterward at a walk, a little bent, with eight things pouring over a bank behind him. He knew every yard of it. The black rock stood at the head, with the water coming out under it in a skin; the scree hung on his right hand like a steep roof, where a girl had lain on plates; and on his left the bank was no higher than his belt, with three worn places in its upper half.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:59:It was the same cut. It was not the place that he had been afraid in, and he could not at first have said what was different. Then he had it. The cold was gone out of it. On that day the air had come up to meet him like the breath of a cellar, and now it was only the shade of a hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:61:Lira went down it with her face turned up to the scree. She had lain on those plates with a knife, on a day when the left arm could do no more than hold. He saw her mark the place, and shut that hand once at her side, and look away. Karis did not look at the banks at all. She was watching the water go under the dray's wheels, with her lips moving, like a person who has written six findings about a thing and suspects that there is a seventh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:63:Behind him the stick's sound had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:65:Vastin had got down at the head, as he had undertaken to do for every slope. He was coming down the dry edge last of all, a tread at a time, with the bound leg swung out stiff; and forty strides from the top he was standing still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:67:There was nothing there to stop for. Wet stones lay in the road, and gravel beside them, with a tuft of dead bent growing out of it. No crack ran from bank to bank. That had been made in ice, and it had gone where the ice went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:69:He did not turn to see who was near him. He looked at the wet stones, and spoke to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:71:"Here," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:73:It was the place. Cael knew it by Brom's stone, three strides below, and by the lie of the two banks; there was no other sign of it in the world. A plane had stood on that spot from one bank to the other, as high as the scree, and a man had been the foot of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:75:The old man looked at the wet stones for the space of several breaths.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:77:Then he lifted the stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:79:He did not lift it far. The ash came up off the gravel by the breadth of a hand, and was held there, level, in his right fist; and the whole of him came over onto the bound leg, as a load is let down onto a prop that nobody is yet sure of. Cael saw the slat take it. He saw the grey mouth shut hard. It was one breath, and the half of another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:81:The stick came down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:83:The knee had held. It was not mended; anybody could have seen that, by the sweat that had sprung on the old forehead in that cold shade. But it had borne a man once, with nothing beside it, on the very ground where eight hounds had been stopped by it and where four holes had been torn in it afterward. He had not been told to try it. He had not asked leave of anybody; and Cael noticed that for once he did not turn to see who would enter the thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:85:Brom had turned on his stone, below. He had put out one flat hand, without knowing that he did it, as a man steadies the foot of a ladder that somebody else is on; and when the ash came down he let the hand fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:87:"Held," he said, low.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:89:The old man looked from the big man to the stones. He had said one word in that cut, and had been given one. Seventeen days ago, on the same ground, the loudest thing on the whole moor had been his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:91:"Two words," Vastin said. "Both of them ours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:93:"Noted," Cael said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:95:He had given that word to a captain three times, at a hearth and on a landing. He had never before given it to this man. It was a clerk's word, and he had kept it from him as a person keeps his purse from somebody who once had the counting of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:97:It did not feel like a clerk's word on that road. It felt like a stroke made at the foot of a slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:99:Lira's two fingers had gone up, below, where the dray's tail was going out into the light. They went on down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:103:He stopped once more in that cut, and it was not for the old man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:105:It was a stride farther down, level with the middle of the road, where the water ran now. Brom had gone by his own flat stone without a look, since he was not a person who visited places. But Cael had stood in that road on the two hundred and seventy-ninth day, behind the middle of an invisible thing, with his face two feet from it. He could not have lifted a hand there, while eight broke on what was in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:107:*This far?* said the eleventh.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:109:It had asked that of every sill and kerb and milestone for six days. It had never asked as it asked now. It had the voice of the place in it. There were two banks, and the gap between them, and one line where a line had been proved; and the thing under his breastbone knew that ground as a key knows its own ward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:111:He had stood two feet from that wall, and nothing had come of it. He had written so in a byre loft. *Ten.* He had not understood then why not, and he thought that he did now. A thing came to him when somebody meant it, with the whole of himself, at something that could mean it back. On this ground the man had stood a wall before hounds, on his own judgment, for a rule that he did not yet believe in. In a stair he had stood one before six people with hoods, for nobody's rule, with nothing behind him but the choosing; and it was there that the thing had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:113:*No*, he told it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:115:It lay down. It did not sulk. But he felt the weight of it as he went on, as he would have felt a coin in his boot; and he knew whose coin it was. He had six people's leave to find before he could hand it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:117:The three carts were gone from the foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:119:Somebody had broken them up for their timber. A single wheel leaned against the bank's end, with two spokes out of it, and there was a black ring on the turf where the staves of a barrel had been burned. A cairn stood beyond, where the foot-track left the ruts and went up the spur to a wall with smoke behind it. The factor had gone back along the lip by the sheep-walk. Nobody had been asked up to the pot, and nobody had expected to be; a hold that has been told no does its civilities afterward by leaving a person alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:121:Seln's fist came up at the cairn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:123:It did not go out to the side, where a hound would have been. It went back, over his shoulder, toward the hill that they had come down from. Cael looked where it went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:125:The cut was a black notch in the spur behind them, with the road coming out of it. Above the notch the shoulder of the moor stood against a grey sky; and on that line, a long way off and quite small, there was a man on a horse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:127:He was not moving. He sat where the ruts came over the skyline, and it was not possible at that distance to tell which way he faced. He wore something dark, and the horse was a bay or a brown; a mile of grey air took the rest. Then the horse put its head down to the bents.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:129:The fist opened, and lay flat. They stood. A carter stood at his cob's head as a carter does, and an old man on a tail-board looked at his own knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:131:Nothing came of it. It was a rider on a road, on a morning when carts were using that road again, behind six people who had no quarrel with any horse. After the space of forty breaths Seln lifted two fingers, and the file went on round the foot of the spur. But the small grey man had let it come up to him, which he never did; and as Cael went by he saw the right hand go out at the hip and turn over, palm upward, not empty but asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:133:*Nothing?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:135:Cael did not know. He made a fist of his own, low, where it could be seen: *seen*. It was all that either of them had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:137:They did nine miles that afternoon, down off the shoulder, with the wind at last a little on the quarter. The moor fell away in long steps toward the east. There was a beck across the ruts in a spread of wet stones, where a grey thing had once walked beside them for half a mile and stopped as if at a wall; the cobs drank there, and nothing paced them. Twice Cael turned his head by the width of a hand. The first time there was only heather. The second time the horseman was on the last rise but one, a mile behind, coming on at exactly the pace of a dray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:141:The halting-ground lay in its fold beside the beck, with a dozen rings of stones on it, and there was ash in three of the rings that had not been there before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:143:Carters had lain on it since the cut was opened. They had not lit fires; the black was old, and the white was only what a wind had turned over. But somebody had left a bundle of dry heather under a flat stone for the next comer's bed, and a crust for the birds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:145:They ate by the same dead ring as before. Cael marked who sat where, since it was the kind of thing that he marked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:147:The old man sat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:149:He did it without a word, on the stone that he had stood beside on the two hundred and seventy-eighth night with his bread in his hand. He let himself down by the stick and one palm, with the bound leg out before him across the ring; and when he was down he looked round the six faces, in the order that he kept, as though he expected the thing to be remarked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:151:"You sat," said Lira, under her breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:153:"I did. I have sat on a board in front of six people for two days, going backward, at a copper a day. I find that the ground is no farther off, and that it is free."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:155:"You'll have to get up again. In front of us. With that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:157:"I am aware of it. I have been got up by one or another of you a good many times since a stair, and I have not yet been charged for it." He laid the stick across his thighs. "I am told that a road has no keeper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:159:Brom had been ladling cold barley into the seventh bowl. He stopped with the ladle lifted, and a slow look went over his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:161:"It hasn't," he said, and put the bowl into the raw hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:163:No copper was laid upon a stone that night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:165:Seln had not sat. He stood at the fold's upper end, where he could see the ruts in both directions, and he spoke when the bowls were empty, at the pitch that the dusk allowed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:167:"There is a horse and a man. He kept a mile from us for the whole of the afternoon, as nearly as I could pace it against a dray. He did not come into the cut while we were in it. He came through afterward, at a walk." The hands were in the sleeves. "I did not see his boots. I should not have known them at a mile."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:169:"A hold's man," said Brom. "Or a carter's lad sent on for the boat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:171:"He may be either. A hold's man does not ride a mile behind a dray for nine miles when he could be past it in a quarter of an hour. A lad sent for a boat does not stop when a file stops." Seln looked down the fold. "I do not say that it is the same man. I say that I told you a road would be watched, and that somebody is on it who behaves as a watcher would. I can put it no higher, and I take no comfort from how low I must put it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:173:"What does he learn?" said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:175:"How many. Which way. That one of you rides, and five carry, and that there is rope on the cart. That you stop at a cairn and do not go up to a gate." He was silent a moment. "It is very little, and it is exact. I have sold less."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:177:Lira had been watching the old man across the ring with her chin on her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:179:Her right hand came out, flat, palm to the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:181:It was done without warning, as she had done it to him on this same road, walking backward down the ruts. Every person at that ring went still, since that was what the hand meant; a big man stopped with his thumb in a bowl. But she was not looking at any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:183:Vastin had not risen. He could not have, in the time. He had let his shoulders come forward, and his head hang, and both hands go loose on the ash across his knees. He looked like an old carter who has nodded off on a stone after his supper. There was nothing upright in him anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:185:She turned the hand over, empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:187:"That's bent," she said. "You said you didn't know that you could do it bent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:189:"I had it from a girl on this road. She told me that a post is a thing which nobody looks at, and that she had looked at me first of everything on the moor." He straightened by degrees. "I have since been a hand on a card, and matter on a cart, and I was not looked at for eleven days by a town that used to send a horse to its bridge for me. I find that I have the way of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:191:"You've the way of sitting," said Lira. "It's a start."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:193:Cael wrote by feel, on his knee, when the light was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:195:*Two hundred and ninety-sixth day. The halting-ground in the fold; thirteen miles. The cut walked east. Water in it, and no glass, and sheep on the top. Vastin walked it: seventeen of thirty. He stood on the leg once, with the stick off the ground, where the line had been. The factor offered twenty at each first frost. Brom said no. A hundred and twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:197:*Known, twice. The factor had been told what we should answer, by Teague, who took the salt up; he asked how long the wall stood and whether its maker moved. And a man on a horse kept a mile behind us from the cut to the dark. Seln cannot put a name to him. The one kind of being known offers a wage. I do not know yet what the other kind is for.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:199:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:203:It was noon of the two hundred and ninety-seventh day when the road came down through the alders, and the river lay beyond them, wide and brown, with the cable swung across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:205:It had fallen two feet since they left it. The lowest plank of the landing was dry. Up the bank the long stack lay under its tarred sheets, and a brisk man with ink on his cuffs was walking along it with his clerk; and when he saw two brown cobs come out of the trees, with forty coils behind them, he began to walk a good deal faster.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:207:Then he saw who walked beside the near wheel, and he stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:209:"That's him," said the lean man, quite loud, to nobody. "That's the wrong kind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:211:Brom had gone a dull red again. He kept his eyes on the sheet. Cael and Lira had the lashings off before the wheels had stopped, and they drew out the one coil that was not the broker's, and ran a stave through its eye. It came off between them like a dead calf. It was the weight of half a man, as it had been called.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:213:"I've four," said the broker. He had come up, in spite of himself. "Round a heart. Forty coils, and more laying."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:215:"I see that you have. It's good four. I looked at it on the peg." Brom said it gravely, and he meant it. "I'd have had mine of you a fortnight since, and been glad."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:217:"You'll not want any now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:219:"I've what I was asked for. I'd not have said a word against the other, to anybody, if I'd known that it would be carried about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:221:The broker opened his mouth, and found nothing in it. Somewhere down the landing a lad laughed. The lean man took the straw out of his own mouth and held up one open hand to the six, as carters did; and it was all the leave that he took of them, after three days and forty-odd miles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:223:Vastin got down off the tail-board for the last time, and paid his third copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:225:The ferry was at the planks. She had come over for the afternoon's carts, set a little aslant on her two straps, and the ferryman stood at her rail with a long sour face and watched a coil of rope carried down a bank toward him on a stave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:227:They laid it on the planks at his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:229:He did not speak. He went down on one knee, which Cael had not seen him do for anything; and he took the rope's end between finger and thumb, and bent it back on itself, and opened the lay with his nail. He looked in for some time. Then he drew a fathom through his two hands, slowly, with his eyes shut, as a woman feels a cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:231:"That's four," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:233:"Round a heart," said Brom. "Sixty yards. I paced it off against her wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:235:"Where's it from?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:237:"Treswick. The walk under the town wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:239:"I know the walk. She lays tight." The ferryman got up. He looked along his own hauling rope, where it came in over the sheave from the oak post, grey and fretted; and Cael remembered that two yarns had been gone from it since a flood. "I've crossed on that since the thaw, forty times, with my heart in my mouth and a turn of old line for a stern strap. I lent you a rope before the snow. I said what I wanted back, and I never looked to have it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:241:"It's what you asked for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:243:"It is." He stooped, and took the coil by its eye in one hand, and dragged it up the planks toward his rope-house without help. He said the rest over his shoulder. "She's a copper a head. Not today. Today she's paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:245:It was the nearest that Cael had ever heard that man come to thanking anybody, and it was a good deal nearer than he had come for a strap held with a back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:247:They went over with the carts. Seln stood at the stern and watched the western bank grow small.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:249:"He has not come down to the landing," he said. "There is a horse tied in the alders, above the last bend. It has no rider on it. I do not know where the rider is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:251:Nobody answered, since there was nothing to be done about a horse in a wood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:253:Lowmarch came up out of the dusk on the eastern side as it had come up on the first evening that Cael ever saw it. But the meadow was not as he had left it. There had been nineteen fires along that water on the night before the first boat. There were more than forty now; the holds had opened, and every crew that had lain up for the cold was down to hear what the plank would say. Carts stood wheel to wheel by the smith's rail. There was a light in every window of the lower inn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:255:He counted the meadow, since he counted anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:257:Then his eyes went up the bank, to the house on the rise. It had one window on the river side. There was a lamp in it; and against the lamp, for a moment, before the scow's bow touched, he saw the shape of a man who was standing quite still, with his thumbs in his belt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:1:# Chapter 42 — Five Piers
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:3:Brom swept the handcart a second time before the light, though he had swept it at dusk, and stood its shafts against the stable wall with their ends level.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:5:"She said swept," he said. "She never said by whom, or how often."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:7:The woman of the half-door came out to look at it with a candle. She looked at the wheels as well, and at the floor of the end stall, where an old man had folded his blanket in four and laid it on the manger. She said that she had kept lodgers over those horses for eleven years, and had never before been handed back a stall tidier than she had let it. Then she counted them out under the arch by their feet, as she had counted them in, and went back to her bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:9:It was the two hundred and ninety-fifth morning past the Line. They had lain thirteen nights in that loft, and Cael had not once gone in at the painted door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:11:The rope-walk was awake before the street was. A lantern burned at the head of the long shed, where the wheel stood, and another far down it, as small as a star; and between the two a line of people walked backward in the half dark, with the yarn running out between their fingers. Brom went along the pegs as he had gone along them thirteen days before. He did not need to hunt. He stopped at the coil that he had stopped at then, and took a fathom off it, and bent the end back, and looked in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:13:"Four," he said. "Round a heart."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:15:"It's the same that you looked at," said the woman with the tarred hands. "I told you it'd be the same going out. I've had it by since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:17:"You'd no call to do that. I never put money on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:19:"You hung my fathom back as you found it. I've sold rope in this shed to men who'd not do as much for their mothers." She had the coil down already, across her two arms. "Sixty yards. It's the price that I named."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:21:He counted it onto the head of a tar barrel: two marks, in silver, out of the purse that lay inside his shirt beside a thumbed slip. He watched the coil while he did it, and Cael saw the frown go out of his face a little at a time, like a knot being worked loose with a spike. That debt had ridden in him since before the snow. It had gone over a river and come back, and up a moor, and into a town, and through eleven days under a registry. Here it lay at last, sixty yards of the right kind, across a woman's forearms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:23:"A hundred and twenty-eight in hand," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:25:Then he looked at his own arms, which he could not lift above the elbow, and at a box that stood beside his boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:27:"I can't," he said. "I've been turning it over since the fire. It's the weight of half a man, and it'll not go on a back that's got a box on it already." He said it without shame, as he would have said that a plank was short. "I asked you last night who hauls."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:29:"You've no need to ask. Look behind you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:31:There was a dray in the lane. It was a long low thing on four wheels, with two brown cobs in its shafts, and it was loaded to the rails with coils, tarred and white, under a sheet that had not yet been lashed down. A lean man with a straw in his mouth was going round it, trying each knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:33:"That's for the western landing," said the woman. "Three days down the moor road. There's a broker there who's sold three-strand for as long as I've laid it, and a fortnight since he sent up for four. Forty coils. He wrote that a big man from over the water had stood in front of his stack with a full purse, and called his rope the wrong kind, and gone away with the purse; and that he'd not have it said to him twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:35:Brom had gone red to the ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:37:"I never called it wrong. I said that it wasn't what the man had asked for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:39:"He's taken it hard, whatever you said. That's forty coils that I'd not have laid." She jerked her chin at the lean man. "It's his road, and you'll ask him. But I'd think that a coil could ride with its own kind, to the place where it's wanted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:41:The carter had heard. He came round the tail of the dray and looked Brom over slowly, from the boots upward, as a man reads a waybill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:43:"You're the wrong kind of excellent," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:45:"I've been told so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:47:"They've had it on that landing for a fortnight, for a saying. A lad drops a keg, and they tell him that he's the wrong kind of excellent." He took the straw out of his mouth. "Throw it on. I'll not charge a coil that's going where I'm going."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:49:His eyes went past Brom then, and found an old man standing on one leg and a stick, with a tarred case under his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:51:"And that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:53:"That walks as far as he's able," said Cael. "After that he doesn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:55:"I have a copper," said Vastin. "I have had it ready since last night, in the expectation of being asked what I should cost to carry. I should prefer to pay it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:57:"There's a tail-board. It's a copper a day to sit on, and you'll get down for every hill; I'll not have my cobs pull what can walk. And you'll hold your tongue on the moor. I'll not need to tell you why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:59:"You will not," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:61:He counted the copper into that brown palm, and it was kept. Cael saw the old shoulders ease by the width of a finger. It appeared that this road, at least, was going to take his money.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:65:The gate was opened at first light, as two painted boards had promised, and they were in the arch with the first of the carts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:67:The keeper in undyed grey sat on his high stool with the frame on his knee. He looked at feet, and wheels, and hooves, as he had looked at them thirteen days before, and his thumb moved the beads: eight hooves, and four wheels. He came to the boots, and Cael counted five pairs with him. The thumb went on to the tail-board, where a pair of boots hung that did not touch the ground, and it stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:69:"Those are feet," said the keeper, to himself. "They're on wheels."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:71:"He came in on them," said Lira. "You moved a bead for him. I watched you do it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:73:The keeper looked at her, for the first time. It was plain that nobody argued with that frame from one cold term to the next. Then he moved two beads along the upper wire, slowly, as a man pays a debt that he had hoped was forgotten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:75:"Six out," he said. "Six in, the day I had you. It comes even."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:77:Nobody at the sloped desk wrote a word of them. A load on a back was the back's, coming or going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:79:Cael stepped over the sill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:81:It was one long stone, worn hollow in the middle, with a socket at each end. *There?* asked Anchor, in its thin held note, of the two sockets, and he told it no. He had crossed that stone thirteen days ago to walk into the oldest registry on the Line, and he crossed it now with no name of his in any book of that town. He had not known that such a thing could be done. He did not think that it could be done twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:83:He counted the piers. He had counted them coming in, twice, since Karis would ask; he counted them now for a reason of his own. There were five. They stood in the water like the knuckles of a hand laid flat, each with its bay above it where a person on foot might stand aside. The thing behind his eyes had come round to the first of them before his boot was on the roadway.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:85:It wanted that bridge badly. It was a thing of dark stone laid dry, each block cut to its neighbour, by somebody who had meant every joint. *Bears*, the thing would have said, of each pier in turn. He could feel the word waiting, five times over, like five steps going down into water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:87:*One*, he said instead, and looked at the first pier's cutwater, and at nothing else of it. *Two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:89:It was his own count, and it was made with his own eyes, which could not do two things at once. The lean came up short against it, as a dog comes up against a door that has been shut without unkindness. At the third pier the grit had not come. At the fifth he knew that he had found a thing which he could use. He had spent his life counting what was in front of him so that he need not feel it, and for once the habit was worth something to somebody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:91:Karis had stopped in the third bay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:93:She had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other; and her bound hand lay open on the parapet's dark cap, palm down, in its linen. She was not measuring anything. She only stood, with her head a little on one side, and looked along the roadway at two grooves that were deeper than a wheel's rim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:95:"I was told that it would be here on the way out," she said. "It is here."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:97:"I said that," said Brom. He had stopped the whole file behind her, box and all, while the dray went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:99:"I have not got a week, and I have not got a sheet for it." She took the hand away. "I know more about what stood here before the walls than any person living. I have it from a skin, and a board, and three floors that are a week east of this. I have not so much as drawn the thing that I walked over to reach them. I should like that entered against me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:101:"It's entered," said Cael. "Draw it going home."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:103:"Which way is that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:105:He had no answer for her, and she had not wanted one. She shifted the hide, and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:107:He was in the last bay when he understood that he was free to turn round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:109:It came to him quite plainly, between one stride and the next. A healer had made him write a thing in a book at a fork-stone: that inside Treswick he did not go near the station. He had kept it for thirteen days, to the letter, down a lane with four white alleys in it, and through a night when a thread of light on a loft's boards had not been steady. And the gate was behind him. The river was the Line, and he was over the river. The promise had been for inside, and he was out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:111:He could stand in this bay, as any carter might, and look back up the whole of that hill to the top of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:113:A girl with a box on her back had come up on his left side. She did not look at him, and she did not look anywhere else. She had kept her chin level down every yard of this road, coming in, without being asked, and it was level now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:115:He did not turn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:117:It was not the promise. That was paid, and she would hear at a mill that it had been. It was only that he had seen a light go unsteady once, for a count of one, and had written *hold* beneath it. A person who has ruled *hold* does not go back to stare at the thing, to learn whether it will do so again; that is not holding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:119:"Five," he said aloud, to Lira. "It's still five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:121:"I'd have been surprised," she said, and went by him off the bridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:125:The road ran on its causeway for three miles, with a ditch on either hand, and a white mist stood in the river meadows as high as the cobs' bellies.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:127:It was a town's ground. A person might speak on it, as far as the first hedge, and for a long while nobody did. The dray set the pace, which was a stick's pace or a little better. The lean man walked at his near cob's head, and the six came after his wheels in the order that they had learned, with a quiet man gone on ahead into the white. Cael walked last, behind the tail-board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:129:So he had an old man in front of him, riding backward, for the whole of twelve miles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:131:He had not thought of that when the copper was paid. Vastin sat on the board's edge with the bound leg laid along a coil and the tarred case across his thighs, and both raw hands upon the stick's head; and since he faced the way that they had come, he faced Cael. There was two yards of road between them. It did not grow or shrink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:133:*This far?* said the eleventh, of the three-mile post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:135:It stood at the western end of a carters' pull-in, where a hedge stopped and a bank ran on beside the ditch. Cael knew that bank. A man had let himself down upon it, in the dusk, where he could not rise again without a boy's arm, and had said why he once declined to read that boy a second time. They had called it ground afterward. It had not been friendship, and it was not now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:137:*No*, he told the thing. It had asked at a milestone, as it asked at every line that anybody had ever drawn across a road. The man whom it had come out of looked over his head at a town, and did not know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:139:"I have ridden up to that gate on four occasions," said Vastin. He spoke low, though the ground allowed more. "Each time I was met at the bridge. I certified what I was shown, and I rode down again, and I did not once see it go small behind me. One does not, from a saddle. One is looking at the next district."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:141:"What's it look like?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:143:"It looks like a hill with three walls on it. I had supposed that it would look like more." He considered it a while longer. "I was eleven days in the bottom of it, as a hand, on a card that I could not have signed. I find that I knew it better from a stall than I ever did from the registrar's table. I do not say so to be thought humble. It is a finding, and it does not flatter the office."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:145:Cael said nothing to that. He had the fair copy of a leaf inside his coat, against a pair of brass legs; and he had two things under his breastbone that the man in front of him had never been told of, of which one was the man's own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:147:That was the weight of the morning, and it was all in those two yards. He had entered a wall once, at this man's asking, and had entered more than was asked for. He could not pay it. A thing of that kind could not be told by itself; whoever heard how the eleventh had come would have heard how all of them came, and that was not Cael's to hand over. It belonged to four others who walked in front of the dray, and to a healer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:149:"You are thinking that I am owed something," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:151:Cael's step did not alter. He had been trained to that by a healer's hand on his chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:153:"I've a face, then."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:155:"You have very little. I sat opposite it for three days once, with a plate, and I came away with nothing. But I have had a fortnight of it now from the side, which is another matter." The grey eyes had come down from the hill. "I am not asking. I said in a loft that I know no more of what you are than the plate did, and that I do not ask; and I have not found it harder to keep than a good many orders that I was given. I say only that I have noticed that I am not told. I enter it without complaint. I kept six people's worth of such things for a living."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:157:"It's six people's," said Cael. "That's why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:159:"I had supposed so. Then it is in better keeping than most of what I was ever owed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:161:He turned his eyes to the hill again. It was the whole of what passed between them on that road, and Cael found it harder to walk behind than a silence would have been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:163:The second post stood by the brook, where a plank had been thrown over for people on foot. There was a gate into a field there, hung on dressed stone, and a headland of unploughed grass beyond it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:165:"That's your field," said Lira. She had dropped back beside him. "You lay in it for a quarter of an hour, and came out looking like a man who's lost a tooth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:167:"I'd lost an answer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:169:"It's the same face." She had slipped her left arm out of its becket while she spoke. The box hung from the right shoulder alone, and she let the left hand take the other becket low, at her hip, and carry there. "Thirty. That's her month. I've been slow for the whole of it, as I was told to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:171:"And now?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:173:"Now it's an arm," said Lira. "She said so. I'm only the first to hear."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:177:The hedges ended at the foot of the rise, a little before noon, and the road's rule came down on them there like a lid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:179:It was Seln who put it on. He had been thirty yards ahead of the cobs all the morning. Where the last laid hedge gave out, and the ruts ran up onto open ground, he stood in the road with his right hand flat at his side, palm down, until the whole file had seen it. Nobody needed the words. The lean man spat out his straw. The girl who had talked for three miles about a broker's face shut her mouth on the rest, and every sigil on that road went into a pocket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:181:Cael felt the twelve of them settle, as hounds settle at a threshold that they know. Wind had his feet already. The read asked, civilly, what the ruts weighed. He went round the house and shut the doors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:183:"Hill," said the carter, without turning his head. It was the one word that he had spent since the hedge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:185:The old man got down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:187:He did it as he had learned to do everything in the last fortnight, by his hands, a little at a time, with the sound foot finding the road first and the stick after it. The case he left on the coils. Nobody offered him an arm, since he would have had to refuse it aloud. But a big man with a box on his shoulder had fallen back to walk on his right, and Cael was on his left, as he had been once before on this very road, going the other way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:189:It was a mile and more to the top, aslant, with the wind coming over it into their faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:191:That was new. It had blown out of the west on every day that they spent upon that moor, and had been at their faces going in; it had gone round in the night, and it was at their faces coming out. Lira looked up at the grey sky with the look that she kept for unfair rulings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:193:Cael counted the plantings of the stick, since he had nothing else to count. Each of them cost a breath. He had walked behind that sound, and beside it, ever since a ferry's planks, and he knew its two voices: the dull one, when the leg was only tired, and the short one, when it was hurt. This was the dull one. It had been the short one all the way down these same ruts, fourteen days ago, with a case on the shoulder above it that a healer had forbidden.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:195:They came to the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:197:The dray had stopped there, to let the cobs blow, and the lean man had a hand on a wet neck. Behind them, a long way below, lay a green plain with hedges on it, and a river in loops; and in the nearest loop a bluff stood up with three walls about it in steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:199:Nobody turned. Seln had gone over the crest already. Lira stood beside the near wheel with her back to the west, tightening a becket that did not need it; and Karis had the hide against her chest, and was looking at the cobs. Cael looked at them too. A horse's ears are a good thing to look at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:201:It was the old man who turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:203:He did it on the stick, the whole of him, since the knee would not let him do it by halves. He stood on the crown of the road with the east wind in the skirts of the brown coat and looked back, for the space of three breaths, at the last town on the Line. He had certified it four times under his own seal; and he had left that seal in a tray, a long way south of here, with nothing written to go beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:205:His right hand came off the stick. It went up a little way, toward the breast of the coat, where a pen would have been. Then it stopped, and hung, and took hold of the ash again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:207:He turned his back on the plain, and held up the hand for the tail-board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:209:The fork-stone stood at the rise's eastern foot, as tall as Brom, with a worn hand cut into each of its faces. One hand pointed along the ruts by which they had come. The other pointed north, where a second pair went away under the moor's edge toward a line of blue hills.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:211:Those ruts were empty. There had been frost in them and rain since a mule went up, and no print was left of four small hooves or of a square woman's boots. There was only the fold a little way along, with its roofed quarter and its stack of peat, where six people and a healer had sat round a hearth while a term was written in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:213:Brom did not stop. But as he went by the stone he put his left hand up, as far as it would go, which was to his own chest; and he laid two fingers on a thing that rode under the lashing of his box, which was a wooden bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:217:After the fork the moor took them, and there was nothing to do for six miles but walk into the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:219:It was a country for people who had made their peace with one colour. The swells went away eastward, brown beyond brown, with the ruts drawn over them like a pair of pencil lines; there was no tree, and no wall, and the only thing that stood higher than a sheep was the dray. The wind came out of the east steadily, with nothing in it but cold. It found the gap between a collar and a neck. It leaned on a box as a hand leans, and by the second mile Cael had the ache of it across both shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:221:He kept the file, behind two brown boots that hung from a tail-board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:223:Nobody had spoken since the hedge. He had forgotten in thirteen days how loud that silence was. A town's noise is a blanket; a road's quiet is a room with every door open, and a person who carries twelve things that would each like to answer has the minding of all the doors. The quietest of them would lift at dusk. He could feel where it lay, as a person feels where the cold place is in a bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:225:He had gone west along these ruts with ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:227:He went over that as he walked, since the road asked for nothing but his feet. There had been a boy on this moor fourteen days ago with a date in him that he had not written down, and a page that he had not touched, and a promise in a book. That boy had been afraid of a gate. He had thought that the worst thing a town could do was to write his name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:229:It had not written it. It had done a good deal more than that. It had let him hold a lamp for ten days over the oldest paper in the country; and at the end of the ten, a small pale woman had read out, once, what the whole of it had been built against. He had two things more in him than he had carried up this road, and a paper in his coat, and a sentence that he had said aloud by a fire. He was the same weight on the scale. He did not think that he was the same load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:231:Seln's fist did not go up once in six miles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:233:That was a thing to be entered too. Eight had laired round a sink on this moor, and five of them had been dragged out of a cut by a hold's people, and three had gone through rotten ice. It seemed that nothing had yet come to learn the road in their place. A wheel could go over that ground now from a river to a town. Five people had seen to that with a rock and a knife, for twenty marks; and an old man had done the rest of it with a knee. There had been thirty of them at the outer gate that morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:235:The outcrop came up out of the east a little after the middle of the afternoon: a block of the dark plated stone as big as a barn, a furlong south of the road, with a thread of green going away from its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:237:The carter knew it. He turned his cobs off the ruts without a sign from anybody, as a man turns in at his own gate; and the dray went rocking over the bents toward a basin that somebody had built round with stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:239:Karis had quickened her step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:241:She had not meant to, and Cael saw her check it. There had been a slab at that spring, twelve feet long and waist-high, lying level on its own rubble in the lee of the rock. She had called it a table. She had laid six years of an office's paper out upon it under river pebbles, and nothing had stirred but a corner now and then by the width of a finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:243:The wind was in the east today.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:245:It came straight along the outcrop's face, with nothing between. The slab lay as it had lain, grey and level and bare; and a dead stem of bracken went over it while they watched, end over end, and off the far side. There was a row of pebbles yet at one edge, set out by size. Two of the smallest had been blown down into the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:247:Karis looked at it for some time, with the flat under her arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:249:Then she walked round the end of the rock, to the western face, which had been the weather side on every other day of that year. It was in shelter now. There was no slab there; there was only wet turf, and a litter of fallen plates, and not a level place the size of a sheet of paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:251:She sat down on one of the plates, with her back to the stone, and laid the flat across her knees, and did not undo its straps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:255:They ate cold in the wrong lee, with their backs against the rock, and the carter ate with them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:257:He had turned his cobs out on the green by the overflow. He took the seventh bowl from Brom as a thing that the road owed him, and he had it empty before the old man had begun; and then he lay down under his own tail-board with his cap over his eyes. Nobody lit anything. It was a furlong off the road, and a person might have spoken there. Nobody did that either, beyond what the bowls wanted. They had six miles of wind in their ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:259:Cael went round to the basin with the pot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:261:Seln was there before him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:263:He stood at the water's edge with his hands in his sleeves and his back to the slab, looking down into the basin; and it was so exactly where he had stood on the two hundred and eightieth day that Cael's foot stopped of itself. He had been at that slab then, with a row of paper under his hands that counted a sheet short. He had looked up, and found a small grey back turned to him at the water. And when he looked down again there had been a sheet lying before him on the rock, which had not been there, with a registry year at its head and a day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:265:He had laid that day against the first line of his own file. He had done it twice, a figure at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:267:He had written none of it. He had said none of it, to a healer or a girl or a man with a lamp. In the fifteen days since, a leaf had been read aloud in a gallery which made a good many things look different, and he had not once let himself carry that sheet across to stand beside it. That was for the clean page, if it was for any; and a woman had laid that page down bare, with a bound hand, and had said that nobody was to rule a line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:269:The grey back had not moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:271:Cael knelt at the basin's lip, upwind, and dipped the pot. The water was as cold as a knife. Neither of them said one word. He did not know what that man had made of a date, and he had never asked. It came to him, on his knees in the bents, that he was not the only person at this spring who kept a thing out of his book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:273:He carried the pot back round the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:275:Karis had not opened the flat. But she had the reader's roll of card out of her coat, with its turn of brown tape; and beside it, on her knee, her own notebook at the fourth line. She sat bent between the two, in what was left of the light, with her bound hand holding the one flat and the pencil going in the other; and her lips were moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:277:"Six," she said, under her breath, as he went by. It was not said to him. "The first. The fourth. And the last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:279:"You said that you'd not guess."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:281:"I am not guessing. I am looking at how sure I am of three." She did not lift her head. "It is another kind of work, and it is the only kind that I am allowed tonight. Go away."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:283:He went, and sat down against the third box, and took out Hesk's book. The brass legs slid from it into his lap, shut, with the cork upon their points. He had carried them for three days and had not once opened them upon a distance. A ruling of his own stood in the way, and it was a hundred and twenty-seven days old. Whatever measured had read one way in four hands and another in his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:287:*Five piers. I did not look back from the bridge. The promise held to the gate and is done; the not looking was mine.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:289:*Known: a carter at a rope-walk knew Brom by a thing that a broker said three days' road from there. I have been written down since I was fourteen. It is not the same as being known, and I do not yet know what it weighs.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:291:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:293:The light was gone before he had ruled the line. Shadow lifted in him, as it lifted at every dusk, and the other eleven dropped away under it like a yard going quiet under snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:295:He told it no, and it lay down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:1:# Chapter 46 — Not Flow
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:3:He found her above the weir, at the last of the light, sitting on a stone with her hands in the race.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:5:She had spent on a shoulder and a hand that evening, and had read an arm and a knee; and she sat as she had sat on a road after four holes in a leg, with her head down and her lips moving. He waited until they stopped, while the water came down the cut channel beside her, fast and brown, toward a wheel that had not yet been shut off for the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:7:"You've a thing to say," said Oryn, without turning. "You've had it in your boots since you came down that notch, and you walked as if one of them had a stone in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:9:"Two things. I wrote that you'd have them from me at this mill, before anything else was said."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:11:"Then say them. I've a loaf to eat before I'm fit for you, and I'll hear it while I eat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:13:He had the book out, and he did not open it; he had found on a ridge that he did not need to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:15:"You told me to write it down and stop touching it. On the two hundred and eighty-first day I read myself twice, with yours: once in a field by a brook, and once at a milestone, in the dusk." He kept it level. "I went round the place, and not into it, and I told myself that round wasn't touching. I knew what you'd meant when you said it. I read your words for the leave that I wanted. I'd do it again, and I'm not asking you to say that it was right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:17:She had taken her hands out of the water, and she dried them, a finger at a time, on the skirt of a dead man's coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:19:"What did you get?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:21:"The same nothing. But I found out what the nothing was. Yours feels what moves; it was all that it ever felt, in you or in me. Where nothing moves it comes back empty, and it would come back empty from a gatepost. I'd been calling that a reading of the place, and it was only the end of the tool."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:23:"I could have told you that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:25:"You did tell me. You said that you'd never felt such a thing in anybody, and I heard a finding. It was a limit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:27:She was silent for a little while, with the loaf in her lap, and then she broke it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:29:"I set a rule on you for a healer's reason," she said. "A person who's found a sore place goes back to it every night with his fingers. You kept my words, and you went round what they were for, as a carter goes round a toll by way of the field; and that's worse than breaking it outright, since it's what clever people do." She chewed. "And then you carried it seventeen days, and came down a ridge to tell me so before I'd laid a hand on you. That's the other half of it, and I'll take the two together. It's entered. What's the second?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:31:"There are twelve. You had ten at the fork."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:33:Her jaw stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:35:"The eleventh's the old man's. It came in a stair, under the town, while he held a wall for us on that knee, and he doesn't know. I can't tell him how it came without telling him how all of them came, and that's six people's to give."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:37:"That's a debt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:39:"It's written as one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:41:"And the twelfth?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:43:"There's a man under the registry who reads how a made thing stands: which stone bears, and which is carried. He was showing Karis the inside of it, and I was holding his lamp." He drew breath. "It wasn't taken. It turned round in me, as a sheet is turned on a desk. He's been told, since it was his; he said that it wasn't supposed to be possible, and then he poured me a cup of water."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:45:"What does it do?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:47:"It leans. It'll lean at a table, or a bridge, or a hinge, and I've to stop it looking. It gives one word, and it bills the eyes for an hour afterward. It reads only what somebody made, and he was plain about that. Not a river, and not a tree." He made himself finish. "Not a man."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:49:Oryn had turned on her stone, and she looked at him as she had not yet looked at him that day, the whole of him, from the boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:51:"You've a place in you that doesn't move," she said slowly. "And you've been handed a thing that reads what doesn't move."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:53:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:55:"Have you turned it there?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:57:"No. It offered, on the first afternoon, and I said not until you'd heard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:59:"I've heard." She ate the last of the crust. "I'll tell you what I think, and it's a healer's, as it was before. The man who owns that tool told you what it's not for, and a man generally knows his own tool. That's his word. Here's mine. Tonight I'm going into you as deep as I go, and I can't read a thing that's being stirred while I'm reading it. So for as long as my hands are in you, nothing's turned: not that, and not any of the others."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:61:"And after?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:63:"After, you're on a road, with your own eyes to spend, and I mend what's brought to me. I don't keep people." She got up, stiffly, with a hand on the stone. "But not on my plank."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:67:The miller shut the sluice at dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:69:Cael heard it done from the lean-to: a rattle of chain, and a gate going down, and then the great wheel slowing through three turns, each longer than the last, until it stood. The knocking that had gone on under every word since they came into that valley left off. There was only the race, running by to waste, and the small hiss of the lantern on its pole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:71:She had her things laid out along the shelf, as she had laid them on a sill at Thornwater, and the plank lay across its two kegs. Beyond the lantern's edge a girl sat on the bench's end, against the mill's warm stone, with her knees drawn up. She had not been asked to stay, and it had not come into anybody's head to ask her to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:73:"On your back," said Oryn. "Boots off. You'll want them off before I've done, since I'll be a good while."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:75:He lay down, and the wood was as hard as he remembered it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:77:"You know how it goes, and I'll say it again, since it's the form. You breathe like a man asleep. You don't hold anything shut, and you don't let anything rise, and you don't help me; and if you must move, you say so first." She stood over him with her sleeves pushed up and her thick hands hanging. "I'll talk, since it's how I keep my place. And this time I'm not stopping at twenty minutes. I'll stop when I've been where I'm going, or when there's none of me left to go with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:79:"How long?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:81:"I don't know. I've never gone so far into anybody who wasn't dying." She looked down at him. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:83:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:85:Her left hand slid in under the back of his neck, broad and warm, and took the weight of his head; and her right came down flat upon his breastbone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:87:He counted breaths, since it was the only count that she had left him. A man asleep draws twelve to the minute, and he began at one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:89:It came as it had come once before, on a scrubbed table. It did not go down into him, as his own read went down into the ground; it went along. He felt it leave her right hand somewhere about the thirtieth breath, and set out toward his feet without haste, as a joiner runs a thumb down the edge of a plank to learn whether it is true. It turned where there was no more of him, and came up the other side, and arrived under his neck, and set out again. There was the old hitch in it, a shade late upon his right side; he knew that now for a taped heel, favoured so long that it had got into everything she did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:91:"That's skin," she said, from a long way off. "That's breath. Those are the three that I taped; they've been leaned on since, and they've forgotten it. That's a heart that's had a hard week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:93:He let them go by, and did what she had taught him on that table, which was nothing at all: every door in the house left standing as it happened to stand. He had thought it then the hardest thing that he had ever been set; and he had done it since on a road, on every day, with ten of them and then with twelve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:95:"Now yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:97:She went among them slowly, and named each as she came to it by what her hands met; and he lay in the dark behind his eyelids and heard himself told off like a load on a landing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:99:"That one's quick, and it's hardly got a bank; I know whose it was. That's a deep narrow one. That's the heavy one, the workhorse, down in the soles, and here's something that waits, right through the frame. A hot point, by the left hand. A thing with a lid on it, which I'll not lean on; it's lifted since dusk, and you're keeping it down, and I'll let you, since it's that or have it up." She breathed. "Three lanes, like a ploughed field. A fine thin one that wants a corner to sit in. A short sharp one. And mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:101:He was at the hundred and fortieth breath, and Tide lay in him as still as she stood, since it knew its owner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:103:"That's ten, and I had ten. Now where's the old man's?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:105:He did not answer, since he was not to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:107:"There. I'd have walked past it. It doesn't run at all; it stands in the stream like a post, and everything parts for it. That's eleven." Her hand had not shifted on his chest. "And there's a little flat thing behind your eyes, laid by, like a rule folded on a shelf. It's doing nothing. That'll be the reader's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:109:She was silent for six breaths, and he felt the slow current pass down him once more and return.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:111:"Twelve," she said. "I'll not ask how many more you mean to bring me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:115:"Now the middle," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:117:It was the two hundred and sixth breath. He had been on that plank for seventeen minutes, and she had only just finished with what he carried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:119:He felt her go to it, as she had gone once before. Her current came up his left side toward the hollow under the breastbone; and where it should have passed through, it parted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:121:He had not had a picture for that until three days ago, and he had one now. He had stood on a bridge and counted piers so that he need not look at them, and he had seen what a brown river does at a cutwater. It does not stop there, and it does not go in. It divides upon the stone, and runs down either side, and joins again below; and a person on the bank can tell the whole shape of the pier by nothing but the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:123:"There," she said, very low. "It's as it was. Everything goes about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:125:She did not leave it, this time. She had come as far as this before, and had said what she met, and gone out again; tonight she stayed. He felt her send the slow limping current round it on the left hand, and then on the right, and then, which she had never done, round it from above and from beneath. She went about that still place as a person goes about a house which has every shutter closed, trying each wall in turn with the flat of a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:127:"There's no bank," she said. "It's not a bank that stops me. There's nothing there to push on. I come up to it, and then I'm past."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:129:He drew the two hundred and fortieth breath. That was twenty minutes, and she had said that she would not stop at twenty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:131:"It's not small. I'd thought that it was small."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:133:She went round, and round again. Her voice had sunk to almost nothing. The heel of her hand on his breastbone eased and settled with each passing, and he lay beneath it and kept every door as it stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:135:It was at about the three hundredth breath that the twelfth turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:137:He had not bidden it, or so much as thought of it, since he had been counting, and the count was all of him that was awake. The twelfth had lain where she said, a little flat thing behind his eyes, folded; for seven days it had leaned at whatever a hand had made, at pegs and rivets and the piers of a bridge, and each time he had given his eyes some other work, and it had come up short.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:139:His eyes were shut now, and there was nothing that he could give them to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:141:And something was being drawn in him, under her hands, which had never been drawn before. It was an outline. Her current went round the place on every side, and round again; and the twelfth came about to that, mildly, as a head comes round in a dark room toward the only sound in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:143:It did not look outward. It leaned in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:145:*No*, he began to say; and he found that he could not say it without saying it with the whole of himself. A refusal was a holding, and she would feel a jaw go hard. She had felt it once, at the hundred and eighth breath, on another table, and had told him that he was helping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:147:He could not shut that door, and he was not to open it, and so he left it as it stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:149:The twelfth leaned, for the space of three breaths, at the middle of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:151:*Still.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:153:There was no voice in it; it was only there, as the weight of a stone is there in the hand that lifts it. It was not *nothing*. He knew how the twelfth said that: flatly, of a red square of tile, or of a table's idle leg. It was not *sits*, which it said of a page. It was a word that he had never had of it before; and he had used that very word himself, in a log, for longer than he had owned the thing which now said it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:155:Her current made one more round, coming up from beneath, and parted, and joined.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:157:And the lean came down flat, as a foot arrives upon a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:159:*Set.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:161:His heart struck once, hard, against the heel of a healer's hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:163:He let the breath out slowly, like a man asleep, to the count, and he drew the next. He had stood on a river bar with a hound's teeth in the sack on his arm, and had not answered it, and that had been easier than this. He kept his jaw loose, and he kept his two hands lying open on the plank, with the palms upward, and did not let one finger of them close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:165:*Set*: as a stone is set, or a stake. He knew three places where he had met that word. The eleventh had come into him in a stair, not as a filling, but as a post is stood in its hole; a small pale woman had found the word under her own glove, when she had wished for a name; and it stood in the fourth line of a list on the back of a skin, hung level with the people at a kindling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:167:There was a next word. He could feel the shape of it, as a tongue feels the gap where a tooth has been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:169:He did not let the next word come. He put it down, as he had seen an old man put down a sentence in a gallery, with one hand off the stick; and he lay under a healer's palm and went on with his count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:171:"Something turned over," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:173:It was a murmur, from very far away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:175:"Not in the middle. Out at the edge of you. One of yours, in its sleep." Her hand had not moved. "Leave it. It's nothing to me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:177:He left it. He was at three hundred and eleven, and he had never in his life lain so still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:181:"That's forty minutes," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:183:She was on her feet yet, a pace from the plank, with both hands hanging. The count of his breaths lay behind him, a little short of five hundred, and Cael had sat up without knowing that he did it. Lira had come off the bench; she stood at the lean-to's post with a cup of water from the race, held out, which had not been taken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:185:"I said that I'd go as deep as I went, and that's it. There's a floor to me, and I've had both feet on it this quarter-hour. You'll want it plain, and I've only the one way of giving it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:187:The lantern had begun to hurt him a little at the edges, already, and he looked at the turf of the roof instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:189:"There's a place in you I can't read because it isn't *flow*. I've spent my life reading flow. Whatever that is, it's not the kind of thing my Path was built for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:191:The race ran by in the dark, and nobody said anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:193:Then she felt behind her for the keg, and sat on it heavily, and took the cup. He had seen her grey, and he had not seen her so. Her face in the lantern was the colour of the ash in a dead ring, and her cropped head hung; and it came to him that the forty minutes had not done the whole of that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:195:He had heard her say before that a thing was past her hands. She had said it on a hillside, of a girl's arm; and she had told him afterward that when she said so he was to believe her, and not stand looking at her as though she had kept something back. He believed her. She had gone round that place for the better part of half an hour, at every height, with all that she had, and she had not come out and said that it was nothing. She had said what she was, and where she ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:197:It had cost her to say it. A person who has read every body that was ever laid before her, for thirty rounds of a country, had stood in front of a boy and a girl and told them the size of her own tool, and had only then sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:199:It was a whole finding, and it was hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:201:He had another. It lay behind his eyes in two words, and the two findings did not say the same thing. Hers said that there was no flow in that place for a Tide to feel. His said that something stood there which had been put.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:203:He looked at the roof, and kept his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:205:It was not the first thing that he had kept back. He had kept a date from everybody, at a spring, and a warning. But he had never before kept a thing from the person whose hands had drawn it out, with those hands hardly cold upon him; and he found that it had a weight which the others had not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:207:"That's all?" said Lira. She was not asking him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:209:"It's all that I've a right to. I could dress it for you. I could say that it's a stone, or a knot, or a door; I thought all three on a road north, between one gate and the next, and they're none of them true." Oryn rubbed her eyes with the heel of her hand. "I know what I read, and I know when I'm not reading. I'll not make a guess sound like a finding because a boy's come a long road for one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:211:"I didn't come for one," said Cael. "I came to hear yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:213:"Then you've heard it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:215:Lira had not moved from the post. She had her chin up, and Cael knew what was coming, since he knew that girl. A thing had been asked for at a fork-stone, in so many words, and she had carried the asking for eighteen days as she would have carried a count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:217:"You said all of it," said Lira. "At the fork. Whatever walked out of that archive. There's a paper in his coat that did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:219:"Is it a finding?" Oryn had not lifted her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:221:"It's a leaf," Lira said. "It's a thing that somebody wrote down, which one of us can read, and the rest of us had to be handed. It's not about a body. I'll not tell you that it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:223:"Then it's not mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:225:It was said without any heat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:227:"I've a plank and two hands. What goes on that plank is what I can put the hands on, and say afterward that I felt it. I worked in a house once where a sheet was hung at the foot of every bed, with what somebody had written of the person in it; and it was never once the patient." She drank. "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:229:The paper stayed where it was. Cael had put his hand to his coat when the girl began, and he took it away again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:231:He was not sorry. She had drawn a line round her own table; and it was the same line that a small pale woman had drawn on a desk with a bound hand: what a person had found went on one page, and what a person had been told went on another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:233:"Go to bed," said Oryn. "The pair of you. I'm going to sit here until I can stand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:235:Her eyes came up to Cael as he got off the plank; and they were as level as a board, tired as she was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:237:"Lie still when I say. That's still the term."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:241:His eyes began to pay before he was across the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:243:It came as it had come on a stair under a registry: first a dryness, and then sand, as though somebody had thrown a handful of the road into his face. By the door of the cart-shed the mill's one lantern had a ring round it, like a moon in mist, and he did not look at it twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:245:They had been given the cart-shed, with last year's hay thrown down between the wheels. Ten people lay in it, and a big man's breathing went up and down at the far end like a slow saw. He found his place by his hands, and lay down on his back with his arm across his face, and let the water run.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:247:It ran for a long time. He had not turned the twelfth; it had turned of itself, and he had not looked at anything, with his eyes or without them. It billed him all the same, to the grain, as a toll-keeper charges a cart that was pushed through his gate by somebody else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:249:"You're awake," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:251:She was a yard from him in the dark, and by her voice she had not lain down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:253:"Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:255:"You'd not look at her lantern. You looked at the roof for the whole time that she was telling you, and you came across that yard like a man going by a forge. And I can hear the water going now." Her voice was quite low. "I had you by the sleeve across a vault, seven days ago, with your lids shut. I know what it is that bills them. It only charges when it's looked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:257:He did not answer at once. He could not have told that girl a false thing, and there was no need to; he had only to find the true one that was his to give.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:259:"Something came," he said. "I didn't ask for it, and I've not got a name for it that I'd put my hand to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:261:"Is it bad?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:263:"I don't know what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:265:"That's twice now that you've given me a clerk's answer and looked sorry about it." The hay rustled; she had put her back against a wheel. "I'll not dig. I dug at you all the time that we were on a bluff, and all I ever turned up was what you'd have told me in a week if I'd let you alone. You'll tell me when it's a thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:267:"On the road."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:269:"If there's road," said Lira, and he heard her lie down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:271:He listened until her breathing had gone long. She had asked him a thing, and had been given less than the whole of it, and had said that she would wait; and he did not know many people who could do the third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:273:He could not see to write. He took the book out and wrote by feel, as he had on a moor, with the pencil's butt against his thumb to keep one line from running into the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:275:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day, night. Millrace; the cart-shed. A hundred and twenty-seven. Told Oryn, before she read me: the two readings at the brook and the post, and why; the eleventh; the twelfth. Then she read me, asked first, for forty minutes, as deep as she goes. Her finding is hers. I have it word for word, and I am not copying it here, since she can say it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:277:*One thing more came, under her hands, that nobody asked for. I am not writing what. Reasons, in order.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:279:*One. It was not bidden. She said that nothing was to be turned while she was in. I turned nothing, and it turned. I'll not hand a healer the thing that broke her term and call it a finding.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:281:*Two. It is one look, by a tool that was never meant to look at a man. A count that one hand has made is a hope, and I have nothing to lay it against.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:283:*Three. What she found is whole. If I set a second word beside it tonight, it stops being what she found, and becomes half of what two people think.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:285:*Four is not mine to write.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:287:*Ruling: hold. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:289:He shut the book on the pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:291:The fourth lay in him where it had lain for seven days. A man with red eyes had poured a cup of water, under a town, and had said what he was to do if the thing ever read something that ought not to be made, and read it all the same. He was not to tell that man, and he was not to tell anybody. He was only to remember that it had been said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:293:He had remembered, and it had been easy, for a week, since it had been a sentence about a day that might never come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:295:It had come at about the three hundredth breath, on a plank, beside a stopped wheel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:297:He lay with his arm across his eyes, and he did not go back to the place. He had been told once what a person does with a sore spot that he has found, and he kept his hands off it. But the sentence had moved. It no longer stood upright, where he had put it; it had begun to lean, like every row on a sheet that somebody had meant, and he knew which way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:301:They forded with the first of the light, on the two hundred and ninety-ninth day, and the woman on the flat stone counted them across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:303:She had a good deal to count, and Seln stood on the far bank, where he had been before anybody else was awake, and did it with her, by the heads.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:305:"Twelve," he said. "And a mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:307:There were the six who had come down a border road: a quiet man, and a girl, and a big one whose arms came up as far as his shoulders that morning, and a small pale woman, and a boy with a book, and an old man on a stick. There were four from the house on the rise. A captain had his right wrist wrapped in linen under the leather. A tall man carried his spear upright, and a broad one eased his weight onto the right leg in the water; and a boy went over last, with his eyes on a girl's feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:309:There was a lean old woman with a staff and a board, who was on nobody's count and had said so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:311:And there was the healer. She came down to the ford leading the brown mule on its rope, with her one pannier across the withers and a rolled strip of canvas; and she stopped at the water's edge and looked at Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:313:"She'll carry three," said Oryn. "She's carried them before. Put them on the low side first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:315:Brom lifted the boxes himself. He did it one at a time, slowly, with both hands, to the height of that brown back, and he looked at his own two arms after each as a man looks at a pair of tools that he had given up for lost. The mule stood for it with her ears back. When the third was on, with the flat strapped over all and a sack of the mill's meal for a counterweight, he laid his palm on her neck for the space of a breath; and it was the first time in twelve days that he had been able to reach it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:317:"That's a healer's road and a researcher's whole archive on one beast," he said. "I'd not care to be the mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:319:"She's had worse company," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:321:"Twenty," said the old man, to nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:323:"Of thirty," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:325:"I am obliged." Vastin considered the far bank, and the track that climbed away from it toward a notch. "I am informed that I am to walk a stride behind a man who is slow to answer. I find it the first posting in some years which I understand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:327:Karis had not crossed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:329:She was on the near bank, kneeling at the flat stone that the wheels aimed for, with the calfskin spread on it under four pebbles; and she had pinned a clean sheet to the skin's eastern edge with two thorns out of the hedge. The Ternhall brass lay along the pencil line, through four rings, and ran on over the join.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:331:"I have made the office's figure again," she said, when he came back through the water to her. "By daylight, with nobody talking, and it is what it was." She held out her right hand. "I shall want them once more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:333:He gave her the brass legs, shut, with the cork on. She opened them upon the hall's ring and the fourth, as she had done at a table; she closed them by a hair, and swung one point round along the strip, and it came down on the clean paper. She pressed, and it left a prick in the sheet no bigger than a grain of sand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:335:Then she took the pencil in her left hand, the bound one, in its new linen, and drew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:337:She began in the middle of the fourth ring, where the carter's skin ended, and the line went over the join of hide and paper without a tremor. It ran along the brass's edge to the prick, and it did not stop there. She carried it on, a hand's breadth beyond, to where the sheet gave out, and lifted the pencil only at the paper's edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:339:"You've gone past it," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:341:"I do not know that it ends at the point. I know that an office measured as far as that, and wrote a figure. I am not going to draw a stop that the paper does not give me." She blew the lead off the sheet. "It is a line. It goes where it goes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:343:She gave him back the dividers, and he put them into the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:345:They went up out of the green valley in the file that had been set under an ash, with a mule and two healers in the middle of it. At the notch's head the ruts divided. Teague did not look along the pair that went back to a plank and a cloth. He lifted two fingers, and the tall man turned his spear the other way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:347:Cael walked at a captain's shoulder, a stride back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:349:He had a healer's finding in him, word for word, and it was whole; he had two words of his own behind his eyes, which did not agree with it, and which he had told to nobody. He had a sentence from under a town that had begun to lean. Ahead of him a quiet man went small into the pale grass, along a line that a woman had ruled past the place where the figures stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:351:It ran east, off the edge of everything that she had. He went where it went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:1:# Chapter 41 — The List of Parts
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:3:"Fifteen," said the old man, from the mounting block, before anybody had wished him a good morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:5:It was the two hundred and ninety-fourth day, and the yard was grey, with the ashes of a fire in an iron basket and eight bowls stacked on a board beside the pump. He had got himself out of the stall on the stick and one shoulder. The tarred case lay across his thighs already. It had lain in the straw at his elbow on every night since he came down a ladder on a rope; nobody had given it into his keeping, and nobody had asked for it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:7:"Of thirty," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:9:"I am obliged. I find that I like it said by a second person."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:11:Cael had slept, which surprised him. He had said a thing on his feet beside that basket, with six faces lit from below, and afterward he had ruled a line in a book and written nothing under it; and he had supposed that it would keep him awake. It had not. It lay in him this morning where it had been set down, like a box that has been carried into a room and is no longer on anybody's back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:13:There was a day's work between him and a gate, and he was glad of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:15:The card for it said four, in the hand of a man who had sat on a bucket with his eyes shut. Brom ran the handcart out under the arch. They went up the hill as they had gone on three mornings, with a big man leaning into the shafts and an old one riding backward on the paper; the keepers in blue counted one, and four, and tore their corner, and the old keeper at the round arch asked whether the lame one meant to make a habit of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:17:"I am told that it is the last time," said Vastin, to the cobbles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:19:In the lane a girl kept the square's side of him at each of four alleys. On the carts' street he looked at the roofs. He knew by now exactly how a promise sat in the neck. He knew, too, that this was the last morning on which he would have to keep it, since by the same hour tomorrow he would be on the far side of a river, where it did not run.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:21:The clerk wrote *The same*, and *four*, and blotted them, as he had on every morning but the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:23:Then there was the iron, and the twenty-seven, with an old man going down them on his seat and a boot's heel in Cael's palm at every tread. At the stair's foot the lamp's ring was hung on his fingers, without a word, by somebody who did not look at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:25:The skin lay on the slope of the desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:27:It had been taken out from under its jar before they came. The upper board of beech stood against the rock; and the man in the dusty coat was at the desk with the lower board in one hand and a third, which Cael had not seen before, in the other, like a cook who means to turn a cake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:29:"I'll not put a finger on it," he said. "Nor a reed. It's lain on that side since before I was born, and I don't know what the under side of it will bear."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:31:He laid the one board over the leaf, and slid the other beneath, and turned the whole between his two flat hands. Then he lifted away what was now the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:33:Cael looked, since the lamp was in his hand and the lamp went where the looking went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:35:The back was paler than the face. No dark stuff had been rubbed into it, or none was left, so that the scored lines showed only as a low sun shows a footprint, by their shadow. There were not many. He counted nine, each of them short, standing one under another down the left side like a column of figures. Beside the fourth, a little below the middle, the skin was thin and rough, and the grain of the beech showed through it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:37:He knew that place. It was the back of the one that a knife had been to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:39:The thing behind his eyes had come round, as it came round to every joint and peg in that gallery, and it leaned, mildly, at nine scratched lines that somebody had ruled first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:41:*No*, he told it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:43:It was easier than it had been. He had been caught by it three times in four days, at a rope-walk and a rivet and a table's leg, and each time he had learned a little more of how it was refused. He did not push it down, as he pushed Wind. He gave his eyes something of his own to do. So he counted the uprights of black oak between the desk and the nearest bay, which were four, as they had been yesterday; and the thing leaned at nothing, and no grit came.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:45:"Lamp," said the rough voice. "Left. Lower than that; it's not inked. I'll want the light nearly along the skin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:47:He knelt, and laid the flame almost level with the desk's slope, a hand to the left, so that every scratch on that leaf threw its small shadow to the right. The man bent to it from the foot, and the water began to run from his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:49:"Nine," he said. "And it's a list."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:53:It took the whole of the forenoon to learn how little a list gives away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:55:Karis had the second chair, with a board across her knees and her notebook open upon the board. She read the first line through in the time that it takes to lace a boot; she had every one of the twenty-three letters now, and a hand that had pressed like a mason's did not blur. Then she sat looking at what she had written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:57:"I can spell it," she said. "I can very nearly say it aloud, as a child says a word in a tongue that it has not been taught. It is a noun, and then a number, and the number I can read." Her pencil tapped. "The noun stands on the face as well. It is the first of the four things that he says were made."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:59:"And the second line?" said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:61:"The second of the four, and a number. The third and the fourth are the same." She looked down the column. "After those there are five that the face does not name. I have their letters, and I have not one of their meanings. A tally-book counts cattle, and hides, and loads of stone; whoever kept those books had no occasion to write down the parts of a thing like this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:63:"So it's a bill," said Brom. He had come up the gallery with an empty box on his shoulder. "So many of this, so many of that. I've had them nailed to a crate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:65:"It is a list of parts, with their count. I shall call it that, since it is what I can see." She did not look up. "I am not going to tell you the numbers. They will be on my page tonight, in words; but a number without its noun is the best bait for guessing that was ever laid, and I have four people in this gallery who can guess."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:67:"Five," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:69:"I had not counted myself. I am the worst of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:71:Cleon had not sat down. He had fetched a sheet of his own from under the desk's slope, a square of the stiff stock that his cards were cut from, and a pen; and Cael saw, with some surprise, that he meant to write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:73:He did not read as she read. He set his pen at the foot of the first line and drew what was there, sign for sign, as a joiner copies a moulding, without once asking what it said; and wherever a run came down flat he made a small tick beneath it, in red, from a second pot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:75:"She'll take hers away, and it'll be right," he said, to the skin. "And in a month she'll want to know whether a stroke was the writer's or the knife's, and she'll be three hundred miles off. So there's to be one that's drawn and not read. That's mine. It goes with her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:77:"I had not asked it of you," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:79:"No. You'd not have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:81:That was all, and they went on, two pens at one leaf. There was a quick small hand that turned a dead tongue into words, and a slow square one that turned it into nothing and kept its shape. Cael held the flame along the skin for both, and moved it up a finger as each line was done. He did not need to be told. He had known for three days where the red eyes would go next, and he kept his own upon his knuckles while he knew it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:83:Behind him the tables were being stripped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:85:The fifty-two sheets had lain there for eleven days in three heaps, as they had lain upon three floors. They were to go up at the bell, and a clerk was to write that they were gone. Lira lifted them one at a time, flat on her two forearms, the slow arm and the quick, while Brom held each box's lid. On the third box, six strides from the desk, sat the old man, with his bound leg along a plank. He had the tarred case for a desk, and a leaf of Karis's notebook upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:87:She had given it to him herself, at the stair's foot, with a pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:89:"You drafted an order at a spring," she had said. "Each row to be taken up singly; a schedule to be made of every sheet as it is turned; and the schedule to be signed by two hands. I told you that it was quite a good order, and that I had nobody to sign the second half." She had held out the leaf. "I have somebody now. I should like them counted into their boxes by a person who has done it before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:91:He had looked at the pencil for a good while before he took it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:93:"The Fallow's, eleven," said Lira now, from the first table. "That's the last of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:95:"Eleven," said Vastin, and made his stroke. "I have eleven. The box may be shut." He turned his head a little, toward the big man with the lid. "I should be obliged if it were said back to me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:97:"Eleven," said Brom. "And I never thought to be asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:99:Cael heard the pencil go down again, and he did not turn round. He had seen that man stopped at a spring in the middle of an order, by nothing more than a small grey person's eyes. It seemed to him a large thing, and one that nobody in the gallery was going to remark upon, that the same order had now been asked for; and it was being carried out to the letter, with a stub of pencil on a kit's case, by the man who had not been let to give it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:103:They ate their bread at noon by the nearest bay. The man went up for his bowl, and came down, and sat his hour with his eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:105:It was after the hour that they came to the fourth line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:107:Karis had left it. She had gone from the third to the fifth without a word, as a person steps over a broken tread, and had done the five that she could not turn; and only when the ninth was written did she bring her pencil back up the column to the place where the skin was thin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:109:"I cannot do this with a glove," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:111:She drew the right one off. Then she looked at her left hand, which lay on the board's edge in its strip of a big man's shirt, and drew a breath through her nose; and she put the pencil into it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:113:"That's sewn," said Brom, from the tables.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:115:"It is sewn across the heel. I do not write with the heel." She had the brass strip from Ternhall in her right hand now, and she laid it along the line, to keep her eye from wandering into the one above. "I have two hands, and one line that wants both of them. I shall be slow. I should be obliged if nobody watched."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:117:Everybody watched. Cael saw the linen darken a very little, at the palm's edge, before she had made three signs; and he saw that she had seen it, and had gone on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:119:The line was a bad one. Somebody had scraped a word from the leaf's face, long ago, and had borne down to do it; and where the edge had gone, the skin was no thicker than the film on a pan of milk. What had been scored on the back at that place had lost its depth. A stroke that lay a hair deep on either side of it was there no more than the scratch that a nail leaves on a slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:121:"There's the writer's," said Cleon. The reed was in his fingers, an inch above the leaf. "And there's the knife's, coming through from the other side. They cross. You'll want to tell which is which before you call any of it a letter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:123:"I know that I shall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:125:"The first of it's sound. It's the word you had on the face, for the mark." He did not say it as she would have said it; he said it as a man names a beam. "That's what bears, and there's only the one. Two runs hang from it." The reed moved. "One there, of five. One there, of six, and that's the one in the thin. And after the pair of them, his number."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:127:"*Three*," said Karis. "I have the number."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:129:"They hang level. I'll tell you that for nothing, since you can't see it and I can. He's not put the one above the other. Whatever those two are, the mark's set over both the same."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:131:On a box, six strides off, a pencil had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:133:Cael did not look round. He looked at his knuckles, and at the flame, and he heard an old man decline to finish a sentence for the second time in two days; there was no sound in it but breath, let go slowly, and then the small scrape of a stroke made on a tally. It appeared that the count of the Stair's had come to twenty-nine, and that nobody at the desk had heard anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:135:"The first run I can read," said Karis. She was writing with the bound hand, very small, and her lips were white. "It is the people at a kindling, as the face has them. The second run is six signs, and it is in the thin; and of the six I am certain of three."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:137:"Which?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:139:"The first, and the fourth, and the last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:141:"I'd have given you the first and the last. I'd not have sworn to your fourth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:143:He had straightened. He rubbed his eyes with the heel of his hand, and bent again, to his own square of card, where the same line stood in his own slow drawing. He looked from it to hers, and he was a long time about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:145:"Your hand's better than mine on the fourth line," said Cleon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:147:He took up his pen, and struck his drawing of that line through with one stroke, and held the card out along the slope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:149:"Put it in on mine. As you've got it, and no better. Where you're not sure, leave me the scratch, and don't make a letter of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:151:She took the card. She looked at him for a breath; and it was the first time in eleven days that anybody in that gallery had seen her at a loss for the small exact thing to say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:153:"It is three signs of six," she said at last. "I could have five by supper, if I let myself. I have a word in my head that would fit the three like a key." Her eyes had gone down to the thin place. "I had a word in my head once before, under a glove, and it was the wrong one. I shall not tell you what this one is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:155:"Good," said Cleon. "Then I'll not have to forget it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:159:The bell went with Cleon's finger on the last line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:161:It had been there some time. He had drawn all nine, and had gone back to the foot of the ninth, to see whether it came down flat or was only broken off by the skin's edge. He stood so, bent over the slope, with the water running from his eyes and one square finger in the air above the last scratch on that leaf, when the wire stirred at the stair's foot and the small flat stroke came up the gallery.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:163:"It ends," he said. "He finished. There's no tenth that's been cut away." He straightened. "That's the one thing that I've been sure of since noon."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:165:Nobody hurried. The boxes stood ready by the cross-way, three of them, roped, with a tally lying on the lid of the third under a stub of pencil. It had two hands' marks at its foot, a K. and a square upright V. Cael had seen the old man look at his own letter after he had made it, as at a thing that he had not expected to be asked for again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:167:The leaf went back upon its face. The man turned it between the boards as he had turned it in the morning, and laid the upper board home, and set the jar upon the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:169:Then he held out his hand, behind him, to nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:171:It was not for the lamp to be moved, and Cael understood that a breath late. The hand was open, with the palm upward, and it waited; and he put the brass ring into it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:173:The fingers shut. The man took his own lamp up, for the first time in ten days, and the thumb shifted on the ring, of itself, until the flame hung a hand to the left of the thing that he was about to look at, which was the floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:175:He had not looked at the boy. He had not done so since he poured a cup of water and set it on a table's corner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:177:Cael got up off his knee, which had a callus on it now, like a tiler's. He had been a lamp in that gallery since the third hour of the second day. He had been told *left*, and *up a finger*, by a man who did not know his name and had never asked what he was; and it came to him, standing there with his hand empty, that it was the only work he had ever done inside a wall which nobody had written down. No form had a line for it. He had held a light where it was wanted, and had learned which way the looking went; and a week of another person's whole life's work had been done by it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:179:He found that he would have liked to say so. He did not. He had been looked away from for his own sake, and the least that he could do was to let it be done well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:181:The old man had asked for his quarter of an hour again, and had been given it. They heard him above them on the second flight when they came to the stair's foot with the boxes: two hands set down, and a breath, and a scrape. He was at the head when they reached it, sitting against the iron with the case in his arms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:183:"Twenty-seven," he said. "Unassisted. I mention it for the tally."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:185:"It's entered," said Lira. "Shift your leg."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:187:Quick flat feet came up behind them, with a brown light. The man drew the bar. He did not swing the iron at once. He stood with his hand on it, in the narrow place at the stair's head, with five people and three boxes pressed along the wall; and he held out a square of card-stock, rolled, with a turn of brown tape about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:189:Karis took it in her right hand. The left she kept against her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:191:"I do not know how to enter this week," she said. "I have been trying since the yard. I have a page for what the leaf says, and a page that is clean. Neither of them has a line for the man who gave me every join in fifty-two sheets, and would not hear what I thought they were. I shall have to write something at the head; and I should like it to be a thing that you would put your name to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:193:"Work. Not a finding."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:195:"That is not enough."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:197:"It's what it was. I stood where I've stood for thirty years, and said which way a thing leaned. You found out what it said." The red eyes were on the roll of card in her hand. "A finding's yours. You'll have to carry it up into the light and be believed, and I've told you what I think of that. Work's a thing that a man can go back down to in the morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:199:"Then I shall write *work*," said Karis. Her voice was not quite steady. "And I shall write whose."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:201:"Write what you like. It's been on worse."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:203:He put his shoulder to the iron, and it swung inward on the vault, where a clerk's lantern waited among the pillars. She had gone a step over the sill when he spoke again, to her back, in the voice like a hinge; and it was drier than Cael had ever heard it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:205:"Mind the stair. It leans."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:207:She stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:209:They had come up the only stair in sight, all twenty-seven steps of it, and it lay behind them in the dark. Cael watched her take that in, and then watched her take in the rest. There had been a row of signs on a sheet, with each one hung from the one before; and a man had said that he did not read such a thing, but was walked down it. She had fifty-two sheets of that in three boxes, and she was about to walk them east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:211:"I shall mind it," she said, and did not turn round. "Thank you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:213:The iron went to behind them, and they heard the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:217:The clerk had his green book open on the hatch's ledge, and for once he did not know what to write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:219:Cael watched him find it. The pen had set down *the same* on that leaf day after day, and tonight the same was not so. The tables below were bare. Three boxes stood upon his paving which would not come back in the morning, and there was no card for the morning in anybody's hand. His eyes went along the stones at the height of a boot, and found seven that stood and one that hung. Then they went back to the form.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:221:He wrote slowly. *Withdrawn by its bearer*, said the long column; and the narrow one said *four*. He blotted both. Then, with the pen still lifted, he ruled a line beneath the whole of it, across both columns, as a man rules off an account that has come out even.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:223:"He's shut us," said Lira, under her breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:225:"He's glad to," said Brom. "Look at his shoulders. That's a man who'll sleep tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:227:The clerk had asked them one thing in eleven days, and he did not ask a second. But when the key had turned in the low door he stood a moment on its sill, with his book against his chest, and looked along the dock at an old man being got onto a handcart. His mouth opened a very little, as it had on the first morning. Then he shut it, and went in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:229:Seln was at the painted pole, with a hired frame at his feet. He fell in at the cart's tail, like a porter who has been paid to, and said what he had to say to the setts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:231:"There was nobody on the kerb, and nobody in the smith's door. I have been down to the quay and looked at the bridge from below. It is one cart wide." He walked a few strides. "A person who wished to see us leave this town need not follow us about it. He need only stand at one end of that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:233:"Then he'll see six people and some boxes," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:235:"He will see how many, and which way. It is all that I should want, in his place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:237:The yard was nearly dark when they had the cart swept. Brom did it himself, with a birch broom from the stable, and stood the shafts against the wall as he had found them. Then he looked at the three boxes in their row, and at his own two arms, which hung.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:239:"I can carry mine, on the right, as I have," he said. "She's two, and so's he. That's the boxes. She's the flat and the hide." He nodded toward the mounting block. "And there's twelve miles of that road before the first hedge ends, and a hill after it, and I've nothing to put under him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:241:"I shall walk for as long as I am able," said Vastin. "I shall say when I am not. I undertake that much, and I do not undertake a yard more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:243:"That's no use to me. I want to know where I'll be standing when you say it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:245:"There's carts on that road," said Lira. "We met thirty."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:247:"Going in. I'll ask at the walk in the morning, when I'm there for the rope. A man who sells cordage knows who's hauling." Brom rubbed his jaw with the back of his wrist. "It's not a mule. I'd give a good deal for the mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:249:Lira had her left arm out before her, and was turning the hand over and back in the last of the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:251:"Twenty-nine," she said. "She told me a month of slow. I've counted every one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:253:He looked at it with her. It was thinner than the right, still, all down the forearm, and its eight marks were pale; but it turned without a hitch, and when she shut the fist there was no tremor in it that he could see. Twenty-nine days ago it had come out of a sling, and could do no more than hold a boot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:255:Cael went up the ladder, and sat down against the third box, and wrote by the lantern.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:257:*Two hundred and ninety-fourth day. Treswick; the loft, and one in the stall; the last night of both. A hundred and thirty. The back of the leaf: a list, nine lines, a thing and its number to each. Two copies, hers and the reader's, and his goes with her. The fourth line is the mark's; of its last six signs, three are sure. Fifty-two sheets counted into three boxes under two hands, K. and V. The clerk wrote* withdrawn*. Vastin: fifteen of thirty, and the stair alone. Karis wrote with the sewn hand. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:259:*A lamp, ten days. It is entered nowhere but here.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:261:He read that last over, and let it stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:263:In the morning there was a gate, and a bridge on five piers, and a road that ran twelve miles between hedges to the foot of a rise; and somewhere beyond that rise, a long way north and east, a square woman was coming down a far water toward a mill. She had a brown mule on a rope, which would have carried three boxes and thought nothing of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:265:She was four days off, and there was still no mule.
NOTED
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:173:"Noted," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:401:"Noted," Vastin said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:93:"Noted," Cael said.
MONEY DAYS COUNTS
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:5:He saw them at the third mile on the two hundred and ninety-sixth morning: a scatter of grey backs on the swell ahead, with their heads down in the bents and the east wind laying their wool over. There had been none on that ground sixteen days ago. A flock is not turned out where eight things lie up round a hole in the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:195:*Two hundred and ninety-sixth day. The halting-ground in the fold; thirteen miles. The cut walked east. Water in it, and no glass, and sheep on the top. Vastin walked it: seventeen of thirty. He stood on the leg once, with the stick off the ground, where the line had been. The factor offered twenty at each first frost. Brom said no. A hundred and twenty-eight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:199:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:203:It was noon of the two hundred and ninety-seventh day when the road came down through the alders, and the river lay beyond them, wide and brown, with the cable swung across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:5:It was the two hundred and ninety-fourth day, and the yard was grey, with the ashes of a fire in an iron basket and eight bowls stacked on a board beside the pump. He had got himself out of the stall on the stick and one shoulder. The tarred case lay across his thighs already. It had lain in the straw at his elbow on every night since he came down a ladder on a rope; nobody had given it into his keeping, and nobody had asked for it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:239:"I can carry mine, on the right, as I have," he said. "She's two, and so's he. That's the boxes. She's the flat and the hide." He nodded toward the mounting block. "And there's twelve miles of that road before the first hedge ends, and a hill after it, and I've nothing to put under him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:257:*Two hundred and ninety-fourth day. Treswick; the loft, and one in the stall; the last night of both. A hundred and thirty. The back of the leaf: a list, nine lines, a thing and its number to each. Two copies, hers and the reader's, and his goes with her. The fourth line is the mark's; of its last six signs, three are sure. Fifty-two sheets counted into three boxes under two hands, K. and V. The clerk wrote* withdrawn*. Vastin: fifteen of thirty, and the stair alone. Karis wrote with the sewn hand. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:263:In the morning there was a gate, and a bridge on five piers, and a road that ran twelve miles between hedges to the foot of a rise; and somewhere beyond that rise, a long way north and east, a square woman was coming down a far water toward a mill. She had a brown mule on a rope, which would have carried three boxes and thought nothing of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:9:It was the two hundred and ninety-fifth morning past the Line. They had lain thirteen nights in that loft, and Cael had not once gone in at the painted door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:23:"A hundred and twenty-eight in hand," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:129:So he had an old man in front of him, riding backward, for the whole of twelve miles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:181:Cael felt the twelve of them settle, as hounds settle at a threshold that they know. Wind had his feet already. The read asked, civilly, what the ruts weighed. He went round the house and shut the doors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:223:Nobody had spoken since the hedge. He had forgotten in thirteen days how loud that silence was. A town's noise is a blanket; a road's quiet is a room with every door open, and a person who carries twelve things that would each like to answer has the minding of all the doors. The quietest of them would lift at dusk. He could feel where it lay, as a person feels where the cold place is in a bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:241:She had not meant to, and Cael saw her check it. There had been a slab at that spring, twelve feet long and waist-high, lying level on its own rubble in the lee of the rock. She had called it a table. She had laid six years of an office's paper out upon it under river pebbles, and nothing had stirred but a corner now and then by the width of a finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:283:He went, and sat down against the third box, and took out Hesk's book. The brass legs slid from it into his lap, shut, with the cork upon their points. He had carried them for three days and had not once opened them upon a distance. A ruling of his own stood in the way, and it was a hundred and twenty-seven days old. Whatever measured had read one way in four hands and another in his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:291:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:31:"There are twelve. You had ten at the fork."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:87:He counted breaths, since it was the only count that she had left him. A man asleep draws twelve to the minute, and he began at one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:93:He let them go by, and did what she had taught him on that table, which was nothing at all: every door in the house left standing as it happened to stand. He had thought it then the hardest thing that he had ever been set; and he had done it since on a road, on every day, with ten of them and then with twelve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:111:"Twelve," she said. "I'll not ask how many more you mean to bring me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:275:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day, night. Millrace; the cart-shed. A hundred and twenty-seven. Told Oryn, before she read me: the two readings at the brook and the post, and why; the eleventh; the twelfth. Then she read me, asked first, for forty minutes, as deep as she goes. Her finding is hers. I have it word for word, and I am not copying it here, since she can say it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:287:*Ruling: hold. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:301:They forded with the first of the light, on the two hundred and ninety-ninth day, and the woman on the flat stone counted them across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:305:"Twelve," he said. "And a mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:315:Brom lifted the boxes himself. He did it one at a time, slowly, with both hands, to the height of that brown back, and he looked at his own two arms after each as a man looks at a pair of tools that he had given up for lost. The mule stood for it with her ears back. When the third was on, with the flat strapped over all and a sack of the mill's meal for a counterweight, he laid his palm on her neck for the space of a breath; and it was the first time in twelve days that he had been able to reach it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:115:"*Clearance; the cut below the shoulder hold. Eight. Twenty; paid.* That's twelve lines." The stone went back on the leaf. "And then you never came home. You left word with me: the cut, and whatever lay beyond. A crew's been gone eighteen days past its slip, and I've a line to rule for it, and I don't know what to put. Where's beyond?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:205:"There's a month on it," said Cael. "It's a healer's, and it's got twelve days to run. He stood one in the middle of it, against her word, for us, and it very nearly cost him the leg. Nobody stands it up before the month's out. Not for your field, and not for mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:*Two hundred and ninety-seventh day. Lowmarch; the hearth's end, and one on the keeper's settle. Eleven miles, and the ferry. The rope is paid: sixty yards of four, and he would take no fare. Pike has twelve lines, and a thirteenth with nothing in its last two columns. He has put a day against the season. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: eighteen of thirty.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:223:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:229:Cael saw it from the bench under the great ash as soon as there was light to see by, on the two hundred and ninety-eighth day, when the meadow was grey and forty fires were being blown up out of their ashes. It was a salt cart, with a pair of mules, and it had been drawn onto the grass at dusk by a carter who did not know how soft the thaw had left that corner. It stood now with its near wheels sunk to the axle. Half its sacks lay on the turf beside it. The carter had the mules out and was standing with his cap in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:269:*One use, and the arm is on a healer's board. He would not do a second. Nothing came. Twelve.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:5:Cael stopped in the door. It was a little past sunrise on the two hundred and ninety-eighth day, and the one small window laid a square of thin light across the whole of it. The courier's flat stood open at the table's head, with its six rows out and a pebble on each. The calfskin lay unrolled beneath them, under the Ternhall brass, and a dead man's book was open at its first page. Three of the Stair's sheets had been taken from their box and set in a row; and in the middle of everything, side by side, lay a leaf of her notebook and a square of stiff card with a turn of brown tape beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:37:She drew the iron out. It was a stake as long as her forearm, numbered thirty-two, with its point still bright, since nobody had ever driven it; and she laid its head under the window, beside the first page of a dead man's book and the corner of an office's sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:91:"Thank you. That is exactly as much as I wanted." Her finger had gone to the hide, and it touched each ring in turn, going east. "The Fallow's newest sheet stands in its sixty-first year, and the Stair's in its ninety-fourth; the hall's is a hundred and thirty-eight. And the place behind the iron—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:133:He took out Hesk's book, and the dividers slid from between its leaves into his palm, shut, with the cork upon their points. He had carried them for six days, under a ruling of his own that was a hundred and thirty days old: he did not put his hand to a thing that measured, since a cord had run short in it and two edges had disagreed. An old man in Denvash had sent them to step off a line with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:159:Cael looked along the skin, from the left hand to the right. There were four rings, and a point that was not yet drawn, and a number against each: sixty-one, and ninety-four, and a hundred and thirty-eight, and two hundred and six. They rose as they went. Beyond the last of them, in a strip of shadow, stood something that the office had stopped counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:223:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day. The fork above Millrace; the flat rock. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: nineteen of thirty. He rides, on the field's ruling. Karis opened the dividers. I lent them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:229:*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:299:Nobody argued it. Brom had gone into the mill by then, and he came out with the miller's wife behind him and a sack on the bound shoulder; it was a mark's worth of meal and a flitch, for five days of country with no gate in it. He said the figure as he came: a hundred and twenty-seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:337:"And this I said I'd not touch. Twice mended, and not a third. It's had a box on it for twelve days, against what I told you at a fork." Two fingers pressed. "It's no worse. I can't think why."

