   248	
   249	"You're awake," said Lira.
   250	
   251	She was a yard from him in the dark, and by her voice she had not lain down.
   252	
   253	"Yes."
   254	
   255	"You'd not look at her lantern. You looked at the roof for the whole time that she was telling you, and you came across that yard like a man going by a forge. And I can hear the water going now." Her voice was quite low. "I had you by the sleeve across a vault, seven days ago, with your lids shut. I know what it is that bills them. It only charges when it's looked."
   256	
   257	He did not answer at once. He could not have told that girl a false thing, and there was no need to; he had only to find the true one that was his to give.
   258	
   259	"Something came," he said. "I didn't ask for it, and I've not got a name for it that I'd put my hand to."
   260	
   261	"Is it bad?"
   262	
   263	"I don't know what it is."
   264	
   265	"That's twice now that you've given me a clerk's answer and looked sorry about it." The hay rustled; she had put her back against a wheel. "I'll not dig. I dug at you all the time that we were on a bluff, and all I ever turned up was what you'd have told me in a week if I'd let you alone. You'll tell me when it's a thing."
   285	*Four is not mine to write.*
   286	
   287	*Ruling: hold. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
   288	
   289	He shut the book on the pencil.
   290	
   291	The fourth lay in him where it had lain for seven days. A man with red eyes had poured a cup of water, under a town, and had said what he was to do if the thing ever read something that ought not to be made, and read it all the same. He was not to tell that man, and he was not to tell anybody. He was only to remember that it had been said.
   292	
   293	He had remembered, and it had been easy, for a week, since it had been a sentence about a day that might never come.
   294	
   295	It had come at about the three hundredth breath, on a plank, beside a stopped wheel.
   296	
   297	He lay with his arm across his eyes, and he did not go back to the place. He had been told once what a person does with a sore spot that he has found, and he kept his hands off it. But the sentence had moved. It no longer stood upright, where he had put it; it had begun to lean, like every row on a sheet that somebody had meant, and he knew which way.
   298	
   299	---
   300	
   301	They forded with the first of the light, on the two hundred and ninety-ninth day, and the woman on the flat stone counted them across.
   302	
   303	She had a good deal to count, and Seln stood on the far bank, where he had been before anybody else was awake, and did it with her, by the heads.
   304	
   305	"Twelve," he said. "And a mule."
   306	
   307	There were the six who had come down a border road: a quiet man, and a girl, and a big one whose arms came up as far as his shoulders that morning, and a small pale woman, and a boy with a book, and an old man on a stick. There were four from the house on the rise. A captain had his right wrist wrapped in linen under the leather. A tall man carried his spear upright, and a broad one eased his weight onto the right leg in the water; and a boy went over last, with his eyes on a girl's feet.
   308	
   309	There was a lean old woman with a staff and a board, who was on nobody's count and had said so.
   310	
   311	And there was the healer. She came down to the ford leading the brown mule on its rope, with her one pannier across the withers and a rolled strip of canvas; and she stopped at the water's edge and looked at Brom.
   312	
   313	"She'll carry three," said Oryn. "She's carried them before. Put them on the low side first."
   314	
   315	Brom lifted the boxes himself. He did it one at a time, slowly, with both hands, to the height of that brown back, and he looked at his own two arms after each as a man looks at a pair of tools that he had given up for lost. The mule stood for it with her ears back. When the third was on, with the flat strapped over all and a sack of the mill's meal for a counterweight, he laid his palm on her neck for the space of a breath; and it was the first time in twelve days that he had been able to reach it.
   316	
   317	"That's a healer's road and a researcher's whole archive on one beast," he said. "I'd not care to be the mule."
   318	
   319	"She's had worse company," said Oryn.
   320	
   321	"Twenty," said the old man, to nobody.
   322	
   323	"Of thirty," said Cael.
   324	
   325	"I am obliged." Vastin considered the far bank, and the track that climbed away from it toward a notch. "I am informed that I am to walk a stride behind a man who is slow to answer. I find it the first posting in some years which I understand."
   326	
   327	Karis had not crossed.
   328	
   329	She was on the near bank, kneeling at the flat stone that the wheels aimed for, with the calfskin spread on it under four pebbles; and she had pinned a clean sheet to the skin's eastern edge with two thorns out of the hedge. The Ternhall brass lay along the pencil line, through four rings, and ran on over the join.
   330	
   331	"I have made the office's figure again," she said, when he came back through the water to her. "By daylight, with nobody talking, and it is what it was." She held out her right hand. "I shall want them once more."
   332	
   333	He gave her the brass legs, shut, with the cork on. She opened them upon the hall's ring and the fourth, as she had done at a table; she closed them by a hair, and swung one point round along the strip, and it came down on the clean paper. She pressed, and it left a prick in the sheet no bigger than a grain of sand.
   334	
   335	Then she took the pencil in her left hand, the bound one, in its new linen, and drew.
   336	
   337	She began in the middle of the fourth ring, where the carter's skin ended, and the line went over the join of hide and paper without a tremor. It ran along the brass's edge to the prick, and it did not stop there. She carried it on, a hand's breadth beyond, to where the sheet gave out, and lifted the pencil only at the paper's edge.
   338	
   339	"You've gone past it," said Cael.
   340	
   341	"I do not know that it ends at the point. I know that an office measured as far as that, and wrote a figure. I am not going to draw a stop that the paper does not give me." She blew the lead off the sheet. "It is a line. It goes where it goes."
   342	
   343	She gave him back the dividers, and he put them into the book.
   344	
   345	They went up out of the green valley in the file that had been set under an ash, with a mule and two healers in the middle of it. At the notch's head the ruts divided. Teague did not look along the pair that went back to a plank and a cloth. He lifted two fingers, and the tall man turned his spear the other way.
   346	
   347	Cael walked at a captain's shoulder, a stride back.
   348	
   349	He had a healer's finding in him, word for word, and it was whole; he had two words of his own behind his eyes, which did not agree with it, and which he had told to nobody. He had a sentence from under a town that had begun to lean. Ahead of him a quiet man went small into the pale grass, along a line that a woman had ruled past the place where the figures stopped.
