   179	"That's ours," she said, under her breath.
   180	
   181	"He's had it by him long enough," said Brom.
   182	
   183	The man in the door had not followed any of that; he had been looking at the hide the whole while, as he looked at a load.
   184	
   185	"Oldest last," said Teague.
   186	
   187	It was not put as a question. Karis answered it all the same, with one small nod, as she would have passed a figure that had been read back to her correctly; and he took his thumbs out of his belt.
   188	
   189	"Then that's where we're going."
   190	
   191	---
   192	
   193	They were on the south road by the middle of the forenoon, in the file that a captain had set under an ash.
   194	
   195	It held. Cael had not been sure that it would, outside a meadow. A quiet man went ahead, small against the pale grass, and after him seven feet of ash with a leaf of steel on it. Then came a pair of shoulders that did not look round; and Cael walked a stride behind the left of those, where he had been put. He had never in his life walked at anybody's shoulder to be asked what he saw.
   196	
   197	Behind him he could hear the rest by their feet. There was Karis's, with the flat and the hide, and beside it the tap of a lean old woman's staff. A big man came next with a box; and last of all there was a slow uneven step, the bad hip of a man with a crossbow on his back.
   198	
   199	There was no stick in it.
   200	
   201	That had been settled in the yard, and it was the first thing that the field had cost anybody. The salt cart that had been put out of the mud was going down to the mill for meal, light, behind its two mules; and its carter had said to Teague, cap in hand, that a man's arm was owed something. Teague had looked at an old man with a slat on his leg, and at the length of a ridge.
   202	
   203	"It's owed a seat," he had said. "For him. I'll not walk ten at the pace of one."
   204	
   205	The old man had opened his mouth. Then he had looked at the nine who would have had to wait for him, and had shut it, and gone to the tail-board. He had offered his copper, and the carter would not have it; he said that it was the captain's fare, and paid.
   206	
   207	So a cart went on a furlong ahead, with a brown coat on its tail, and for the fourth day together Cael walked toward a pair of boots that hung.
   208	
   209	They passed the well at the fork, which had a house over it now, with a door hung and a new rope on the windlass; a crew had taken that from the plank in the cold. Then the road climbed, and they were on the back of the ridge.
   210	
   211	It was the same pale grass, combed all one way, and the same wind. He had come along it once in the dark before a frost, behind a mule, to ask a healer whether she would come; and he had gone back along it with the answer.
   212	
   213	Nobody spoke. Twice a flat hand went up, far ahead, for nothing that Cael ever saw, and the file stood, and went on at two fingers. The second time the boy stood exactly when Lira did.
   214	
    60	
    61	Lira went down it with her face turned up to the scree. She had lain on those plates with a knife, on a day when the left arm could do no more than hold. He saw her mark the place, and shut that hand once at her side, and look away. Karis did not look at the banks at all. She was watching the water go under the dray's wheels, with her lips moving, like a person who has written six findings about a thing and suspects that there is a seventh.
    62	
    63	Behind him the stick's sound had stopped.
    64	
    65	Vastin had got down at the head, as he had undertaken to do for every slope. He was coming down the dry edge last of all, a tread at a time, with the bound leg swung out stiff; and forty strides from the top he was standing still.
    66	
    67	There was nothing there to stop for. Wet stones lay in the road, and gravel beside them, with a tuft of dead bent growing out of it. No crack ran from bank to bank. That had been made in ice, and it had gone where the ice went.
    68	
    69	He did not turn to see who was near him. He looked at the wet stones, and spoke to them.
    70	
    71	"Here," said Vastin.
    72	
    73	It was the place. Cael knew it by Brom's stone, three strides below, and by the lie of the two banks; there was no other sign of it in the world. A plane had stood on that spot from one bank to the other, as high as the scree, and a man had been the foot of it.
    74	
    75	The old man looked at the wet stones for the space of several breaths.
    76	
    77	Then he lifted the stick.
    78	
    79	He did not lift it far. The ash came up off the gravel by the breadth of a hand, and was held there, level, in his right fist; and the whole of him came over onto the bound leg, as a load is let down onto a prop that nobody is yet sure of. Cael saw the slat take it. He saw the grey mouth shut hard. It was one breath, and the half of another.
    80	
    81	The stick came down.
    82	
    83	The knee had held. It was not mended; anybody could have seen that, by the sweat that had sprung on the old forehead in that cold shade. But it had borne a man once, with nothing beside it, on the very ground where eight hounds had been stopped by it and where four holes had been torn in it afterward. He had not been told to try it. He had not asked leave of anybody; and Cael noticed that for once he did not turn to see who would enter the thing.
    84	
