    95	He had given that word to a captain three times, at a hearth and on a landing. He had never before given it to this man. It was a clerk's word, and he had kept it from him as a person keeps his purse from somebody who once had the counting of it.
    96	
    97	It did not feel like a clerk's word on that road. It felt like a stroke made at the foot of a slip.
    98	
    99	Lira's two fingers had gone up, below, where the dray's tail was going out into the light. They went on down.
   100	
   120	
   121	"What'd you do there?"
   122	
   123	"We read a survey," said Karis. She stood at Cael's shoulder, with the flat under her arm. "An office has measured four places beyond this river for six years. I had its sheets, and I could not read them; and there was a man there who could show me how such a thing is put together. I have read them now."
   124	
   125	"What'd it pay?"
   126	
   127	"Nothing."
   128	
   129	"And what came of it?"
   130	
   131	"A bearing," said Cael.
   132	
   133	Pike considered him. Then the pen went into the horn, and he wrote, slowly, in the square black hand.
   134	
   135	*Treswick. Survey read. Bearing.*
   136	
   137	Cael read it as it went down, a word at a time, and thought only that it was short.
   138	
   139	The pen had gone on to the two ruled columns at the line's end, where a price stood, and what became of it. It hung over the first, and passed on, and hung over the second.
   140	
   141	"I've nothing for those," said Pike. "It paid nothing, and it's not out yet. They'll stand empty, and I'll say that they're empty."
   142	
   143	He said so, to the room. Then he turned back three leaves.
   144	
   145	It was the season's line, with its ruled space at the end. Cael had seen that space left blank on purpose at first snow, and carried blank over a thaw onto a new cloth.
   146	
   147	"I said that I'd leave that until I'd a column for it," said Pike. "I've not found the column. I've had a hundred and sixty-eight days to look." He dipped. "A blank's a kind of entry, so long as a man knows why it's there. But a blank with nothing against it can't be told from one that was forgotten. So I'll put a day."
   148	
   149	He wrote it in the space, small: the day, by his own book's reckoning, and no figure after it.
   150	
   151	"That's when I left off looking," he said. "It's not a number. It says that on this day the keeper of this book knew what he didn't know, and signed for it." He blotted the place with the heel of his hand. "Eleventh. And dated."
   152	
