   169	"I said after the thaw. It's a month gone. I said whatever's on that bearing, and I've just heard him write the word." His eyes went along them, and they did not stop at five; they went on to the door-post, where a stick was grounded. "I'll say it here, where it's heard."
   170	
   171	He let the room wait for it.
   172	
   173	"Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four."
   174	
   175	Cael heard the count before he heard anything else, and so did the room.
   176	
   177	There had been five names on that cloth for the whole of a cold term and more. A captain had just said six of them, aloud, and nobody at the bench had corrected him; it was a count for a contract. It was not an admission, and it had not been made by any of the five; and Cael, who did not turn his head, knew by the stillness at the door-post that an old man had heard exactly how much it was and how much it was not.
   178	
   179	"Say what it holds," said Brom. "I'll not put my thumb to a figure."
   180	
   181	"It holds four things. The count's the first, and you've had it. Neither healer's on it. A healer goes where she's wanted, and I'll not have one sent." Teague drew a thumb out of his belt. "Shares by the count. Whatever it pays is cut ten ways; and what it costs is carried the same, so far as a cost can be."
   182	
   183	"It's paid nothing yet."
   184	
   185	"I know what it's paid. My bow's told me three times." He drew the other thumb. "The field's mine. On a road, with steel out, there's one voice, and it's the one that's had the most practice. I'd not ask it if I thought that you'd say otherwise."
   186	
   187	It was said to Cael, and it was said level.
   188	
   189	He priced it, as he had been taught to price a thing by a big man who would not let a figure go unsaid. He had led five people into three places where a Path was worth nothing, and had brought them out; nobody else in that room could say as much. But he had been touched three times in a square of snow without once seeing it come, and the man in front of him had learned more from his boots in those three touches than Cael had learned from a face in a year. On open ground, with a watch to be read and ten people to be put somewhere, there was one of them who was better. Cael had written so in a book. He had not been asked to say it in front of fifty.
   190	
   191	"It's yours," he said. "I'd have asked you to take it."
