   206	
   207	"I'd not ask a thing with a month on it to be shown for a count." Teague looked once more toward the door-post, and it was the look of a man reading a load that he does not mean to lift yet. "I asked what I needed, of a factor. I know where it stands, and that it stays. That'll do me."
   208	
   209	He put both thumbs back into his belt, like a man drawing the last rope over a load.
   210	
   211	"Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more."
   212	
   213	He nodded, by the width of a finger, and it was done, and he had not offered his hand. There was a cloth on a nail behind Pike's head with one name alone on its first line and five in a row on its eleventh; and a contract did not take either of them down. Cael found that he did not wish it to. He would sooner walk east beside a man who was ten places better at a thing, and said so, than beside a friend who had to be reminded which of them the ledger preferred.
   214	
   215	He wrote it at the hearth's end, when the room had thinned.
   216	
   217	*Two hundred and ninety-seventh day. Lowmarch; the hearth's end, and one on the keeper's settle. Eleven miles, and the ferry. The rope is paid: sixty yards of four, and he would take no fare. Pike has twelve lines, and a thirteenth with nothing in its last two columns. He has put a day against the season. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: eighteen of thirty.*
   218	
   219	*Teague's term is taken. Ten on his count, by a contract and nothing else. The field is his. He is still the better at it.*
   220	
   235	"He's going to do something," said Brom.
   236	
   237	Teague walked round the cart's tail. The ash had put a root out there, as thick as a man's thigh, a pace behind the sunk wheel. He set his right heel against it, with care, shifting the boot twice, like a mason bedding a stone. Then he laid his right palm flat on the end of the cart's bed, with the fingers spread.
   238	
   239	What he did next took him longer than the thing itself.
   240	
   241	He lined himself up; Cael had no better word for it. The heel was set, and the knee came in over the heel, and the hip over the knee; the shoulder dropped, and turned, until the whole of that side of him ran straight from a root in the ground to a palm on a board, like a prop that is set under a beam before the wedges are driven. Not a part of him lay off that line. He stood so for three breaths, and Cael saw the boy move up behind him, on the left, with a cloth steaming across both hands.
   242	
   243	There was no light, and there was no sound but the cart's.
   244	
   245	It went forward. It did not roll; it was put. The mud let go of two wheels with a noise like a boot drawn out of a bog, and a ton and more of oak and salt went the length of a man's body up onto firm ground, and stopped there, dead, as though a team of six had taken it up at once and then been cut out of the traces.
   246	
   247	It went that far and not a hand farther.
   248	
   249	Something came back. Cael saw it arrive: the straight arm jarred, from the palm to the shoulder, like a pole that has been struck on its end. And he heard the wrist, at twenty strides, across a meadow: one small dry click, such as a latch makes.
   250	
   251	The cloth was round it before the arm had dropped.
   252	
   253	"That's a Force," said Brom, very low. "I'd wondered what he kept in that leather. It comes up out of the ground, through a man, and out at the hand; I saw one shift a block at a quarry once, when I was small. They'd sent for him from three valleys off." He was watching the old woman, who had the arm on her board already. "They said that he'd been a deal stronger at twenty."
   254	
   255	The carter had found his tongue, and was pointing down the meadow, to where a second cart stood canted in the same soft ground.
   256	
   257	Teague looked where he pointed. He answered across the boy's head, in the voice that he kept for a string of mules, and it carried to the bench.
   258	
