   260	
   261	Then the tall man and the bow went down the meadow with three iron bars and a baulk of timber, and the second cart was shifted as a carter would have shifted it, in the better part of an hour.
   262	
   263	Cael had the book on his knee before the first bar was set.
   264	
   265	Nothing had come to him, and he had not looked for it. He was twenty strides off, on a bench, and nobody in that meadow had meant anything at anybody; a man had put his hand to a cart. But he had seen a line drawn through a body, plainer than he had ever seen one drawn on a hide, and he knew better than to trust it to his memory.
   266	
   267	*Seen, at first light, the meadow, from twenty strides. Teague's is Force. He set a heel against a root and a palm against a cart's tail, and stood until every joint between the two lay straight. Then it went: one push, the length of a body, and no more of it after. It wants firm ground behind and a thing in front. What comes back comes down the same road and is paid where it left him. The wrist. I heard it.*
   268	
   269	*One use, and the arm is on a healer's board. He would not do a second. Nothing came. Twelve.*
   270	
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:227:*Karis cracked the grade. The sites are ranked by age and the bearing runs oldest-last — somebody laid them out in order, and the order runs into the deep edge, toward the first one they ever made. Second-tier's the fourth site, the one the Compact fenced. Third-tier's out past it, on the line, quiet for centuries. Teague's crew came after the thaw like he said — ten of us now, and he's still better at this than I am, and the ledger still says so. Two crews, one bearing, and a straightedge my guardian sent me because my grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. Turns out she was right about the line. Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end.*

