## AFTER MOVEMENT 7 — chapters 41–46, days 294–299 (drafted 2026-10-12, author claude-opus-5-5, NO-SOURCE method)

### Coordinator rulings (binding)
- **Entry seam with Movement 6 ratified** (Sol plan audit): twelve fragments; deployment thirty-two; purse 130; the loft and handcart to the 295th; the leaf's back unread; Vastin's knee day fourteen on the 293rd; Karis's stitched palm; Brom's arms; Lira's days out of wool; sixty yards of rope owed.
- **The 294th: the list of parts.** Cleon turned the skin between boards; its back is paler, scored and not inked, **nine short lines, a thing and its number to each**. Karis can spell all nine and turn four (the four things the face says were made); the other five are spelled and unturned, and **she has said none of the numbers aloud**. **The fourth line is the mark's:** the word for the mark is what bears; two runs hang from it LEVEL (those who are kindled; and a six-sign word in the scraped-thin place), and after both the number *three*. Cleon made a second copy, drawn and not read, and gave it to Karis ("Your hand's better than mine on the fourth line."). The fifty-two sheets were counted into three boxes under two hands, K. and V. The clerk wrote *Withdrawn by its bearer* and *four* and ruled the account off. Protected, Cleon's, at the iron door: "Work. Not a finding." / "Mind the stair. It leans." **He is not above ground and did not look at Cael.** This edition has no noun "bearer" for a sign (signs bear, are carried, or are nothing).
- **The road east (the 295th–297th).** The rope: **sixty yards of four-strand, two marks (purse 128)**, bought at the walk under Treswick's wall and paid to the Lowmarch ferryman, who waived the fare; the debt is closed. Out by the outer arch: six out as six in, none written. **The bridge: five piers counted; the crown not looked at** ("the promise held to the gate and is done; the not looking was mine"). The spring, with the wind in their faces. The fork-stone passed.
- **How Vastin travelled (ruled against the audit).** After the alley his knee would not carry him a day's road: **he rode the rope-walk dray's tail-board for three days at a copper a day of his own, got down for every hill, and walked the whole cut**; on the 298th Teague put him on a salt cart ("It's owed a seat. For him. I'll not walk ten at the pace of one."). **From the ford on the 299th he walks in the file.** The packet's "walked again on a bad knee" is met by the hills and the cut; closed Movement 6 (a rope down a ladder, a stall, a handcart, a stair on his seat) governs the rest.
- **The shoulder hold.** The factor offered twenty at each first frost to the five; Brom declined ("We're walking east"). Protected: "He said you'd say that." — **"he" is Teague**, who took the salt up himself, asked the factor twice how long the wall stood and whether its maker moved, and built his term on the answer. (The draft's offer of a seat at the hold's fire for Vastin was CUT: where he belongs is not this book's to open.)
- **The cut revisited (the 296th).** Protected, **Vastin's**: "Here."; he stood on the bound leg once with the stick off the ground; "Two words," … "Both of them ours." (Vastin's, one speech); Cael: "Noted" — the first time he has given that word to this man. **A horseman kept a mile behind from the cut to the dark; a riderless horse stood in the alders on the western bank on the 297th.** No face, no name; Seln "can put it no higher". Nothing was seen east of the river.
- **Lowmarch (the 297th).** Pike's twelfth line is the clearance (*Eight. Twenty; paid.*); the thirteenth, protected: *Treswick. Survey read. Bearing.*, with price and outcome left empty and said to be empty. **Cael read it the right way up, from Pike's side of the bench.** Pike put a DAY against the season line (nothing printed; "Eleventh. And dated."). **Teague's term, taken as a contract** (protected: "Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four." … "Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more." / "The wall's still the wall." — all Teague's): **ten on the count — Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, Vastin; Teague, the tall man with the spear, the bow, the young one; NEITHER healer**; shares and costs by the count; **the field is Teague's** (Cael: "It's yours. I'd have asked you to take it."); the reading is Karis's ("You'll be asked."). The wall has a month on it and is not stood before the month is out. No hand was offered; the cloth still reads first and eleventh.
- **Teague (cutaway).** He is going "to be at the front of the thing" and says so. He knows that a factor saw an Iron Wall and that its maker "had been somebody … and had left off being it"; **nothing of the mechanism, the fragments, the leaf, the list, the date, or who Vastin was.** The bow (a broad man with a bad hip), the tall man and the older healer (a lean old woman with a staff and a board, "not counted … not told where to stand") have not spoken to the six. The young one "does what he's told a little too soon".
- **Force SEEN, not acquired (the 298th, first light).** Teague lined heel, knee, hip, shoulder and palm from an ash root to a sunk salt cart and put it a body's length; the recoil came back down the same line to the wrist (a click heard at twenty strides); the young one's cloth; the old woman's board. Protected, Teague's: "Don't put it in front of anything that needs doing twice." **Nothing came. Twelve. Thirty-two.** The two crews walked the meadow in file to four hand-signs (*stand*, *seen*, *come on*, *nothing*); no drill under a wall.
- **The march order (Teague's):** Seln ahead; the tall man; Teague, **with Cael at his left shoulder a stride back**; Lira; the young one (watching her feet); Karis, the healers and the mule in the middle; Brom; Vastin a stride behind him; the bow last.
- **THE GRADE (the 298th).** By laying Cleon's drawn copy on her own, Karis has four of the six signs: the word is ***set***. The fourth line: the mark, over those who are kindled and what is set; three. **Three marks:** a stroke alone (a box ruled and EMPTY — no sheet carries it; no first-tier site appears); a stroke with one bar (stake thirty-two, the dead surveyor's book, every sheet of the four places); a stroke with two bars (six sheets of a FIFTH place). Protected, Karis's: "A mark is a grade. The one they give a boy at fourteen says what he's *for*." … "The one on a sheet says how *old* the thing is."; "Somebody laid them down in order"; "It's observation. I'm telling you what the paper says. Not what it means." **Ages, each counted from the office's first sheet for the place: the Fallow sixty-one, the Stair ninety-four, the Hall a hundred and thirty-eight, the place behind the iron two hundred and six**; the fifth place has two unread signs where its figure should be ("older than two hundred and six", no figure). The paper says how long ago a first sheet was drawn and nothing of who walked there since. **Vastin's first "Noted."** Protected, Teague's: "Oldest last." … "Then that's where we're going." Hesk's dividers were **opened by Karis on Cael's loan** ("They're lent. I'll not open them."): the office's run from the fourth place to the fifth is "three days past the iron, a little less"; its compass point and its figure in lengths are unsaid. Stake thirty-two is back in the tarred case, never driven.
- **The log of the 298th** (protected, whole, once; the grandmother is Cael's, Hesk's late wife): "Karis cracked the grade … Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end." **"Four hundred" stands nowhere else in the movement**; the centuries and the figure are Cael's own inference, put on his own page.
- **Millrace (the 298th evening).** "Eleven off the ridge." (the woman with the net, as in Book 7) / "I know." (Oryn). Oryn to Vastin: "Stand there."; he stood an hour without a clause. Protected: "Nineteen days," (Vastin) / "Then it's eleven more." (Oryn). Oryn's terms: three holds owed yet on her round; "I'm a stop on my own route."; on nobody's count. "Sixth chair." (Lira) / "On my terms. Which are the only kind." (Oryn); she sat. **Readings, each asked first:** Brom's LEFT shoulder mended (a quarter-hour; she was grey), the right re-tied shorter by the older healer ("That'll do"); Lira's arm ("You listened when it told you the second time." / "Once." / "Once is once more than I priced." — "It's an arm."); Karis's palm ("Who sewed this." — the seven stitches drawn; "Write. Don't lean."); Vastin's knee — he told her of the alley wall himself; "It held in a cut," (Oryn); new linen and two laths; **the month stands as counted**; "Noted," (Vastin) / "That's new." (Oryn) / "It's a useful word." (Vastin) / "It's his." (Oryn). **Purse 127** (meal and a flitch, one mark).
- **What Oryn was told before she read him:** the two self-readings of the 281st and why; **the eleventh** (Vastin's, in the stair; he does not know); **the twelfth** (from "a man under the registry"; made things only; "Not a man."). Her ruling: nothing is turned "on my plank"; the road is his own.
- **THE DEEP READING.** "May I?" / "Yes." Forty minutes, to her own floor; she found the eleventh ("stands in the stream like a post") and the twelfth ("a little flat thing behind your eyes") unprompted; her current went round the still place from every side. **At about the three hundredth breath the twelfth turned inward, unbidden: *Still.* Then *Set.*** He kept his jaw and hands loose and did not let the next word come. Oryn felt only "Something turned over … out at the edge of you. One of yours, in its sleep," and dismissed it. **Her finding, protected, whole:** "There's a place in you I can't read because it isn't *flow*. I've spent my life reading flow. Whatever that is, it's not the kind of thing my Path was built for." **The two findings do not agree, and the two words are told to NO ONE** — not Oryn, not Lira. Lira offered the leaf; protected: "Is it a finding?" / "It's a leaf" … "Then it's not mine." … "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table." … "Lie still when I say. That's still the term." **Oryn has not heard the leaf.** The eyes paid in the dark. Lira knows that something unasked came and has said that she will wait; the log gives four reasons for writing nothing ("Four is not mine to write") and the ruling *hold*. **Cleon's warning "has begun to lean".**
- **Dawn, the 299th.** "Twelve," … "And a mule." (Seln). The mule carries the three boxes, the flat, a sack of meal, Oryn's pannier and canvas. Karis pinned a clean sheet to the hide's eastern edge, pricked the divider's point on it, and ruled the pencil line from the fourth ring through the point and a hand's breadth past ("I am not going to draw a stop that the paper does not give me").
- **Bodies at dawn on the 299th.** Vastin: **day twenty of thirty**, Oryn's linen and two laths, stick, walking. Brom: both arms up to the shoulder. Lira: the arm released. Karis: stitches out, linen on, not to lean. Teague: the right wrist in linen under the leather. Cael: sound.
- **Money:** 130 → 128 → **127 marks**; the reserve sealed in the third box. **Names:** none minted. **Length:** about 32,000 against 32,000.

### Review record
All reads on Sol (gpt-5.6-sol): editorial 316k; cold 98k; source-distance 190k (2 tracked passages; probe 1% / 12%); plan / canon / protected-text audit 744k tokens (REPAIR; seventy-six exact fixes). **Twenty-five applied by the coordinator** (the sixteen marker restorations, minimal, with the source's speakers; two sign-nouns; a broken join; three thaw intervals; the factor's seat cut), five of them amended. **Fifty-one DECLINED:** Vastin made to walk the whole road (the audit did not weigh closed Movement 6's knee); the ages re-derived from a printed date (date subtraction is reserved to the unspoken sum; the author softens the corner figure's claim instead); the Millrace count given to a "ford-keeper" (the woman with the net is locked Book 7's); the night exchange with Lira cut whole (only "You went stiff" is wrong; the author re-grounds it); "Twelve. And a mule." taken from Seln. **Repair r1 in progress with the same author** (seven joins, the ages passage, Lira's line, two tracked chains, a referent pass).

### New canon minted (author's list)
All of this is mine and is flagged for ruling.

- **The back of the leaf (41:29–49; 41:55–59; 41:113–135).** Paler than the face, with no dark stuff rubbed in; nine short lines in a column down the left side, ruled first; each a noun and a number written out in words. The first four begin with the four things that the face names, in the face's order; the other five are nouns that Karis can spell and cannot turn. No number is said aloud. The fourth line (the mark's) crosses the thin place behind the scraping: one bearer (the face's word for the mark), two runs hung level from it (five signs; six signs), and the number *three*. On the 294th the first run is "the people at a kindling"; of the second, three signs of six are sure. Nobody asks whether the back sits or goes; Cleon says only that it is a list, that the two runs hang level, and that it ends ("There's no tenth that's been cut away").
- **Cleon turns the skin** between two boards, a third brought for it, without touching it (41:27–31). **Cleon's copy:** a square of his card-stock, drawn sign for sign and not read, with a red tick under each place where a run ends; he strikes his own drawing of the fourth line through and has Karis put hers in (41:145–151); he gives it to her at the iron, rolled, with a turn of the bundle's brown tape (41:187). He takes his own lamp back from Cael's fingers (41:169–173).
- **The fifty-two sheets go up** counted into three boxes by a tally kept by Vastin at Karis's asking, and signed K. and V. (41:83–99; 41:163). **The clerk's last entry:** *Withdrawn by its bearer*, and *four*, with a line ruled beneath across both columns (41:221).
- **The rope (42:13–23).** The same coil that Brom looked at on the 282nd, kept by for him; **two marks**. **The rope-walk's dray (42:31–63):** four wheels, two brown cobs, forty coils of four-strand for the western landing's broker, who sent for it a fortnight since because "a big man from over the water" called his rope the wrong kind; "the wrong kind of excellent" is a saying on that landing. The carter: a lean man with a straw in his mouth, unnamed. The coil rides free; Vastin rides the tail-board at a copper a day of his own, and gets down for every hill.
- **The outer gate going out (42:65–77):** the keeper moves two beads for the boots on the tail-board when Lira holds him to his own count: "Six out. Six in, the day I had you."
- **The bridge (42:79–119).** The twelfth is refused by counting the piers; Karis lays her bound hand on the parapet and draws nothing; Cael finds that he is free to turn and does not, because he ruled *hold* on the light.
- **The road east.** The wind has gone round to the east (42:187; 42:229; 42:245). Vastin walks the rise, turns once at its top, and lifts a hand toward a pen that is not there (42:201–205). Brom touches Oryn's bowl at the fork-stone (42:213). At the spring the slab is open to the wind and no paper is laid out; the camp is under the outcrop's western face (42:239–251). Seln stands at the basin with his back to the slab (42:261–271).
- **Cael to Vastin, on the causeway (42:149–159):** Vastin says that he has noticed that he is not told, and does not ask; Cael answers only "It's six people's. That's why."
- **Sheep on the high moor,** turned out since the cut was cleared (43:3–5); thirty-one carts up the cut in a fortnight (43:15).
- **The factor's offer (43:19–43):** twenty at each first frost to the five who walk the cut before a wheel goes on, with the long house's fire and the pot for the week; and for Vastin a seat at that fire for the whole of any cold term, "within a shout of my road". Brom declines. **"He" is Teague,** who brought the hold's salt up behind the first carts with three of his own, asked what the factor saw, asked it again, and said that the five were for the east. He asked how long the wall stood and whether its maker lifted a foot.
- **The cut on the 296th (43:47–95).** The seep runs as water; the glass and the white crack are gone. The line is forty strides below the head and three above Brom's flat stone. Vastin lifts the stick a hand's breadth off the gravel and stands on the bound leg for a breath and a half. The three wrecks have been broken up; one wheel is left (43:113–115).
- **Why nothing came at the wall on the 279th (Cael's own reckoning, 43:107):** a thing comes when somebody means it, with the whole of himself, at something that can mean it back.
- **The horseman (43:117–135; 43:157–165; 43:245).** A man on a bay or brown horse, in something dark, on the skyline above the cut; he keeps a mile behind the dray all the afternoon of the 296th, does not enter the cut while the six are in it, and comes through afterward at a walk. On the 297th a horse is tied in the alders above the western landing's last bend, with no rider on it. No face; no boots seen. Seln: "somebody is on it who behaves as a watcher would. I can put it no higher."
- **The halting-ground (43:139–187).** Vastin sits before the six; no copper is laid on a stone ("I am told that a road has no keeper."); Lira's flat hand, and he does it bent.
- **The western landing and the ferry (43:199–245).** The river has fallen two feet. The broker has "forty coils, and more laying". The ferryman kneels to open the lay, drags the coil to his rope-house himself, and waives the fare: "She's a copper a head. Not today. Today she's paid." He has crossed forty times since the thaw on a hauling rope with two yarns gone.
- **Lowmarch on the 297th:** more than forty fires in the meadow (43:249).
- **Teague's window (44:3–97).** He has been home from the shoulder hold a week. He has never seen an Iron Wall; he knows that they belong to tribunals, and does not ask who the old man was. What he took from the factor: the wall cannot come to the trouble, and wants a count of who is behind it. Why he goes: "I mean to be at the front of the thing. I'll not dress it." The bow's objection (it pays nothing; salt work on four holds; the weir at the mill wants three stakes yet) and his answer ("I'd like to see you find the mill without me"). The older healer comes for the wrist, uncounted ("There's a valley up the north water that breaks its legs every dry spell"). The young one asks whether he is on the count: "You've two legs, and a Path, and you do what you're told a little too soon. That's a man. I can count as far as four." He nods with his whole head. Teague can read a slip, a ledger and a man's hands (44:91). Why "contract" and no more: two crews that become one bury each other's dead as kin, and a captain turns back on a ledge for a man who is not his; "He had four, and he meant to walk four home."
- **Pike (44:101–157).** The twelfth line, said twice: *Clearance; the cut below the shoulder hold. Eight. Twenty; paid.* The thirteenth is the protected line, with its price and outcome columns empty and said to be empty. **The season line is dated:** in the ruled space he writes "the day, by his own book's reckoning, and no figure after it" (no date is printed); "That's when I left off looking… Eleventh. And dated." He has had "a hundred and sixty-eight days to look". Cael reads the thirteenth line from Pike's own side of the bench (44:133–147). What is said in the room: "We read a survey… An office has measured four places beyond this river for six years" (Karis, 44:123); "A bearing" (Cael).
- **The contract's four heads, in my words as Teague's (44:175–187):** the count (neither healer on it: "A healer goes where she's wanted, and I'll not have one sent"); shares by the count, "and what it costs is carried the same, so far as a cost can be"; the field ("On a road, with steel out, there's one voice"); the reading ("I'll take that from her as I'd take a ford from a man who'd crossed it"). Karis: "I shall want to be asked before anybody steps over a thing." Cael: "It's yours. I'd have asked you to take it." Teague on the wall: "I'd not ask a thing with a month on it to be shown for a count." No hand is offered.
- **The meadow cart (44:215–257).** A salt cart sunk to its axle by the great ash; Teague's right heel against the ash's root, his right palm on the cart's bed; "He lined himself up": heel, knee, hip, shoulder, palm in one straight line, held three breaths; no light and no sound; the cart goes the length of a body and stops dead; the arm jars from palm to shoulder; the wrist clicks, heard at twenty strides; the young one's cloth is round it before the arm drops. Brom names the Path ("It comes up out of the ground, through a man, and out at the hand") and has seen one at a quarry as a child. The second cart is shifted with three iron bars and a baulk in the better part of an hour.
- **The file (44:261–307).** Teague's reason: ten who have never walked together walk like two crews, "and a thing that hunts will count two". Order: Seln out front; the tall man; Teague, with Cael at his left hand a stride back ("he sees a thing before he knows that he's seen it, and I want to be told"); Lira; the young one behind her; the papers in the middle with whoever is healing, and a beast when there is one; Brom; Vastin a stride behind him; the bow last. The four hands are taught by Lira. Teague and Vastin exchange their first words ("I'm told that's where you've stood." / "It is."). Nobody asks to see the wall.
- **How the grade comes off the sheets (45:3–177).**
  - With Cleon's drawing and her own laid one on the other by a window, Karis has four signs of the six, and they are the six-sign word that she uncovered on the Stair's sheets on her ninth day under the town: *set* (45:13–25). The fourth line: the mark, and hung from it, level, "those who are kindled" and "what is set"; then *three* (45:29). She lays stake thirty-two, the dead man's first page and the Stair's abstract together (45:35–41).
  - Three boxes ruled on a clean sheet (45:55). The middle: a stroke with a bar (the four places of the hide). The right: a stroke with two bars — the unmatched sheets of the spring, which are **one group, six sheets, one to a year**; she saw their square at the spring and said nothing of it (45:57–63). The left is ruled and empty: no sheet in six rows carries a stroke alone (45:65–71). Lira: "It's tiers. First, second, third."
  - **The running number (45:77–99):** small, in the right-hand corner at each sheet's foot, after a clerk's flourish; it falls by one with every row (the Stair: 94 to 89). Vastin: "It is a series number… each sheet is numbered from the first that the office holds… I say what the form is; I do not say who drew the first." The newest row: the Fallow 61; the Stair 94; the hall 138; the place behind the iron 206. Seln: the six rows "were only as much of it as had been kept from the people who ought to have received it."
  - **The two-bar place (45:101–109):** its corner has no figure, only two signs out of the index; Vastin: "A series that was opened before the present manner of numbering is carried under its old style." Karis: older than two hundred and six, and no figure given.
  - **The office's distance (45:113–151):** the two-bar sheets carry one line above their eight stations: the fourth place's four signs, a point of the compass, a measure in lengths. Neither the point nor the figure is printed. Karis turns the lengths into days by what she knows of the cord: "three such days, less a little". **The dividers** are opened for the first time on a distance, by Karis, on Cael's loan ("They're lent. I'll not open them."); set on the hall's ring and the fourth, closed by a hair, and swung along the brass, the point comes down a hand's breadth off the skin, in the Ternhall strip's shadow. "Three days past the iron." / "As we walk. A little less." (Teague; Karis.)
- **The south road (45:181–231).** Vastin rides the salt cart to the fork above the mill on Teague's ruling ("It's owed a seat. For him. I'll not walk ten at the pace of one."); the carter will not take his copper ("the captain's fare, and paid"). The well at the fork has its house and a new rope. Lira reads the log at the flat rock: "Your grandmother." / "Hesk's wife, and I never knew her."
- **Millrace (45:235–291).** Three stakes still gone from the weir. Oryn is thinner. The two healers look once at each other and then at Brom's right shoulder. Teague speaks to Oryn for the first time. **A mark's worth of meal and a flitch** at the mill (45:283). Brom sets Oryn's bowl, carried seventeen days under his lashing, on the bench's end before an upturned keg; she sits.
- **The readings (45:295–379).** Brom: the older healer unties her own knot; Oryn shuts the wrench in the left shoulder in a quarter of an hour (the arm goes to the height of his chin); the right is "no worse. I can't think why"; the older healer re-ties it in two yards with the turn behind the arm; Oryn: "That'll do." Lira: "It's an arm." Karis: the seven stitches drawn with a small knife, a little spent "where the thumb pulls", new linen; "Write. Don't lean." Vastin: he has stood at the post the better part of an hour ("I was told to stand there. I was not told that I might leave off."); he tells her of the wall himself; she takes the slat off, binds the joint in her own linen with two new laths, and says that the month stands as it was counted.
- **The two tellings (46:3–63).** Oryn's judgment of the readings of the 281st: "You kept my words, and you went round what they were for, as a carter goes round a toll by way of the field… And then you carried it seventeen days, and came down a ridge to tell me so… It's entered." She is told the eleventh as well as the twelfth, and that Vastin does not know. Her term, in my words: "for as long as my hands are in you, nothing's turned"; "After, you're on a road, with your own eyes to spend… I don't keep people. But not on my plank."
- **The deep reading (46:67–175; 46:179–205).** After the miller shuts the sluice; Lira on the bench's end. Forty minutes: four hundred and ninety breaths at twelve to the minute. She names the ten by what her hands meet, finds the eleventh ("it stands in the stream like a post, and everything parts for it") and the twelfth ("a little flat thing behind your eyes, laid by, like a rule folded on a shelf"). At the place her current parts as a river parts at a cutwater; she goes round it left, right, above and beneath ("It's not small. I'd thought that it was small."). At about the three hundredth breath the twelfth leans in: *Still.* and, a round later, *Set.* His heart strikes once; he keeps his jaw and his hands. He feels a next word and does not let it come. Oryn: "Something turned over… Out at the edge of you. One of yours, in its sleep. Leave it. It's nothing to me." She comes out grey and counts to fifty twice.
- **After (46:207–261).** Cael marks that the two findings do not say the same thing: "Hers said that there was no flow in that place for a Tide to feel. His said that something stood there which had been put." Lira saw him go stiff and is told: "Something came. I didn't ask for it, and I've not got a name for it that I'd put my hand to." / "You'll tell me when it's a thing." / "On the road." / "If there's road." The eyes are paid in the cart-shed. The four reasons (46:269–277). The warning "had begun to lean" (46:291).
- **Dawn (46:295–339).** Seln: "Twelve. And a mule." The mule carries three boxes, the flat and a sack of meal. Brom lifts each box with both hands. "Twenty." / "Of thirty." Karis pins a clean sheet to the hide's eastern edge with two thorns, pricks the divider's point, and rules from the fourth ring over the join and a hand's breadth past the prick, with the bound left hand: "I am not going to draw a stop that the paper does not give me."
- **Roles (all unnamed):** the woman of the half-door; the woman with the tarred hands; the rope-walk's carter; the keeper with the bead-frame; the factor of the shoulder hold (no pronoun); the horseman; the landing's broker and his clerk; the ferryman; the salt carter; the miller and the miller's wife; the woman with the net; Teague's tall man, bow, boy (the young one) and old woman.

### State at movement end (author; governed by the rulings above and by the surviving prose)
**Calendar.** First light on the **two hundred and ninety-ninth day past the Line**, at the head of the notch above Millrace. Days on the page: 294 (the Deepstacks; the hatch; the yard), 295 (the rope-walk; the outer gate; the bridge; the posts; the rise; the fork-stone; the spring), 296 (the cut's head; the cut; the cairn; the halting-ground), 297 (the western landing; the ferry; Lowmarch at dusk; Teague's window; the long room), 298 (the meadow; the back room; the south road; the fork above the mill; Millrace; the readings; the tellings; the deep reading; the cart-shed), 299 (the ford). No season, month or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no date printed for Pike's season line; no count of years since the Kindling.

**Where everyone is.** Twelve and a mule, in file, leaving the notch's head by the ruts that do not go back to Lowmarch: Seln ahead; the tall man; Teague, with Cael at his left shoulder a stride back; Lira; the young one; Karis, the two healers and the brown mule in the middle; Brom; Vastin a stride behind him; the bow last. Cleon is under Treswick with the leaf, the chronicle and the board. Pike is at Lowmarch.

**Cael.** Nineteen. **Twelve fragments. Deployment thirty-two.** He was a lamp for ten days and has entered it. He did not look back at the crown from the bridge; the promise is kept and done, and Oryn has not been told in so many words that it was kept (he told her the two things he had written that he owed). He has told Oryn: the two self-readings of the 281st and why; the eleventh; the twelfth. He has not told her, or anybody: **the two words (*still*; *set*)**; the warning; the date. He holds Oryn's finding word for word and has not written it. He has written that one thing came under her hands, unasked, and four reasons for not writing what (the fourth "not mine to write"). He has told Lira that something came and that he has no name for it. He walks at Teague's left by the field's ruling and has said aloud that the field is Teague's. He lent the dividers twice and did not open them (the ruling of the 168th stands, a hundred and thirty days old on the 298th). He has told Vastin that what is kept from him is "six people's". The eleventh's debt stands. Body: sound; the eyes paid on the night of the 298th.

**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **The arm is "an arm"** (day thirty-three out of the wool on the 298th; marker 13 holds the exchange). She taught the four hands to Teague's four and corrects the young one. She offered the leaf to Oryn (marker 16) and was declined. She saw Cael go stiff on the plank and has agreed to wait. She has read the 298th's noon log. Her registry paper is not mentioned.

**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No grey. **Left shoulder mended by Oryn** on the 298th; **right re-tied by the older healer** in two yards; both arms come up to his shoulders on the 299th. He paid the rope debt and gave Pike the thumbed slip. **The purse: a hundred and twenty-seven.** Oryn's bowl is back with her.

**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck. She has copied the leaf's back (her notebook) and holds Cleon's drawn copy (a roll of card in brown tape). She has the fourth line: the mark, over those who are kindled and what is set; three. She has the grade as three marks in three ruled boxes, the first empty; the four running numbers; a fifth place of the two-bar mark, older than two hundred and six, with no figure; the office's run from the fourth place to it. She has not said the office's compass point or its figure in lengths aloud. Five lines of the list are spelled and unturned; she has said none of its numbers aloud. Left palm: stitches drawn, in Oryn's linen; she writes and draws with it; she is not to lean on it. The hide has a clean sheet pinned to its eastern edge, with a prick at the divider's point and a pencil line ruled through and a hand's breadth past it.

**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. Out front. He has seen a horseman who behaves as a watcher would, and a riderless horse in the alders; he puts it no higher. He stood again at the spring's basin with his back to the slab. The case is on him, shut; not mentioned.

**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **Right knee: day twenty of thirty on the 299th**; Oryn's linen and two new laths; the slat is off; the month stands as counted. He stood on the leg once, with the stick up, at the line in the cut (the 296th). He rode the dray's tail-board for three days at a copper a day, and the salt cart on the 298th at Teague's ruling; he walks every hill. He kept the tally of the fifty-two sheets and signed it V. He sat before the six at the halting-ground and laid no copper. He took Oryn's "Stand there." without a clause and stood an hour. He has said "Noted." once (45:165; marker 15 may hold a second). He told Oryn of the wall of the eleventh day himself. He is on Teague's count of ten as one of "your six"; that is a count for a contract; he has not asked to join the six and nobody has spoken of it. He has been offered a seat at the shoulder hold's fire and has not answered. **He does not know** the mechanism, the eleventh, the twelfth, the two words, the date, or what is under the bar; he knows that he is not told, and that it is "six people's". He stopped the sentence about the mark twice more, without a word.

**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. At Millrace on the 298th, thinner; nobody has died under her hands. She has read Brom's shoulders, Lira's arm, Karis's hand and Vastin's knee, each asked first; and Cael, asked first, for forty minutes, to her own floor. **Her finding is given** (46:197). She knows of twelve, and whose the eleventh and twelfth were (the twelfth's source as "a man under the registry"); she knows what the twelfth reads and that it has not been turned inward by Cael's will; she felt "something turn over, out at the edge" and thought nothing of it. **She has not heard the leaf.** She has not been shown the grade on the page. She walks east with the mule in the middle of the file, on her own terms (marker 11), and has sat in the sixth place (marker 12).

**Teague and his crew.** The term is taken as a contract: ten on the count (Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, Vastin; Teague, the tall man, the bow, the young one); shares by the count; the field his; the reading Karis's. His wrist was used once, on a cart, clicked, and is wrapped in linen under the leather. He has not said "Noted." The tall man, the bow and the older healer have said nothing to the six. **What his four have been told:** that an office's survey was read at Treswick and gave a bearing (the long room); that the old man's wall has a month on it and is not stood before the month is out (Cael); the four numbers on the hide and "three days past the iron" (Teague, at the door; he came for the distance and did not look at the marks). **Not told:** the leaf or any word of it; the list; the mechanism; any fragment; the date; who Vastin was. Nobody in his crew is named.

**Pike's book and the cloth.** **Thirteen lines:** the twelfth is the clearance (*twenty; paid*); the thirteenth is *Treswick. Survey read. Bearing.* with its last two columns empty. **The season line is dated** (a day in its ruled space; no figure). Eleventh on the cloth.

**Cleon.** Rune, Bronze. He turned the skin, drew its back, gave Karis his copy, took his lamp back, and said his two lines at the iron. He did not look at Cael. He is not above ground.

**The faction.** One horseman, unidentified, a mile behind on the 296th; a riderless horse on the western bank on the 297th. Nothing east of the river in these chapters.

**Objects and money.** **130 → 128** (the 295th: sixty yards of four-strand, two marks) **→ 127** (the 298th: meal and a flitch at the mill, one mark). Coppers, which the closed text keeps apart from the purse: Vastin's three to the dray's carter, his own; the ferry's fare waived; the hearth at Lowmarch and the salt carter's seat not charged on the page. The reserve sealed in the third box. **The rope debt is paid.** The handcart went back swept. On the mule: three boxes (the fifty-two sheets in their order, with the tally), the flat, a sack of the mill's meal, Oryn's pannier and canvas. With Karis: the hide with its pinned sheet, the Ternhall brass, her notebook, Cleon's roll, the sheet of three boxes, Vastin's copied page. With Cael: Hesk's book, the dividers shut in it, the fair copy of the leaf. With Vastin: the tarred kit case (stake thirty-two was taken out on the 298th and laid on the table; its return to the case is not stated). The skin leaf, the chronicle and the black oak board are under Treswick.

**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before; Cleon knows how one fragment came. The twelfth: the six and Cleon, **Oryn now among them**. The eleventh: Cael, Lira (the log), **and Oryn**; not Vastin. The warning: Cael and Cleon; Cael has written that a fourth reason exists and is not his to write. **The two words: Cael alone.** That something came on the plank: Cael, Lira; Oryn only as "one of yours, in its sleep". The leaf: unchanged (not Oryn, Teague or Pike). The list's fourth line and the grade: the six and Vastin; Teague in part. The date: Cael and Seln, each alone.

**Open threads.** Sixteen markers. Two findings that do not agree, and one of them in nobody's book. A warning that has begun to lean. A box ruled for a mark that no sheet carries. A fifth place with no figure for its age and two signs unread in its corner. Five lines of a list spelled and not turned. A compass point and a measure that Karis has not said aloud. A line ruled past a point. A debt to a man who walks a stride behind Brom. A seat at a hold's fire, offered and not answered. A horse in the alders. A wrist in linen. Oryn's road, which runs with theirs for a day and a half.

### Next-movement inheritance (Sol plan audit §8; also written into the next packet)
- **Inventory and order:** twelve fragments; deployment thirty-two; Seln ahead, tall spear, Teague with Cael at his left a stride back, Lira, young one, Karis plus both healers and mule in the middle, Brom, Vastin a stride behind, bow last (`chapter-44.md:281–291`; `chapter-46.md:337–341`).
- **Contract exactly as accepted:** Cael/Lira/Brom/Karis/Seln/Vastin plus Teague/tall/bow/young = ten; neither healer; shares and costs by count; field Teague's; reading Karis's; contract only, not merger (`chapter-44.md:167–201`).
- **Teague's knowledge perimeter:** survey read and bearing; wall under a month; four ages and “three days past the iron.” His crew does not know leaf, list, mechanism, fragments, date, Vastin's former office, or the two inward words (`chapter-44.md:117–147,197–199`; `chapter-45.md:149–151`). Tall, bow and older healer still have not spoken to the six.
- **Oryn:** knows both self-readings and why, eleven and twelve and their sources/limits, and that Cael had not deliberately turned the twelfth inward; has not heard the leaf or seen the grade; was refused nothing she was owed, but Cael keeps *still/set*, Cleon's warning and the date. She sensed only “one of yours” turn and dismissed it (`chapter-46.md:9–63,171–175,221–227`). Her own finding is known to Cael and Lira because it was spoken before both (`46:193–219`).
- **Cael's two findings:** Oryn's exact finding is held but not copied; *still* then *set* is Cael's alone; the next word was suppressed; the four reasons and “hold” are in the log without the words (`chapter-46.md:197–211,267–289`).
- **Grade and sheet:** three marks; first-tier box unused; four second-tier first-sheet ages 61/94/138/206; two-bar point past the fourth on the line; compass point and length figure remain unspoken; clean sheet pinned to the hide; pencil line from fourth circle through and a hand's breadth past the divider point (`chapter-45.md:53–177`; `chapter-46.md:319–335`).
- **Dividers and stake:** Karis has opened Hesk's dividers twice on the office distance, on Cael's leave; Cael has not opened them and they end shut in his book (`chapter-45.md:129–143`; `chapter-46.md:323–335`). Stake thirty-two is back in Vastin's tarred case and has never been driven.
- **Bodies/counts at dawn 299:** Vastin day 20 of 30, Oryn's linen/two laths, stick; Brom's left shoulder mended, right re-tied, both arms to shoulder height; Lira's arm released; Karis's stitches out, linen on, scar promised, do not lean; Teague's wrist wrapped; Cael's eyes paid in the dark (`chapter-45.md:295–377`; `chapter-46.md:233–239,299–317`).
- **Money/load:** purse 127; reserve sealed in third box; mule carries three boxes, flat, meal sack, Oryn's pannier and canvas; Cael carries fair leaf copy and dividers; Karis has hide/pinned sheet, notebook, Cleon roll, three-box sheet and Vastin copy (`chapter-45.md:283`; `chapter-46.md:303–307,321–335`).
- **Watcher pressure:** an unidentified rider kept a mile back on the 296th and a riderless horse stood in the western alders on the 297th; no sighting east of the river and no identity (`chapter-43.md:117–171,245`).
- **Other live threads:** Cleon remains below with skin leaf, chronicle and board; Seln's case stays shut on his chest, contents unnamed; Vastin's debt and ignorance stand; Pike has thirteen lines and a dated season-space with no printed date; rope debt is paid; Oryn's road is her own and will diverge (`chapter-41.md:213`; `chapter-44.md:137–157,205–211`; `chapter-46.md:31–47,337–341`).

